\id ACT Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Acts
\toc1 The Acts of the Apostles.
\toc2 Acts
\toc3 ACT
\mt1 The Acts of the Apostles.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE ACTES OF THE APOSTLES.
\ip THE Goſpel hauing ſhewed, how the Jews moſt impiouſly reiected Chriſt (as alſo Moyſes and the Prophets had foretold of them:) and therefore deſerued to be reiected themſelues alſo of him: now foloweth this booke of the \it Actes of the Apoſtles\it* ( written by S. Luke in Rome the fourth yere of \f - \ft * Hier. in Catal.\f* Nero, An. Dom. 61.) and ſheweth, how notwithſtanding their deſertes, Chriſt of his mercy (as the Prophets alſo had foretold of him) offered himſelf vnto that vnworthy people, yea after that they had Crucified him, ſending vnto them his twelue Apoſtles to moue them to penance, and ſo by Baptiſme to make them of his Church: and whiles al the Twelue were ſo occupied about the Jews:
\ip how of a perſecuting Iewe he made an extraordinarie Apoſtle (who was S. Paul) and to auoide the ſcãdal of the Jews (to whom only himſelf likewiſe for the ſame cauſe had preached) ſent him, and not any of his Twelue by and by, who were his knowen Apoſtles, vnto the Gentiles, who neuer afore had heard of Chriſt, and were worſhippers of many Gods, to moue them alſo (for, that likewiſe the Prophets had foretold) to faith and penance, and ſo by Baptiſme to make them of his Church: and how the incredulous Jews euery-where reſiſted the ſame Apoſtle and his preaching to the Gentiles, perſecuting him and ſeeking his death, and neuer ceaſing vntil he fel into the handes of the Gentils: that ſo (as not onely he euery-where, but alſo the Prophets afore him, and Chriſt had foretold ) the Goſpel might be taken away from them, and giuen to the Gentiles: euen from \f - \ft \+it Act. 13, 46. 18, 6. 19, 9. 28, 28. Mat. 21, 23. Eſa. 1. Luc. 13, 33.\+it*\f* Hieruſalem (whoſe reprobation alſo by name had been often foretold) the head-citie of the Jews, where it began, tranſlated to Rome the head-citie of the Gentiles. Al this wil be euident by the partes of the booke: which may be theſe ſixe.
\ip Firſt, how Chriſt Aſcending in the ſight of his Diſciples, promiſed vnto them the Holy Ghoſt, fortelling that of him they ſhould receiue ſtrength, and ſo begin his Church in Hieruſalem: and from thence dilate it into al that Countrie, that is into al Iurie: yea and into Samaria alſo, yea into al Nations of the Gentiles, be they neuer ſo far of. \it You ſhal receiue\it* (ſaith he) \it the vertue of the Holy Ghoſt cõming vpon you: and you ſhal be witneſſes vnto me in Hieruſalem, & in al Iurie, and Samaria, and euen to the vtmoſt of the earth\it*. Chap. 1.
\ip Secondly, the beginning of the Church in Hieruſalem, accordingly. Chap. 2.
\ip Thirdly, the propagation of it conſequently into al Iurie, and alſo to Samaria. Chap. 8.
\ip Fourthly, the propagation of it to the Gentiles alſo. Chap. 10.
\ip Fifthly, the taking of it away from the obſtinate Jews, and giving of it to the Gentiles, by the miniſterie of S. Paul and S. Barnabee. Chap. 13.
\ip Sixthly, of taking it away from Hieruſalem itſelf, the head-citie of the Jews, and ſending it (as it were) to Rome the head-citie of the Gentiles, and that, in their perſecuting of Paul ſo far, that \f - \ft \+it Act. 25, 11.\+it*\f* he appealed to Cæſar, and ſo deliuering him after a ſort vnto the Romanes: as they had before \f - \ft \+it Luc. 23, 1.\+it*\f* deliuered to them alſo Chriſt himſelf. Whereas S. Peters firſt cõming thither, was vpon another occaſion, as ſhal be ſaid anone. Of which Romanes and Gentiles therfore, the ſame S. Paul being now come to Rome (the laſt Chap. of the Actes ) foretelleth the obſtinat Jews there, ſaying: \it Et\it* \f - \ft \+it Act. 28, 28.\+it*\f* \it ipſi audient\it*: You wil not heare, but, \it they wil heare\it*: That ſo the prediction of Chriſt aboue rehearſed might be fulfilled: \it And euen to the vtmoſt of the earth\it*. And there doth S. Luke end the booke, not caring to tel ſo much as the fulfilling of that which our Lord had foretold (Act. 27. 24.) to S. Paul:
\ip \it Thou muſt appeare before Cæſar\it*. Becauſe his purpoſe was no more but to ſhew the new Hieruſalem of the Chriſtians, where Chriſt would place the cheefe ſeate of his Church: as alſo indeed the Fathers and al other Catholikes haue in al Ages looked thither, when they were in any great doubt: no leſſe then the Jews to Hieruſalem, as they were appointed in the old Teſtament. Deut. 17, 8.
\ip And ſo this Booke doth ſhew the true Church, as plainely, as the Goſpel doth ſhew the true Chriſt, vnto al that do not wilfully ſhut their owne eies. To wit, this to be the true Church, which beginning viſibly at Hieruſalem, was taken from the Jews, and tranſlated to the Gentils (and namely to Rome) continuing viſibly, and viſibly to continue hereafter alſo, \it Vntil the fulnes of\it* \f - \ft \+it Rom. 11, 25.\+it*\f* \it the Gentiles ſhall be come in\it*: that then alſo \it Al Iſraël may be ſaued.\it* And then is come the end of the world. For ſo did Chriſt moſt plainely foretel vs: \it This Goſpel of the Kingdom ſhall be preached\it* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 24, 14.\+it*\f* \it in the whole world, for a teſtimonie to al Nations: and then ſhal come the conſummation.\it* For the conuerſion of which Nations and accompliſhing the fulnes of al Gentiles, the foreſaid Church Catholike, being mindful of her office, \it to be Chriſtes witnes euen to the vtmoſt of the earth\it*, doth at this preſent (as alwaies) ſend preachers to conuert and make them alſo Chriſtians: whereas the Proteſtants and al other Heretikes do nothing els but ſubuert ſuch as before were Chriſtians. \f - \ft Tertul. de praes.\f*
\ip And this being the Summe and ſcope of this Booke, thus to giue vs hiſtorically a iuſt ſight of the fulfilling of the Prophets & Chriſtes prediction about the Church: it is not to be marueiled at, why it telleth not of S. Peters cõming to Rome: conſidering that his firſt cõming thither was not, as S. Paules was, by the Jews' deliuerie of him, working ſo to their owne reprobatiõ, but vpon another occaſion, to wit, to confound Simon Magus. \it Eus. Hiſt. li. 2. c. 12. 13.\it* For who alſo ſeeth not, that it maketh no mention of his preaching to any Gentiles at al, thoſe few onely Act. 10. excepted, who were the firſt, and therefore (leſt the Gentiles ſhould ſeeme leſſe cared for of God, then the Jews) Peter being the Head of al, was elected of God, to incorporate them into the Church, as before he had done the Jews. \it God\it* (ſaith he) \it among vs choſe, that by my mouth the Gentiles ſhould heare\it* \f - \ft \+it Act. 15, 7.\+it*\f* \it the word of the Goſpel, and beleeue.\it* And S. Iames therevpon: \it Simon hath told how God firſt\it* \f - \ft \+it Act. 15, 14.\+it*\f* \it viſited to take of the Gentiles a people to his name.\it* But otherwiſe (I ſay) here is no mention of Peters preaching to any Gentiles: no nor of the other eleuen Apoſtles. Wil any man therfore inferre, that neither Peter, nor the other Eleuen preached to any Nation or citie of the Gentiles? No, the meaning of the Holy Ghoſt was not to write al the Actes of al the Apoſtles, no nor the preaching of Peter and his, to the Gentiles, but onely to the Jews: thereby to ſet out vnto the world, the great mercy of Chriſt toward thoſe vnworthy Jews, and conſequently their moſt worthy reprobation for cõtemning ſuch grace and mercy. As alſo on the other ſide to ſhew, how readily the Gentiles in ſo many Nations, were conuerted by one Apoſtle onely, who \it From Hieruſalem\it* \f - \ft \+it Rom. 15, 19.\+it*\f* \it euen to Illyricum repleniſhed the Goſpel of Chriſt.\it* And this parting of the worke ſo made by S. Peter with the reſt doth S. Paul himſelf touche: \it That we vnto the Gentiles, and they vnto the\it* \f - \ft \+it Gal. 2, 9.\+it*\f* \it Circunciſion.\it* Neuertheleſſe before his cõming to Rome, not onely was the Church come to Rome (as it is euident Act. the laſt chap.) there plãted by Saint Peter and others (as likewiſe by Saint Peter it was planted in the firſt Gentils, before that S. Paul began the taking of it away from the multitude of the Jews, and the tranſlating of it to the multitude of the Gentils) but alſo ſo notable was the ſame Church of Rome, that S. Paul writing his Epiſtle to the Romanes, before he came thither, ſaith: \it Your faith is renowmed in the whole world.\it* And therefore they with the reſt of the Gentiles, be \f - \ft \+it Rom. 1, 8.\+it*\f* that Nation whereof Chriſt told the Jews, ſaying: \it The Kingdom of God ſhal be taken away from\it* \f - \ft \+it Mat. 21, 43.\+it*\f* \it you, and ſhal be given to a Nation yelding the fruites thereof.\it*
\ip As before we noted the Goſpels, as they are read both at Mattins and Maſſe, throughout the yere, in their conuenient time and place: ſo the bookes folowing (as alſo the bookes of the old Teſtament) are read in the ſaid Seruice of the Church, for Epiſtles and Leſſons, in their time & place, as hereafter ſhal be noted in euery of them. \it See the very ſame order and cuſtome of the primitiue Church, in S. Ambroſe ep. 33. S. Auguſtine Serm. de Tempore 139, 140, 141, 144. S. Leo Ser. 2. & 4. de Quadrag. & Ser. 13. & 19. de Paſs. Domini. S. Gregorie in his 40 homilies vpon the Goſpels.\it*
\c 1
\cd Chriſt now ready to aſcend, biddeth the Apoſtles to expect the Holy Ghoſt which he had promiſed, foretelling where (being ſtrengthened by him) they ſhould begin his Church, and hovv far they ſhould cary it. 9 After his Aſcenſion they are warned by two Angels to ſet their mindes vpon his ſecond comming. 14 In the daies of their expectation, 15 Peter beginneth to execute his vicarſhip, giuing inſtruction and order, by vvhich Mathias is elected Apoſtle in the place of Iudas. \f - \ft The CHURCH readeth this book at Matins from Low Sunday unto the 3. Sunday after Eaſter: even as in S. Auguſtine's time. See ſerm. & de Diverſis. to. 10.\f* \f - \ft The 1. part. The expectation of the Holy Ghoſt between the Aſcenſion of Chriſt, and the beginning of the Church.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc THE\sc* \x - \xt Lu. 1, 3.\x* \f 2 \fr 1.1 \ft The Epiſtle at Maſſe vpon Aſcenſion day.\f* firſt treatiſe I made of \f 3 \fr 1.1 \ft Not al particularly, (for the other Euangeliſts write diuers thinges not touched by him) but al the principal and moſt neceſſarie thinges.\f* al things, O Theophilus, which \sc IESVS\sc* began to doe and to teache,
\v 2 vntil the day vvherein giuing commaundement by the holy Ghoſt to the Apoſtles vvhom he choſe, he vvas aſſumpted:
\v 3 to whom he ſhewed alſo him ſelf aliue after his paſſion in many arguments, for fourtie daies appearing to them, & ſpeaking of the kingdom of God.
\v 4 And eating vvith them, \x - \xt Lu. 24, 49.\x* he commaunded them, that they ſhould not depart from Hieruſalem, but ſhould expect the promiſſe of the Father, vvhich you \x - \xt Io. 14, 26.\x* haue heard (ſaith he) by my mouth:
\v 5 for Iohn in deede baptized vvith \x - \xt Lu. 3, 16.\x* vvater, but \f 2 \fr 1.5 \ft Iohns Baptiſme gaue not the Holy Ghoſt.\f* you ſhal be \f 3 \fr 1.5 \ft The aboundant powring of the Holy Ghoſt vpon them on Whitſunday, he calleth baptiſme.\f* baptized vvith the holy Ghoſt after theſe fevv daies.
\v 6 They therfore that vvere aſſembled, aſked him, ſaying, Lord, whether at this time vvilt thou reſtore the kingdom to Iſrael?
\v 7 but he ſaid to them, It is not for you to know times or moments, vvhich the Father hath put in his owne power:
\v 8 but you ſhal receiue the \x - \xt Lu. 24, 49. Act. 2, 1.\x* vertue of the holy Ghoſt comming vpon you, and you ſhal be witneſſes vnto me in Hieruſalem, and in al Iewrie, and Samaria, and euen to the vtmoſt of the earth.
\v 9 And \x - \xt Mar. 16, 19. Lu. 24, 51.\x* vvhen he had ſaid theſe things, in their ſight he vvas eleuated: and a cloud receiued him out of their ſight.
\v 10 And vvhen they beheld him going into heauen, behold two men ſtoode beſide them in white garments,
\v 11 vvho alſo ſaid, Ye men of Galilee, vvhy ſtand you looking into heauen? This \sc IESVS\sc* vvhich is aſſumpted from you into heauen, ſhal ſo come as you haue ſeen him going into heauen. ⊢
\v 12 Then they returned to Hieruſalem from the mount that is called Oliuet, which is by Hieruſalem, diſtant a Sabboths iourney.
\v 13 And vvhen they vvere entred in, they vvent vp into an vpper chamber, vvhere abode \f 1 \fr 1.13 \ft This viſible companie was the true Churche of Chriſt, which he left & commaunded to keepe together til the comming of the Holy Ghoſt, by him to be further informed & furniſhed to gaine al natiōs to the ſame Societie.\f* Peter & Iohn, Iames and Andrevv, Philippe and Thomas, Bartholomevv and Mathevv, Iames of Alphæus and Simon Zelótes, and Iude of Iames.
\v 14 Al theſe vvere perſeuering vvith one minde in praier vvith the \f 1 \fr 1.14 \ft The Heretikes, ſome in the text, other in the margent, tranſlate, \+it Wiues\+it*, to wit, of the Apoſtles, moſt impudently, knowing in their conſciences that he meaneth the Maries & other holy women that folowed Chriſt. as \+it Lu. 8, 2. 24, 10\+it*. See Beza & the Engl. Bible 1579.\f* vvomen and \sc MARIE\sc* the mother of \sc IESVS\sc*, and his brethren.
\v 15 \f 1 \fr 1.15 \ft a The Epiſtle vpon S. Matthias day \+it Febr. 24.\+it*\f* In thoſe daies Peter riſing vp in the middes of the brethren, ſaid: (and the multitude of perſons together, vvas almoſt an hundred and tvventie)
\v 16 You men, brethren, the \x - \xt Ps. 40, 9. Io. 13, 18.\x* ſcripture muſt be fulfilled vvhich the holy Ghoſt ſpake before by the mouth of Dauid concerning Iudas, vvho vvas the \x - \xt Luc. 22, 47. Io. 18, 3.\x* captaine of them that apprehended \sc IESVS\sc*:
\v 17 vvho vvas numbred among vs and obteined the lot of this miniſterie.
\v 18 And he in deede hath poſſeſſed a \x - \xt Mt. 27, 5. 7.\x* field of the revvard of iniquitie, and being hanged he burſt in the middes, and al his bovvels guſhed out.
\v 19 And it vvas made notorious to al the inhabitants of Hieruſalem: ſo that the ſame field vvas called in their tonge, \it Hacel-dema\it*, that is to ſay, the field of bloud.
\v 20 For it is vvritten in the booke of Pſalmes. \x - \xt Pſa. 68, 26.\x* \it Be their habitation made deſert, and be there none to dvvel in it. \it* \x - \xt Ps. 108, 8.\x* \it And his Biſhoprike let an other take\it*.
\v 21 Therfore, of theſe men that haue aſſembled vvith vs, al the time that our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* vvent in and vvent out among vs,
\v 22 beginning from the baptiſme of Iohn vntil the day vvherein he vvas aſſumpted from vs, \f 1 \fr 1.22 \ft No ſmal myſterie, that the number of the twelue Apoſtles muſt needes be made vp againe.\f* there muſt one of theſe be made a vvitnes vvith vs of his reſurrection.
\v 23 And they appointed tvvo, Ioſeph, vvho was called Bárſabas, vvho vvas ſurnamed Iuſtus: and Mathias.
\v 24 And praying they ſaid, Thou Lord that knovveſt the harts of al men, ſhevv of theſe two, one, vvhom thou haſt choſen,
\v 25 to take the place of this miniſterie and Apoſtleſhip, from the vvhich Iudas hath preuaricated that he might goe to his ovvne place.
\v 26 And they gaue them lottes, and the lot fel vpon Mathias, and he vvas numbered vvith the eleuen Apoſtles. ⊢
\c 2
\cd The Holy Ghoſt comming to the Faithful vpon whitſunday. 5 Iewes in Hieruſalem of al Nations do wonder to heare them ſpeake al tongues. 14 And Peter to the deriders declareth, that it is not drunkennes, but the Holy Ghoſt which Ioel did prophecie of, vvhich IESVS (whom they crucified) being now riſen againe and aſcended (as he ſheweth alſo out of the Scriptures) hath powred out from heauen, concluding therfore that he is CHRIST, and they moſt horrible murderers. 37 Whereat they being compuncte, and ſubmitting them ſelues, he telleth them that they muſt be baptized, and then they alſo ſhal receiue the ſame Holy Ghoſt, as being promiſed to al the baptized. 41 And ſo 3000 are baptized that very day. 42 Whoſe godly exerciſes are here reported, and alſo their liuing in ſtate of perfection. The Apoſtles worke many miracles, and God daily increaſeth the number of the Church. \f - \ft The 2. part. The coming of the Holy Ghoſt and beginning of the Church in Hieruſalem.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* \x - \xt Act. 1, 4.\x*\f 2 \fr 2.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Whitſunday.\f* when the daies of Pentecoſt vvere accompliſhed, they vvere al together in one place:
\v 2 and ſodenly there vvas made a ſound from heauen, as of a vehement vvinde comming, & it filled the whole houſe vvhere they vvere ſitting.
\v 3 And there appeared to them parted tonges as it were \x - \xt Mt. 3, 11.\x* of fire, and it ſate vpon euery one of them:
\v 4 and they vvere al repleniſhed vvith the \x - \xt Io. 7, 39.\x* \sc HOLY GHOST\sc*, and they began to ſpeake vvith diuerſe tonges, according as the \sc HOLY GHOST\sc* gaue them to ſpeake.
\v 5 And there vvere dvvelling at Hieruſalem Iewes, deuout men of euery nation that is vnder heauen.
\v 6 And vvhen this voice vvas made, the multitude came together, and vvas aſtonied in minde, becauſe euery man heard them ſpeake in his ovvne tongue.
\v 7 And they vvere al amaſed, and marueled ſaying, Are not, lo, al theſe that ſpeake, Galilæans,
\v 8 and hovv \f 1 \fr 2.8 \fq `haue vve heard \ft \+it `do vve heare\+it*.\f* `haue vve heard' eche man our ovvne tongue vvherein vve vvere borne?
\v 9 Parthians, and Medians, and Elamites, and that inhabite Meſopotámia, Iewrie, and Cappadocia, Pontus, and Aſia,
\v 10 Phrygia, and Pamphilia, Ægypt and the partes of Lybia that is about Cyrénee, and ſtrangers of Rome,
\v 11 Iewes alſo, and Proſelytes, Cretenſians, and Arabians: Vve \f 1 \fr 2.11 \fq `haue heard \ft \+it do heare\+it*\f* `haue heard' them ſpeake in our ovvne tonges the great workes of God. ⊢
\v 12 And they vvere al aſtonied, and marueled, ſaying one to an other, Vvhat meaneth this?
\v 13 But others deriding ſaid, That theſe are ful of nevv vvine.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 2.14 \ft a The leſſon before the Epiſtle on Imber Wedneſday in Whitſunweek.\f* But \f 2 \fr 2.14 \ft Peter the head of the reſt and now newly repleniſhed with al knowledge and fortitude, maketh the firſt ſermon.\f* Peter ſtanding vvith the Eleuen, lifted vp his voice, and ſpake to them, Ye men, Iewes, and al you that dwel in Hieruſalem, be this knovven to you, and vvith your eares receiue my vvordes.
\v 15 For theſe are not drunke, as you ſuppoſe, vvhereas it is the third houre of the day:
\v 16 But this is it that vvas ſaid by the Prophet Ioël,
\v 17 \x - \xt Ioel. 2, 28.\x* \it And it ſhal be, in the laſt daies (ſaith our Lord) of my Spirit I vvil powre out vpon al fleſh: and your ſonnes and your daughters ſhal prophecie, and your yong men ſhal ſee viſions, and your auncients ſhal dreame dreames\it*.
\v 18 \it And vpon my ſeruants truely, and vpon my handmaides vvil I povvre out in thoſe daies, of my Spirit, and they ſhal prophecie\it*:
\v 19 \it and I vvil giue vvonders in the heauen aboue, and ſignes in the earth beneath, bloud, and fire, and vapour of ſmoke\it*.
\v 20 \it The ſunne ſhal be turned into darkenes, and the moone into bloud, before the great and manifeſt day of our Lord doth come\it*.
\v 21 \it And it ſhal be, euery one vvhoſoeuer calleth vpon the name of our Lord, ſhal be ſaued\it*. ⊢
\v 22 Ye men of Iſraël heare theſe vvordes, \sc IESVS\sc* of Nazareth a man approued of God among you, by miracles and vvonders and ſignes vvhich God did by him in the middes of you, as you knovv:
\v 23 this ſame, by the determinate counſel and preſcience of God being deliuered, you by the handes of vvicked men haue crucified and ſlaine.
\v 24 vvhom God hath raiſed vp looſing the ſorovves of hel, according as it vvas impoſſible that he ſhould be holden of it.
\v 25 For Dauid ſaith concerning him, \x - \xt Ps. 15, 8.\x* \it I foreſavv the Lord in my ſight alvvaies: becauſe he is at my right hand that I be not moued\it*.
\v 26 \it For this, my hart hath been glad and my tongue hath reioyced: moreouer my fleſh alſo ſhal reſt in hope\it*.
\v 27 \it becauſe thou vvilt not leaue my ſoul \it* \f 1 \fr 2.27 \ft who but an infidel (ſaith S. Auguſtine) wil deny Chriſt to haue deſcended to Hel? \+it ep. 99\+it*.\f* \it in hel, nor giue thy Holy one to ſee \it* \f 2 \fr 2.27 \ft As his ſoul ſuffered no paines in Hel, ſo neither did his body take any corruption in the graue.\f* \it corruption\it*.
\v 28 \it Thou haſt made knovven to me the vvaies of life: thou ſhalt make me ful of ioyfulnes vvith thy face\it*.
\v 29 Ye men, brethren, let me boldly ſpeake to you of the Patriarch Dauid: that \x - \xt 3. Reg. 2, 10.\x* he died, and vvas buried: and his ſepulchre is vvith vs vntil this preſent day.
\v 30 VVhereas therfore he vvas a Prophet, and knevv that by an othe \x - \xt Ps. 131, 11.\x* \it God had ſvvorne to him that of the fruite of his loynes there ſhould ſit vpon his ſeate\it*:
\v 31 forſeeing he ſpake of the reſurrection of Chriſt, for neither vvas he left in hel, neither did his fleſh ſee corruption.
\v 32 This \sc IESVS\sc* hath God raiſed againe, vvhereof al vve are vvitneſſes.
\v 33 Being exalted therfore by the right hand of God, and hauing receiued of his father the promiſſe of the Holy Ghoſt, he hath povvred out \f 1 \fr 2.33 \fq `this vvhom \ft \+it `this gift vvhich\+it*\f* `this vvhom' you ſee and heare.
\v 34 For Dauid aſcended not into heauen: but he ſaith, \x - \xt Ps. 109, 1.\x* \it Our Lord hath ſaid to my Lord, ſit on my right hand\it*,
\v 35 \it vntil I make thine enemies the footeſtoole of thy feete\it*.
\v 36 Therfore let al the houſe of Iſraël know moſt certainly that God hath made him both Lord, and \sc CHRIST\sc*, this \sc IESVS\sc*, vvhom you haue crucified.
\v 37 \f 1 \fr 2.37 \ft Contrition.\f* And hearing theſe things, they were compuncte in hart, and ſaid to Peter and to the reſt of the Apoſtles, Vvhat ſhal vve doe men, brethren?
\v 38 But Peter ſaid to them, \f 1 \fr 2.38 \ft Not onely amendment of life, but penance alſo required before Baptiſme, in ſuch as be of age, though not in that ſort as aftervvard in the Sacrament of penance. \+it Aug. de fid. et oper. c. 11. et ep. 108\+it*.\f* Doe penance, and be euery one of you baptized in the name of \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* for remiſſion of your ſinnes: and you ſhal receiue the gift of the holy Ghoſt.
\v 39 For to you is the promiſſe, and to your children, and to al that are farre of, vvhomſoeuer the Lord our God ſhal call.
\v 40 Vvith very many other vvordes alſo did he teſtifie, and exhorted them, ſaying: Saue your ſelues from this peruerſe generation.
\v 41 They therfore that receiued his vvord, vvere baptized: and there vvere added in that day about \f 1 \fr 2.41 \ft Three thouſand vvere conuerted at this firſt ſermon, & they vvere put to the other viſible companie and Church.\f* three thouſand ſoules.
\v 42 And they vvere perſeuering in the doctrine of the Apoſtles, and in the communication of \f 1 \fr 2.42 \ft This vvas the B. Sacrament, vvhich the Apoſtles daily miniſtred to the Chriſtians at leaſt in one kinde. See chap. 20, 7.\f* the breaking of bread, and praiers.
\v 43 And feare came vpon euery ſoul: many vvonders alſo and ſignes vvere done by the Apoſtles in Hieruſalem, and there vvas great feare in al.
\v 44 Al they alſo that beleeued, vvere together, \x - \xt Act. 4, 32.\x* and had al things common.
\v 45 Their poſſeſſions and ſubſtance they ſold, and deuided them to al, according as euery one had neede.
\v 46 Daily alſo continuing vvith one accord in the temple, and breaking bread from houſe to houſe, they tooke their meate vvith ioy and ſimplicitie of hart:
\v 47 praiſing God, and hauing grace vvith al the people. And our Lord increaſed them that ſhould be ſaued, daily together.
\c 3
\cd A miracle, and a Sermon of Peters to the people, ſhewing that IESVS is Chriſt, and exhorting them to faith in him and penance for their ſinnes, and ſo they ſhal haue by him (in Baptiſme) the Benediction which was promiſed to Abraham.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* \f 1 \fr 3.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpõ SS. Peter and Paules eue. Iun 28.\f* Peter and Iohn vvent vp into the temple, at the \f 2 \fr 3.1 \ft This maketh for diſtinction of Canonical houres and diuerſitie of appointed times to pray in. \+it See Annot. c. 10, 9\+it*.\f* ninthe houre of praier.
\v 2 And a certaine man that vvas lame from his mothers vvombe, vvas caried: vvhom they laid euery day at the gate of the temple, that is called Specious, that he might aſke almes of them that vvent into the temple.
\v 3 He, vvhen he had ſeen Peter and Iohn about to enter into the temple, aſked to receiue an almes.
\v 4 But Peter vvith Iohn looking vpon him, ſaid, Looke vpon vs.
\v 5 But he looked earneſtly vpon them, hoping that he ſhould receiue ſome thing of them.
\v 6 But Peter ſaid, Siluer and gold I haue not, but that vvhich I haue, the ſame I giue to thee: In the name of \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* of Nazareth ariſe, and vvalke.
\v 7 And taking his right hand, he lifted him vp, and forthvvith his feete and ſoles vvere made ſtrong.
\v 8 And ſpringing he ſtoode, and vvalked: and vvent in vvith them into the temple vvalking, and leaping, and praiſing God.
\v 9 And al the people ſavv him vvalking and praiſing God.
\v 10 And they knevv him, that, it vvas he vvhich ſate for almes at the Specious gate of the temple: and they vvere excedingly aſtonied and agaſt at that that had chaunced to him.
\v 11 And as he held Peter and Iohn, al the people ranne to them vnto the porche vvhich is called Salomons, vvondering.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 3.12 \ft The Epiſtle vpõ weneſday in Eaſter weeke.\f* But Peter ſeing them, made anſvver to the people, Ye men of Iſraël, vvhy maruel you at this, or vvhy looke you vpon vs, as though by our povver or holines vve haue made this man to vvalke?
\v 13 The God of Abraham, and the God of Iſaac, and the God of Iacob, the God of our fathers hath glorified his ſonne \sc IESVS\sc*, vvhom you in deede deliuered and denied before the face of Pilate, he iudging him to be releaſed.
\v 14 But you denied the holy and the iuſt one, \x - \xt Luc. 23, 18.\x* and aſked a man killer to be giuen vnto you.
\v 15 but the authour of life you killed, vvhom God hath raiſed from the dead, of vvhich vve are vvitneſſes.
\v 16 And in the \f 1 \fr 3.16 \ft This faith was not the faith of the lame man (for he looked onely for almes) nor a ſpecial faith of the Apoſtles owne ſaluatiõ: but the whole beleefe of Chriſtian Religion.\f* faith of his name, this man vvhom you ſee and knovv, his name hath ſtrengthened: & the faith vvhich is by him, hath giuen this perfect health in the ſight of al you.
\v 17 And novv (brethren) I knovv that you did it through ignorance, as alſo your princes.
\v 18 But God vvho foreſhevved by the mouth of al the prophets, that his \sc CHRIST\sc* ſhould ſuffer, hath ſo fulfilled it.
\v 19 Be Penitent therfore & conuert, that your ſinnes may be put out. ⊢
\v 20 that, vvhen the times ſhal come of refreſhing by the ſight of our Lord, and he ſhal ſend him that hath been preached vnto you \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc*,
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 3.21 \ft \+it c ὃν δεῖ οὐρανὸν μὲν δέξασθαι\+it*\f* vvhom \f 2 \fr 3.21 \ft Some Heretikes fouly corrupt this place, thus, \+it Who muſt be conteined in heauen\+it*, of purpoſe (as they proteſt) to hold Chriſt in heauẽ, from the B. Sacramẽt. \+it Beza\+it*. As though his presẽce there, drew him out of heauen. Neither cã they pretend the Greeke, vvhich is vvord for word as in the vulgar latin, and as we tranſlate.\f* heauen truely muſt receiue vntil the times of the reſtitution of al things, vvhich God ſpake by the mouth of his holy prophets from the beginning of the world.
\v 22 Moyſes in deede ſaid, \x - \xt Deu. 18, 15.\x* \it That a prophet ſhal the\it* Lord your God raiſe vp to you of your brethren, as \it my ſelf: him you ſhal heare according to al things vvhatſoeuer he ſhal ſpeake to you\it*.
\v 23 \it And it ſhal be, euery ſoule that ſhal not heare that prophet, ſhal be deſtroied out of the people\it*.
\v 24 And al the Prophets from Samuël and aftervvard that haue ſpoken, told of theſe daies.
\v 25 You are the children of the Prophets and of the teſtament vvhich God made to our fathers, ſaying to Abraham, \x - \xt Gen. 22, 18. 26, 4.\x* \it And in thy ſeede ſhal al the families of the earth be bleſſed\it*.
\v 26 To you firſt God raiſing vp his ſonne, hath ſent him bleſſing you: that euery one ſhould conuert him ſelf from his naughtines.
\c 4
\cd The Rulers of the Iewes oppoſe them ſelues and impriſon Peter and Iohn. 4 But yet thouſands of the people are conuerted: 5 and to the Rulers alſo, Peter boldly auoucheth by the forſaid miracle, that IESVS is Chriſt, telling them of their heinous fault out of the Pſalmes, and that vvithout him they can not be ſaued. 13 They though confounded vvith the miracle, yet procede in their obſtinacie, forbidding them to ſpeake any more of IESVS, adding alſo threates. 23 Wherevpon the Church fleeth to praier, vvherein they comfort them ſelues vvith the omnipotencie of God, and prediction of Dauid, and aſke for the gift of boldnes and miracles againſt thoſe threates. 31 And God ſhevveth miraculouſly that he hath heard their praier. 32 The vvhole Churches vnitie and communitie of life. 36 Of Barnabas by name.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* vvhen they vvere ſpeaking to the people, the Prieſts and magiſtrates of the temple and the Sadducees came vpon them,
\v 2 being greeued that they taught the people, and ſhevved in \sc IESVS\sc* the reſurrection from the dead:
\v 3 and they laid handes vpon them, and put them into vvard, vntil the morovv, for it vvas novv euening.
\v 4 And \f 1 \fr 4.4 \ft Here againe vve ſee the proceeding and increaſe of the Church viſibly.\f* many of them that had heard the vvord, beleeued: and the number of the men vvas made fiue thouſand.
\v 5 And it came to paſſe on the morovv, that their princes, and Auncients, and Scribes vvere gathered into Hieruſalem.
\v 6 and Annas the high prieſt, and Caiphas and Iohn, and Alexander, and as many as vvere of the prieſts ſtocke.
\v 7 And ſetting them in the middes, they aſked: In vvhat povver or in vvhat name haue you done this?
\v 8 Then Peter repleniſhed vvith the holy Ghoſt, ſaid to them, Ye princes of the people & Auncients:
\v 9 If vve this day be examined for a good deede vpon an impotent man, in vvhat he hath been made whole,
\v 10 be it knovven to al you and to al the people of Iſraël, that in \f 1 \fr 4.10 \ft The name of IESVS. \+it See Annot. Philip. 2,10\+it*.\f* the name of \sc IESVS Chriſt\sc* of Nazareth, vvhom you did crucifie, vvhom God hath raiſed from the dead, in this ſame this man ſtandeth before you vvhole.
\v 11 This is \x - \xt Pſa. 117, 22.\x* \it the ſtone that vvas reiected of you the builders: vvhich is made into the head of the corner\it*.
\v 12 and there is not ſaluation in any other. for neither is there any other name vnder heauen giuen to men, vvherein vve muſt be ſaued.
\v 13 \f 1 \fr 4.13 \ft The Apoſtles conſtancie, learning and vviſdom after the comming of the Holy Ghoſt, being but idiotes, that is, ſimple vnlettered men and timorous before.\f* And ſeeing Peters conſtancie and Iohns, vnderſtanding that they vvere men vnlettered, and of the vulgar ſort, they marueled, and they knew them that they had been vvith \sc IESVS\sc*:
\v 14 ſeeing the man alſo that had been cured, ſtanding vvith them, they could ſay nothing to the contrarie.
\v 15 But they commaunded them to goe aſide forth out of the councel: and they conferred together,
\v 16 ſaying, VVhat ſhal vve doe to theſe men? for a notorious ſigne in deede hath been done by them, to al the inhabitants of Hieruſalem: it is manifeſt, and vve can not denie it.
\v 17 But that it be no further ſpred abrode among the people, let vs threaten them, that they ſpeake no more in this name to any man.
\v 18 And calling them, they charged them that they ſhould not ſpeake at al, nor teache in the name of \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 19 But Peter and Iohn anſvvering, ſaid to them, \f 1 \fr 4.19 \ft Their conſtancie and courage after their confirmation, being ſo weake before. And if any Magiſtrate commaund againſt God, that is to ſay, forbid Catholike Chriſtian men to preach or ſerue God, this ſame muſt be their anſwer, though they be vvhipped and killed for their labour. \+it See c. 5. v. 29\+it*.\f* If it be iuſt in the ſight of God, to heare you rather then God, iudge ye.
\v 20 for vve can not but ſpeake the things vvhich vve haue ſeen and heard.
\v 21 But they threatening, dimiſſed them: not finding hovv they might puniſh them, for the people, becauſe all glorified that vvhich had been done, in that vvhich vvas chaunced.
\v 22 For the man vvas more then fourtie yeres old, in vvhom that ſigne of health had been vvrought.
\v 23 And being dimiſſed they came to theirs, and ſhevved al that the cheefe prieſts and Auncients had ſaid to them.
\v 24 VVho hauing heard it, with one accord lifted vp their voice to God, and ſaid, Lord, thou that didſt make heauen & earth, the ſea, and al things that are in them,
\v 25 vvho in the holy Ghoſt by the mouth of our father Dauid thy ſeruant haſt ſaid, \x - \xt Ps. 2, 1.\x* \it VVhy did the Gentiles rage, and the people meditate vaine thinges\it*:
\v 26 \it the kings of the earth ſtand vp, and the Princes aſſemble together againſt our Lord, and againſt his \+sc Chriſt\+sc*?\it*
\v 27 For there aſſembled in deede in this citie againſt thy holy childe \sc IESVS\sc* vvhom thou haſt anointed, Herod, and Pontius Pilate, vvith the Gentiles and the people of Iſraël,
\v 28 to doe vvhat \f 1 \fr 4.28 \ft Chriſtes death, as needeful for mans redemption, was of Gods determination: but as of the malice of the Ievves, it vvas not his act othervviſe then by permiſſion.\f* thy hand & thy counſel decreed to be done.
\v 29 And novv Lord looke vpon their threatenings, and giue vnto thy ſeruants vvith al confidence to ſpeake thy vvorde,
\v 30 in that, that thou extend thy hand to cures and ſignes and vvonders to be done by the name of thy holy ſonne \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 31 And vvhen they had praied, the place vvas moued vvherein they vvere gathered: and they vvere al repleniſhed vvith the holy Ghoſt, and they ſpake the vvord of God vvith confidence.
\v 32 And the multitude of beleeuers had one hart and one ſoule: neither did any one ſay that ought vvas his ovvne of thoſe things vvhich he poſſeſſed, \x - \xt Act. 2, 44.\x* but al things vvere common vnto them.
\v 33 And vvith great povver did the Apoſtles giue teſtimonie of the reſurrection of \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* our Lord: and great grace vvas in al them.
\v 34 For neither vvas there any one needie among them. For as many as vvere ovvners of landes or houſes, \f 1 \fr 4.34 \ft Note the ardent charitie and contempt of worldly things in the firſt Chriſtians: who did not only giue great alms, but\f* ſold and brought the prices of thoſe things vvhich they ſold,
\v 35 and laid it before the feete of the Apoſtles. And to euery one vvas deuided according as euery one hade neede.
\v 36 And Ioſeph vvho vvas ſurnamed of the Apoſtles Barnabas (vvhich is by interpretation, the ſonne of conſolation) a Leuite, a Cyprian borne,
\v 37 vvhereas he had a peece of land, ſold it, and brought the price, and laid it before the feete of the Apoſtles.
\c 5
\cd Ananias and his vvife Saphira, for their ſacrilege, at Peters word fall downe dead, to the great terrour of the reſt. 12 By the Apoſtles miracles, not onely the number, but alſo their faith ſo increaſeth, that they ſeeke in the ſtreates to the very ſhadow of Peter, the townes alſo about bringing their diſeaſed to Hieruſalem. 17 The Rulers againe oppoſe them ſelues, but in vaine. 19 For out of priſon an Angel deliuereth them, bidding them preach openly to al: 27 and in their Councel Peter is nothing afraid of their bigge wordes: 34 Yea Gamaliel being one of them ſelues caſteth a doubt among them, leſt the matter be of God, and therfore impoſſible to be diſſolued. 40 Finally, the Apoſtles being ſcourged by them, count it an honour, and ceaſe no day from preaching.
\p
\v 1 \sc BVT\sc* a certaine man named Ananias, vvith Saphîra his vvife ſold a peece of land,
\v 2 and defrauded of the price of the land, his vvife being priuie thereto: and bringing a certaine portion, laide it at the feete of the Apoſtles.
\v 3 And Peter ſaid, Ananias, vvhy hath Satan tempted thy hart, that thou ſhouldeſt lie to the holy Ghoſt, and defraude of the price of the land?
\v 4 Remaining, did it not remaine to thee: and being ſold, vvas it not in thy povver? Vvhy haſt thou put this thing in thy hart? Thou haſt not lied to men, but to God.
\v 5 And Ananias hearing theſe vvordes, fel dovvne, and gaue vp the ghoſt. And there came great feare vpon al that heard it.
\v 6 And yong men riſing vp, remoued him, and bearing him forth buried him.
\v 7 And it vvas the ſpace as it vvere of three houres, and his vvife, not knovving vvhat vvas chaunced, came in.
\v 8 And Peter anſvvered her, Tel me vvoman, vvhether did you ſel the land for ſo much? But ſhe ſaid, Yea, for ſo much.
\v 9 And Peter vnto her, Vvhy haue you agreed together to tempt the Spirit of our Lord? Behold, their feete that haue buried thy huſband, at the doore, and they ſhal beare thee forth.
\v 10 Forthvvith ſhe fel before his feete, and gaue vp the ghoſt. And the yong men going in, found her dead: and caried her forth, and buried her by her huſband.
\v 11 And there fel great \f 1 \fr 5.11 \ft Hereupon roſe great reuerence, awe, and feare of the vulgar Chriſtians toward the holy Apoſtles. for an example to al Chriſtian people how to behaue them ſelues toward their Biſhops and Prieſts.\f* feare in the vvhole Church, and vpon al that heard theſe things.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 5.12 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Imber weneſday in whitſon-weeke. And within the octaue. & in a votiue of SS. Peter and Paul.\f* And by the handes of the Apoſtles vvere many ſignes and vvonders done among the people. And they vvere al vvith one accord in Salomons porche.
\v 13 But of the reſt none durſt ioyne them ſelues vnto them: but the people magnified them.
\v 14 And the multitude of men and vvomen that beleeued in our Lord, vvas more increaſed:
\v 15 ſo that they did bring forth the ſicke into the ſtreates, and laid them in beddes and couches, that vvhen Peter came, \f 1 \fr 5.15 \ft Peters ſhadovv.\f* his ſhadovv at the leaſt might ouerſhadovv any of them, and they all might be deliuered from their infirmities.
\v 16 And there ranne together vnto Hieruſalem the multitude alſo of the cities adioyning, bringing ſicke perſons and ſuch as vvere vexed of vncleane ſpirits: vvho vvere al cured. ⊢
\v 17 And the high prieſt riſing vp, and al that vvere vvith him, vvhich is the hereſie of the Sadduces, vvere repleniſhed vvith zeale:
\v 18 laid hands vpon the Apoſtles, and put them in the common priſon.
\v 19 But an Angel of our Lord by night opening the gates of the priſon, & leading them forth, \f 1 \fr 5.19 \ft An Angel leadeth them out of priſon.\f* ſaid,
\v 20 Goe: and ſtanding ſpeake in the temple to the people al the vvordes of this life.
\v 21 Vvho hauing heard this, early in the morning entred into the temple, and taught. And the high prieſt comming, and they that vvere vvith him, called together the Councel & al the auncients of the children of Iſraël: and they ſent to the priſon that they might be brought.
\v 22 But vvhen the miniſters vvere come, and opening the priſon, found them not: returning they told,
\v 23 ſaying, The priſon truely vve found ſhut vvith al diligence, and the keepers ſtanding before the gates: but opening it, vve found no man vvithin.
\v 24 And as ſoone as the Magiſtrate of the temple and the cheefe prieſts heard theſe vvordes, they vvere in doubt of them, vvhat vvould befall.
\v 25 And there came a certaine man and told them, That the men, loe, vvhich you did put in priſon, are in the temple ſtanding, and teaching the people.
\v 26 Then vvent the Magiſtrate vvith the miniſters, and brought them vvithout force, for they feared the people leſt they ſhould be ſtoned.
\v 27 And vvhen they had brought them, they ſet them in the Councel. And the high prieſt aſked them,
\v 28 ſaying, \x - \xt Act. 4, 18.\x* Commaunding vve commaunded you that you ſhould not teach in this name: and behold you haue filled Hieruſalem vvith your doctrine, and you vvil bring vpon vs the bloud of this man.
\v 29 But Peter anſvvering and the Apoſtles, ſaid, God muſt be obeied, rather then men.
\v 30 The God of our Fathers hath raiſed vp \sc IESVS\sc*, vvhom you did kil, hanging him vpon a tree.
\v 31 This Prince and Sauiour God hath exalted vvith his right hand, to giue repentance to Iſraël, and remiſſion of ſinnes.
\v 32 and vve are vvitneſſes of theſe vvordes, and the holy Ghoſt, vvhom God hath giuen to al that obey him.
\v 33 Vvhen they had heard theſe things, it cut them to the hart, and they conſulted to kil them.
\v 34 But one in the Councel riſing vp, a Phariſee named Gamaliel, a doctor of lavv honorable to al the people, commaunded the men to be put forth a vvhile.
\v 35 and he ſaid to them, Ye men of Iſraël, take heede to your ſelues touching theſe men vvhat you meane to doe.
\v 36 For before theſe daies there roſe \f 1 \fr 5.36 \ft \+it Theudas\+it*.\f* Theódas, ſaying he vvas ſome body, to vvhom conſented a numbre of men about foure hundred, vvho vvas ſlaine: and al that beleeued him, vvere diſperſed, and brought to nothing.
\v 37 After this fellovv there roſe Iudas of Galilee in the daies of the Enrolling, and drevv avvay the people after him, and he periſhed: and as many as euer conſented to him, vvere diſperſed.
\v 38 And novv therfore I ſay to you, depart from theſe men and let them alone: for if this counſel or vvorke be of men, it vvil be diſſolued:
\v 39 but if it be \f 1 \fr 5.39 \ft Time, and the euident ſucceſſe of Chriſtes Church and religion, proue it to be of God: no violence of the Iewes, no perſecution of the Heathen Princes, no endeuour of domeſtical Aduerſaries, heretikes, Schiſmatikes, or il liuers, preuailing againſt it. as on the other ſide, many attempts haue been made by Arius, Macedonius, Neſtorius, Luther, & the like, who thought them ſelues to be ſome body: but after they had plaied their partes a while, their memory is buried, or liueth only in malediction & infamie, & their ſcholers come to naught Therfore let no Cath. man be ſcandalized that this hereſie holdeth vp for a time. For the Arians & ſome others floriſhed much longer then theſe, and were better ſupported by Princes and learning, & yet had an end.\f* of God, you are not able to diſſolue \f 2 \fr 5.39 \fq ‘them \ft \+it it\+it*\f* ‘them’, leſt perhaps you be found to reſiſt God alſo. And they conſented to him.
\v 40 And calling in the Apoſtles, after they had ſcourged them, they charged them that they ſhould not ſpeake in the name of \sc IESVS\sc*, and dimiſſed them.
\v 41 And they vvent from the ſight of the councel reioycing, becauſe they vvere accounted vvorthy to ſuffer reproche for the name of \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 42 And euery day they ceaſed not in the temple and from houſe to houſe to teach and euangelize Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\c 6
\cd By occaſion of a murmur in the Church (vvhoſe number novv is ſo growen that it can not be numbred) Seuen of them being ordered by the Apoſtles in the holy order of Deacons: 8 one of them, Steuen, worketh great miracles: and is by ſuch as he confounded in diſputation, falſely accuſed in the Councel, of blaſphemie againſt the Temple and rites thereof.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* in thoſe daies the numbre of diſciples increaſing, there aroſe a murmuring of the Greekes againſt the Hebrues, for that their vvidovves vvere deſpiſed in the daily miniſterie.
\v 2 And the Tvvelue calling together the multitude of the diſciples, ſaid, It is not reaſon, that vve leaue the vvord of God, and ſerue tables.
\v 3 Conſider therfore brethren, ſeuen men of you of good teſtimonie, ful of the holy Ghoſt and vviſedom, vvhom vve may appoint ouer this buſines.
\v 4 But vve vvil be inſtant in praier and the miniſterie of the vvord.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 6.5 \ft The election of the 7 firſt Deacons.\f* And the ſaying vvas liked before al the multitude. And they choſe Steuen a man ful of faith and of the holy Ghoſt, and Philippe, and Próchorus, and Nicánor, and Timon, and Pármenas, and Nicolas a c \f 2 \fr 6.5 \ft \+it c προσήλυτον.\+it*\f* ſtranger of Antioche,
\v 6 Theſe they did ſet in the preſence of the Apoſtles: and praying \f 1 \fr 6.6 \ft Order of Deacons given by impoſition of hands. See Annot. Act. 13, 3.\f* they impoſed handes vpon them.
\v 7 And the vvord of God increaſed, and the number of the diſciples vvas multiplied in Hieruſalem excedingly: a great multitude alſo of the \f 1 \fr 6.7 \ft Now alſo the Prieſts and they of greater knowledge & eſtimation began to beleeue.\f* prieſts obeied the faith.
\v 8 b \f 1 \fr 6.8 \ft b The Epiſtle vpon S. Steuens day in Chriſtmas. And his Invention Aug. 3.\f* And Steuen ful of grace and fortitude did great vvonders & ſignes among the people.
\v 9 And there aroſe certaine of that vvhich is called the Synagogue of the Libertines, and of the Cyrenians, and of the Alexandrians, and of them that vvere of Cilicia and Aſia, diſputing vvith Steuen:
\v 10 and they could not reſiſt the vviſedom and the Spirit that ſpake.
\v 11 Then they ſuborned men, to ſay they had heard him ſpeake vvordes of blaſphemie againſt Moyſes and God.
\v 12 They therfore ſtirred vp the people, and the Auncients, and the Scribes: and running together they tooke him, and brought him into the Councel,
\v 13 and they ſet falſe vvitneſſes that ſaid, This man ceaſeth not to ſpeake vvordes againſt the holy place and the Lavv.
\v 14 for vve haue heard him ſay, that this ſame \sc IESVS\sc* of Nazareth ſhal deſtroy this place, and ſhal change the traditions, vvhich Moyſes deliuered vnto vs.
\v 15 And al that ſate in the Councel beholding him, ſavv his face as it vvere \f 1 \fr 6.15 \ft Such is the face of al conſtant & cheereful Martyrs, to their perſecutors and iudges.\f* the face of an Angel.
\c 7
\cd Steuen being permitted to anſvver, beginning at Abraham, ſhevveth that God was with their fathers both in other places, and alſo long before the Temple. 48 and that after it vvas built, it could not be (as they groſly imagined) a houſe for God to dvvel in. 51 then he inueigheth againſt their ſtifneckednes, and telleth them boldly of their traiterous murdering of Chriſt, as their fathers had done his Prophets afore him. 54 Whereat they being vvood, he ſeeth heauen open, and IESVS there in his Diuine Maieſtie. 57 Whereat they become more mad, ſo that they ſtone him to death (Saul conſenting) he commending his ſoul to IESVS, and humbly praying for them.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* the cheefe prieſt ſaid, Are theſe things ſo?
\v 2 Vvho ſaid, Ye men, brethren and fathers, heare. The God of glorie appeared to our father Abraham vvhen he vvas in Meſopotamia, before that he abode in Charan,
\v 3 and ſaid to him, \x - \xt Gen. 12, 1.\x* \it Goe forth out of thy countrie, and out of thy kinred, and come into a land that I ſhal ſhevv thee\it*.
\v 4 Then vvent he forth out of the land of the Chaldees, and dvvelt in Charan. And from thence, after his father vvas dead, he tranſlated him into this land, vvherein you doe novv dvvel.
\v 5 And he gaue him no inheritance in it, no not the paſe of a foote: and he promiſed to giue it him in poſſeſſion, and to his ſeede after him, vvhen as he had no childe.
\v 6 And God ſpake to him, \x - \xt Gen. 15, 13.\x* \it That his ſeede ſhal be a ſoiourner in a ſtrange countrie, and they ſhal ſubdue them to ſeruitude, and ſhal euil intreate them foure-hundred yeres\it*:
\v 7 \it and the nation vvhich they ſhal ſerue, vvil I iudge\it*, ſaid God. \it and after theſe things they ſhal goe forth, and \it* \f 1 \fr 7.7 \ft \+it c λατρεύσουσι.\+it*\f* \it c ſhal ſerue me in this place\it*.
\v 8 And he \x - \xt Gen. 17.\x* gaue him the teſtament of circumciſion, and ſo he \x - \xt Gen. 21.\x* begat Iſaac, and circumciſed him the eight day: and \x - \xt Gen. 25.\x* Iſaac, Iacob: and \x - \xt Gen. 29, 30. 35.\x* Iacob, the tvvelue Patriarches.
\v 9 And the Patriarches through emulation, \x - \xt Gen. 37.\x* ſold Ioſeph into Ægypt. and God vvas vvith him:
\v 10 and deliuered him out of al his tribulations. and he \x - \xt Gen. 41.\x* gaue him grace and vviſedom in the ſight of Pharao the king of Ægypt, and he appointed him Gouernour ouer Ægypt and ouer al his houſe.
\v 11 And there came famin vpon al Ægypt and Chanaan, and great tribulation: and our fathers found no victuals.
\v 12 But vvhen \x - \xt Gen. 42.\x* Iacob had heard that there vvas corne in Ægypt: he ſent our fathers firſt:
\v 13 and at the \x - \xt Gen. 45.\x* ſecond time Ioſeph vvas knovven of his brethren, and his kinred vvas made knovven vnto Pharao.
\v 14 And Ioſeph ſending, called thither Iacob his father and al his kinred in ſeuentie fiue ſoules.
\v 15 And \x - \xt Gen. 46.\x* Iacob deſcended into Ægypt: and \x - \xt Gen. 49. Gen. 50.\x* he died, and our fathers.
\v 16 And they vvere \x - \xt Gen. 23.\x* tranſlated into Sichem, and vvere \x - \xt Ios. 24.\x* laid in the ſepulchre that Abraham \f 3 \fr 7.16 \ft Tranſlation of Saincts bodies agreable to nature & Scripture. And the deſire to be buried in one place more then an other (vvhich the holy Patriarches alſo had \+it Gen. 49, 29. 50, 24. Hebr. 11, 22\+it*) hath ſometime great cauſes. \+it Aug. de Cur. pro mort. c. 1. & vlt\+it*.\f* bought for a price of ſiluer of the ſonnes of Hemor the ſonne of Sichem.
\v 17 And vvhen the time drevv neere of the promiſſe vvhich God had promiſed to Abraham, the people \x - \xt Exo. 1, 7.\x* increaſed and vvas multiplied in Ægypt,
\v 18 vntil an other king aroſe in Ægypt, that knevv not Ioſeph.
\v 19 This ſame circumuenting our ſtocke, afflicted our fathers: that they ſhould expoſe their children, to the end they might not be kept aliue.
\v 20 The ſame time vvas \x - \xt Exo. 2, 2.\x* Moyſes borne, and he vvas acceptable to God, who was nouriſhed three moneths in his fathers houſe.
\v 21 And vvhen he vvas expoſed, Pharaos daughter tooke him vp, and nouriſhed him for her ovvne ſonne.
\v 22 And Moyſes vvas inſtructed in al the vviſedom of the Ægyptians: and he vvas mightie in his vvordes and vvorkes.
\v 23 And \x - \xt Exo. 2, 11.\x* vvhen he vvas fully of the age of fourtie yeres, it came to his minde to viſite his brethren the children of Iſraël.
\v 24 And vvhen he had ſeen one ſuffer vvrong, he defended him: and ſtriking the Ægyptian, he reuenged his quarel that ſuſteined the vvrong.
\v 25 And he thought that his brethren did vnderſtand that God by his hand vvould ſaue them: but they vnderſtoode it not.
\v 26 And the day folovving \x - \xt Exo. 2, 13.\x* he appeared to them being at ſtrife: and he reconciled them vnto peace, ſaying, Men, ye are brethren, vvherfore hurt you one an other?
\v 27 But he that did the iniurie to his neighbour, repelled him, ſaying, \it Vvho hath appointed thee prince and iudge ouer vs\it*?
\v 28 \it Vvhat, vvilt thou kil me, as thou didſt yeſterday kil the Ægyptian\it*?
\v 29 And Moyſes fled vpon this vvord: and he became a ſoiourner in the land of Mádian, vvhere he begat tvvo ſonnes.
\v 30 And after fourtie yeres vvere expired, there \x - \xt Exo. 3, 2.\x* appeared to him in the deſert of mount Sina an Angel in the fire of the flame of a buſh.
\v 31 And Moyſes ſeeing it, marueled at the viſion. And as he vvent neere to vevve it, the voice of our Lord vvas made to him,
\v 32 \it I am the God of thy fathers, the God of Abraham, the God of Iſaac, and the God of Iacob\it*. And Moyſes being made to tremble, durſt not vevve it.
\v 33 And our Lord ſaid to him, \it Looſe of the ſhoe of thy feete: for the place vvherein thou ſtandeſt, is holy ground\it*.
\v 34 \it Seeing I haue ſeen the affliction of my people vvhich is in Ægypt, and I haue heard their groning, and am deſcended to deliuer them. And novv come, and I vvil ſend thee into Ægypt\it*.
\v 35 This Moyſes, vvhom they denied, ſaying, \it Vvho hath appointed the prince and Captaine\it*? him God ſent prince & \f 1 \fr 7.35 \ft Chriſt is our Redeemer, and yet Moyſes is here called redeemer. ſo Chriſt is our Mediator and Aduocate, and yet we may haue Saincts our inferior mediators and aduocates alſo. \+it See Annot. 1 Io. 2, 1\+it*.\f* redeemer, vvith the hand of the Angel that appeared to him in the buſh.
\v 36 He \x - \xt Exo. 7. 8. 9. 10. 11. 12, 37.\x* brought them forth doing vvonders and ſignes in the land of Ægypt, and in the redde ſea, and in the \x - \xt Exo. 16.\x* deſert fourtie yeres.
\v 37 This is that Moyſes vvhich ſaid to the children of Iſraël, \x - \xt Deu. 18.\x* \it A prophet vvil God raiſe vp to you of your ovvne brethren as my ſelf: him you ſhal heare\it*.
\v 38 This is he that \x - \xt Exo. 19, 3. 19.\x* vvas in the aſſemblie in the vvilderneſſe, vvith the Angel that ſpake to him in Mount-Sina, and vvith our fathers: vvho receiued the vvordes of life to giue vnto vs.
\v 39 To vvhom our fathers vvould not be obedient: but they repelled him, and in their hartes turned avvay into Aegypt,
\v 40 ſaying to Aaron: \x - \xt Exo. 32, 1.\x* \it Make vs goddes that may goe before vs. for this Moyſes that brought vs out of the land of Aegypt, vve knovv not vvhat is befallen to him\it*.
\v 41 And they made a calfe in thoſe daies, and offered ſacrifice to the Idol, and reioyced in the vvorkes of their ovvne handes.
\v 42 And God turned, and \x - \xt Amos. 5, 25.\x* deliuered them vp c \f 2 \fr 7.42 \ft \+it c λατρεύειν.\+it*\f* to ſerue the hoſt of heauen, as it is vvritten in the booke of the Prophets: \f 3 \fr 7.42 \ft For a iuſt puniſhment of their former offenſes God gaue them vp to vvorke vvhat vvickednes they vvould them ſelues, as it is ſaid of the Gentils \+it Rom. 1\+it*.\f* \it Did you offer victims and hoſtes vnto me fourtie yeres in the deſert, O houſe of Iſraël\it*?
\v 43 \it And you tooke vnto you the tabernacle of Moloch, and the ſtarre of your God Rempham, figures vvhich you made, to adore them. And I vvil tranſlate you beyond Babylon\it*.
\v 44 The tabernacle of teſtimonie vvas among our fathers in the deſert, as God ordained ſpeaking to Moyſes, \x - \xt Exo. 25, 40.\x* \it that he ſhould make it according to the forme vvhich he had ſeen\it*.
\v 45 Vvhich our fathers \x - \xt Ios. 3, 14.\x* vvith \f 2 \fr 7.45 \ft This is Ioſuè, ſo called in Greeke in type of our Sauiour.\f* c Ieſus receiuing, brought it in alſo into the poſſeſſion of the Gentiles, vvhich God expelled from the face of our fathers, till in the daies of Dauid,
\v 46 Vvho found grace before God, and \x - \xt Ps. 131, 5.\x* deſired that he might finde a tabernacle for the God of Iacob.
\v 47 And \x - \xt 1. Par. 17.\x* Salomon built him a houſe.
\v 48 But the Higheſt dvvelleth not in houſes \x - \xt Act. 17. 25.\x* made by hand, as the prophet ſaith:
\v 49 \x - \xt Eſa. 66, 1.\x* \it Heauen is my ſeate: and the earth the foote-ſtole of my feete. Vvhat houſe vvil you build me, ſaith our Lord? or vvhat place is there of my reſting\it*?
\v 50 \it Hath not my hand made al theſe things\it*?
\v 51 You ſtiffe-necked and of vncircumciſed hartes and eares, you alvvaies reſiſt the holy Ghoſt: as your fathers, your ſelues alſo.
\v 52 Vvhich of the prophets did not your fathers perſecute? And they ſlevve them that foretold of the comming of the Iuſt one, of vvhom novv
\v 53 you haue been betraiers and murderers: vvho receiued the Lavv by the diſpoſition of Angels, and haue not kept it.
\v 54 And hearing theſe things they vvere cut in their hartes, and they gnaſhed vvith their teeth at him.
\v 55 But he being ful of the holy Ghoſt, looking ſtedfaſtly vnto heauen, \f 1 \fr 7.55 \ft The comfort of al Martyrs.\f* c ſavv the glorie of God, and \sc IESVS\sc* ſtanding on the right hand of God. And he ſaid, Behold I ſee the heauens opened, and the Sonne of man ſtanding on the right hand of God.
\v 56 And they crying out vvith a loude voice, ſtopped their eares, & vvith one accord ranne violently vpon him.
\v 57 And caſting him forth vvithout the citie, they ſtoned him: and the vvitneſſes laid of their garments \x - \xt Act. 22, 20.\x* beſide the feete of a yong man that vvas called Saul.
\v 58 And they ſtoned Steuen inuocating, and ſaying: Lord \sc IESVS\sc*, receiue my ſpirit.
\v 59 And falling on his knees, he cried vvith a loude voice, ſaying: \f 1 \fr 7.59 \ft Euſebius Emiſſenus ſaith, vvhen he praieth for his perſecutors, he promiſeth to his vvorſhippers his manifeſt interceſſion & ſuffrages. in ho. S. Steph. & S. Auguſtine, Si Stephanus ſic non oraſſet, Eccleſia Paulum non haberet. Serm. 1 de S. Stephano.\f* Lord, lay not this ſinne vnto them. And vvhen he had ſaid this, he fel a ſleepe. And Saul vvas conſenting to his death.
\c 8
\cd So farre is perſecution from preuailing againſt the Church, that by it the Church groweth from Hieruſalem into al Ievvrie and Samaria. 5 The ſecond of the Deacons, Philip, conuerteth vvith his miracles the citie it ſelf of Samaria, and baptizeth them, euen Simon Magus alſo him ſelf among the reſt, 14 But the Apoſtles Peter and Iohn are the Miniſters to giue them the Holy Ghoſt. 18 Which miniſterie Simon Magus vvould bie of them. 26 The ſame Philip being ſent of an Angel to a great man of Æthiopia, Who came a Pilgrimage to Hieruſalem, firſt catechizeth him: 36 and then (he profeſſing his faith and deſiring Baptiſme) doth alſo baptize him. \f - \ft The 3. part. THE propagation of the Church from Hieruſalem into all Jewry and to Samaria.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* the ſame day there vvas made a great perſecution in the Church, vvhich vvas at Hieruſalem, and al vvere diſperſed through the countries of Ievvrie and Samaria, ſauing the Apoſtles.
\v 2 And deuout men \f 1 \fr 8.2 \ft \+it c curauerunt συνεκόμισαν\+it*\f* c tooke order for Steuens funeral, and made great mourning vpon him.
\v 3 But Saul \x - \xt Act. 22, 4.\x* vvaſted the Church: entring in from houſe to houſe, and dravving men and vvomen, deliuered them into priſon.
\v 4 They therfore that vvere diſperſed, paſſed through, \f 1 \fr 8.4 \ft This perſecution vvrought much good, being an occaſion that the diſperſed preached Chriſt in diuers Coũtries vvhere they came.\f* euangelizing the vvord.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 8.5 \ft The Epiſtle vpõ Thurſday in vvhitſunvveeke.\f* And Philippe deſcending into the citie of Samaria, preached \sc CHRIST\sc* vnto them.
\v 6 And the multitudes vvere attent to thoſe things vvhich vvere ſaid of Philippe, vvith one accord hearing, and ſeing the ſignes that he did.
\v 7 For many of them that had vncleane ſpirits, crying vvith a loud voice, vvent out. And many ſicke of the palſey and lame vvere cured.
\v 8 There vvas made therfore great ioy in that citie. ⊢
\v 9 And a certaine man named Simon, vvho before had been in that citie a Magician, ſeducing the nation of Samaria, ſaying him ſelf to be ſome great one:
\v 10 vnto vvhom al harkened from the leaſt to the greateſt, ſaying, This man is the povver of God, that is called great.
\v 11 And they vvere attent vpon him, becauſe a long time he had bewitched them vvith his magical practiſes.
\v 12 But vvhen they had beleeued Philippe euangelizing of the kingdom of God, and of the name of \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc*, they vvere baptized, men and vvomen.
\v 13 Then Simon alſo him ſelf beleeued: and being baptized, he cleaued to Philippe. Seing alſo ſignes and very great miracles to be done, he vvas aſtonied vvith admiration.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 8.14 \ft The Epiſtle vpõ Tueſday in vvhitſunvveeke. And in a votiue of the Holy Ghoſt.\f* And vvhen the Apoſtles vvho vvere in Hieruſalem, had heard that Samaria had receiued the vvord of God: they ſent vnto them \f 2 \fr 8.14 \ft \+it Sæpe ſibi ſocium Petrus facit eſſe Ioannem: Eccleſiæ quia virgo placet.\+it* Peter often maketh John his companion, becauſe the Church loveth a virgin. Arator apud Bedam in Act.\f* Peter & Iohn.
\v 15 Vvho vvhen they vvere come, praied for them, that they might receiue the holy Ghoſt.
\v 16 For he vvas not yet come vpon any of them, but they vvere only baptized in the name of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 17 Then did they impoſe their handes vpon them, and they receiued the holy Ghoſt. ⊢
\v 18 And vvhen Simon had ſeen that by the impoſition of the hand of the Apoſtles, the holy Ghoſt vvas giuen, he offered them money,
\v 19 ſaying, Giue me alſo this povver, that on vvhomſoeuer I impoſe my handes, he may receiue the holy Ghoſt.
\v 20 But Peter ſaid to him, Thy money be vvith thee vnto perdition: becauſe thou haſt thought that the gift of God is purchaſed vvith money.
\v 21 Thou haſt no part, nor lot in this vvord. For thy hart is not right before God.
\v 22 \f 1 \fr 8.22 \ft \+it c μετανόησον ἀπὸ See Apocal. 9, 21.\+it*\f* Doe penance therfore from this thy vvickedneſſe: and pray to God, if perhaps this cogitation of thy hart may be remitted thee.
\v 23 For I ſee thou art in the gall of bitternes and the obligation of iniquitie.
\v 24 And Simon anſvvering ſaid, Pray you for me to our Lord, that nothing come vpon me of theſe things vvhich you haue ſaid.
\v 25 And they in deede hauing teſtified and ſpoken the vvord of our Lord, returned to Hieruſalem, and euangelized to many countries of the Samaritans.
\v 26 b \f 1 \fr 8.26 \ft b The Epiſtle vpon Thurſday in Eaſter vveeke.\f* And an Angel of our Lord ſpake to Philippe, ſaying: Ariſe, and goe tovvard the South, to the vvay that goeth dovvne from Hieruſalem into Gaza: this is deſert.
\v 27 And riſing he vvent. And behold, a man of Æthiopia, an eunuch, of great authoritie vnder Candáce the Queene of the Æthiopians, vvho vvas ouer al her treaſures, vvas come to Hieruſalem \f 1 \fr 8.27 \ft Note that this Æthiopian came to Hieruſalem to adore, that is, on Pilgrimage. vvhere by vve may learne that it is an acceptable acte of religion to go from home to places of greater deuotiõ & ſanctificatiõ.\f* to adore:
\v 28 and he vvas returning and ſitting vpon his chariot, and reading Eſay the prophet.
\v 29 And the Spirit ſaid to Philippe, Goe neere, and ioyne thy ſelf to this ſame chariot.
\v 30 And Philippe running therevnto, heard him reading Eſay the prophet, and he ſaid: Trovveſt thou that thou vnderſtandeſt the things vvhich thou readeſt?
\v 31 Vvho ſaid, And \f 1 \fr 8.31 \ft The Scriptures are ſo vvritten that they can not be vnderſtood vvithout an interpreter, as eaſy as our Proteſtants make them. See S. Hierom \+it Ep. ad Paulinũ de omnibus diuinæ hiſtoriæ libris\+it*, ſet in the beginning of latin bibles.\f* hovv can I, vnleſſe ſome man ſhevv me? & he deſired Philippe that he vvould come vp and ſit vvith him.
\v 32 And the place of the ſcripture vvhich he did reade, vvas this: \x - \xt Es. 53, 7.\x* \it As a ſheepe to ſlaughter vvas he led: and as a lambe before his ſhearer, vvithout voice, ſo did he not open his mouth\it*.
\v 33 \it In humilitie his iudgement vvas taken avvay. His generation vvho ſhal declare, for from the earth ſhal his life be taken\it*?
\v 34 And the eunuch anſvvering Philip, ſaid, I beſeeche thee, of vvhom doth the Prophet ſpeake this? of him ſelf, or of ſome other?
\v 35 And Philip opening his mouth, and beginning from this ſcripture, euangelized vnto him \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 36 And as they vvent by the vvay, they came to a certaine vvater: and the eunuch ſaid, Lo vvater, \f 1 \fr 8.36 \fq ‘vvho \ft what\f* ‘vvho’ doth let me to be baptized?
\v 37 And Philip ſaid, If thou beleeue vvith al thy hart, thou maieſt. And he anſvvering ſaid, I beleeue that \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* is the ſonne of God.
\v 38 And he commaunded the chariot to ſtay: and both vvent dovvne into the vvater, Philip and the Eunuch, and he baptized him.
\v 39 And when they vvere come vp out of the vvater, the Spirit of our Lord tooke avvay Philip, and the eunuch ſavv him no more. And he vvent on his vvay reioycing.
\v 40 But Philip vvas found in Azótus, & paſſing through, he euangelized to al the cities, til he came to Cæſaréa. ⊢
\c 9
\cd Saul not content to perſecute ſo cruelly in Hieruſalem, 3 is in the vvay to Damaſcus told by our Lord IESVS of his vaine attempt, and miraculouſly conuerted to be an Apoſtle: and after great penance, reſtored to his ſight by Ananias, and baptized. 20 And preſently he dealeth mightily againſt the Ievves, prouing IESVS to be Chriſt, to their great admiration. 23 But ſuch is their obſtinacie, that they lay al Damaſcus to kil him, 26 From thence he goeth to Hieruſalem, and there ioyneth vvith the Apoſtles, and againe by the obſtinate Ievves his death is ſought. 31 The Church being novv grovven ouer al Ievvrie, Galilee, and Samaria, Peter viſiteth al: and in his viſitation, 33 healing a lame man, 36 and raiſing a dead vvoman, conuerteth very many.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* \x - \xt Act. 22, 4. Gal. 1, 13.\x*\f 2 \fr 9.1 \ft The Epiſtle upon the Conuerſion of S. Paul Ian. 25.\f* Saul as yet breathing forth threatenings and ſlaughter againſt the diſciples of our Lord, came to the high prieſt,
\v 2 and aſked letters of him vnto Damaſcus to the ſynagogs, that if he had found any men and vvomen of this vvay, he might bring them bound vnto Hieruſalem.
\v 3 And as he vvent on his iourney, it chaunced that he drevv nigh to Damaſcus: and \x - \xt 1. Cor. 15, 8.\x* ſodenly a light from heauen ſhined round about him.
\v 4 And falling on the ground, he heard a voice ſaying to him, \f 1 \fr 9.4 \ft The heretikes that conclude CHRIST ſo in heauen that he can be no vvhere els til the day of Iudgement, ſhal hardly reſolue a mã that vvould knovv vvhere CHRIST vvas vvhen he appeared here in the vvay, and ſpake theſe vvords to Saul.\f* Saul, Saul vvhy perſecuteſt thou me?
\v 5 Vvho ſaid, Vvho art thou Lord? And he, I am \sc IESVS\sc* vvhom thou doeſt perſecute. it is hard for thee to kicke againſt the pricke.
\v 6 And trembling and being aſtonied he ſaid, Lord, vvhat vvilt thou haue me to doe?
\v 7 And our Lord to him, Ariſe, and goe into the citie, and it ſhal be told thee vvhat thou muſt doe. But the men that vvent in companie vvith him, ſtood amaſed, hearing the voice, but ſeeing no man.
\v 8 And Saul roſe vp from the ground, and his eies being opened, he ſavv nothing. And they dravving him by the handes, brought him into Damaſcus.
\v 9 And he vvas three daies not ſeeing, and he did neither eate nor drinke.
\v 10 And there vvas a certaine diſciple at Damaſcus, named Ananias: and our Lord ſaid to him in a viſion, Ananias. But he ſaid, Loe, here I am Lord.
\v 11 And our Lord to him, Ariſe, & goe into the ſtreate that is called Straight: and ſeeke in the houſe of Iudas, one named Saul of Tarſus. for behold he prayeth.
\v 12 ( And he ſavv a man named Ananias, comming in and impoſing handes vpon him for to receiue his ſight.)
\v 13 But Ananias anſvvered, Lord, I haue heard by many of this man, hovv much euil he hath done to thy ſainctes in Hieruſalem:
\v 14 and here he hath authoritie from the cheefe prieſts to binde al that inuocate thy name.
\v 15 And our Lord ſaid to him, Goe, for a veſſel of election is this man vnto me, to carie my name before the Gentiles, and kinges, and the children of Iſraël.
\v 16 For I vvil ſhevv him hovv great things he muſt ſuffer for my name.
\v 17 And Ananias vvent, and entred into the houſe: and impoſing handes vpon him, he ſaid, Brother Saul, our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* hath ſent me, he that appeared to thee in the vvay that thou cameſt: that thou maieſt ſee and be filled vvith the holy Ghoſt.
\v 18 And forthvvith there fel from his eies as it vvere ſcales, and he receiued ſight. and riſing he vvas \f 1 \fr 9.18 \ft Paul alſo him ſelf, though vvith the diuine and heauenly voice proſtrated and inſtructed, yet vvas ſent to a man to receiue the Sacraments, & to be ioyned to the Church. \+it Auguſt. de doct. Chr. lib. 1. in proœm\+it*.\f* baptized.
\v 19 And vvhen he had taken meate, he vvas ſtrengthened. And he vvas vvith the diſciples that vvere at Damaſcus, for certaine daies.
\v 20 And incontinent entring into the ſynagogs, \f 1 \fr 9.20 \fq he \ft \+it in the Synagogues he.\+it*\f* he preached \sc IESVS\sc*, that this is the ſonne of God.
\v 21 And al that heard, vvere aſtonied, and ſaid, Is not this he that expugned in Hieruſalem thoſe that inuocated this name: and came hither to this purpoſe that he might bring them bound to the cheefe prieſts?
\v 22 But Saul vvaxed mightie much more, and confounded the Ievves that dvvelt at Damaſcus, affirming that this is \sc CHRIST\sc*. ⊢
\v 23 And vvhen many daies vvere paſſed, the Ievves conſulted that they might kil him.
\v 24 But their conſpiracie came to Sauls knovvledge. And \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11, 32.\x* they kept the gates alſo day and night, that they might kil him.
\v 25 But the diſciples taking him in the night, conueied him avvay by the vvall, letting him dovvne in a baſket.
\v 26 And vvhen he vvas come into Hieruſalem, he aſſaied to ioyne him ſelf to the diſciples, & al feared him, not beleeuing that he vvas a diſciple.
\v 27 But Barnabas tooke him & brought him to the Apoſtles, and told them hovv in the vvay he had ſeen our Lord, and that he ſpake vnto him, and hovv in Damaſcus he dealt confidently in the name of \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 28 And he vvas vvith them going in and going out in Hieruſalem, and dealing confidently in the name of our Lord.
\v 29 He ſpake alſo to the Gentiles, and diſputed vvith the Greekes: but they ſought to kil him.
\v 30 Vvhich vvhen the brethren had knovven, they brought him dovvne to Cæſaréa, and ſent him avvay to Tarſus.
\v 31 The \f 1 \fr 9.31 \ft The Church viſibly procedeth ſtill vvith much comfort & manifold increaſe euen by perſecution.\f* \sc CHVRCH\sc* truely through al Ievvrie & Galilee and Samaria had peace, & vvas edified, vvalking in the feare of our Lord, and vvas repleniſhed vvith the conſolation of the holy Ghoſt.
\v 32 And it came to paſſe, that Peter as he paſſed through al, came to the ſainctes that dvvelt at Lydda.
\v 33 and he found there a certaine man named Æneas, lying in his bed from eight yeres before, vvho had the palſey.
\v 34 And Peter ſaid to him, Æneas, our Lord \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* \f 1 \fr 9.34 \fq heale \ft healeth.\f* heale thee: ariſe, and make thy bed. And incontinent he aroſe.
\v 35 And al that dvvelt at Lydda and Saróna, ſavv him: vvho conuerted to our Lord.
\v 36 And in Ioppé there vvas a certaine diſciple named Tabitha, vvhich by interpretation is called Dorcas. This vvoman vvas ful of \f 1 \fr 9.36 \ft Behold good vvorkes and almes-deedes, & the force thereof reaching euen to the next life.\f* good vvorkes and almes-deedes vvhich ſhe did.
\v 37 And it came to paſſe in thoſe daies, that ſhe vvas ſicke and died. Vvhom vvhen they had vvaſhed, they laid her in an vpper chamber.
\v 38 And vvhereas Lydda vvas nigh to Ioppé, the diſciples hearing that Peter vvas in it, they ſent tvvo men vnto him, deſiring him, Be not loth to come ſo farre as to vs.
\v 39 And Peter riſing vp came vvith them. And vvhen he vvas come, they brought him into the vpper chamber: and al the vvidovves ſtoode about him vveeping, \f 1 \fr 9.39 \ft The praiers of our Almes folke & beadſmen may do vs great good euen after our departure. For if they procured her temporal life, much more may they helpe vs to Gods mercie and to releaſe of puniſhment in Purgatorie.\f* and ſhevving him the coates and garments vvhich Dorcas made them.
\v 40 And al being put forth, Peter falling on his knees praied, and turning to the body he ſaid: Tabitha, ariſe. And ſhe opened her eies: and ſeeing Peter, ſhe ſate vp.
\v 41 And giuing her his hand, he lifted her vp. And vvhen he had called the ſainctes and the vvidovves, he preſented her aliue.
\v 42 And it vvas made knovven through out al Ioppé: and many beleeued in our Lord.
\v 43 And it came to paſſe that he abode many daies in Ioppé, vvith one Simon a tanner.
\c 10
\cd Becauſe the Ievves ſo much abhorred the Gentils, for the better vvarrant of their Chriſtening, an Angel appeareth to Cornelius the deuout Italian. 9 and a viſion is ſhevved to Peter him ſelf ( the cheefe and Paſtor of al) 19 and the Spirit ſpeaketh to him, 34 yea and as he is Catechizing them about IESVS, 44 the holy Ghoſt commeth viſibly vpon them: and therfore not fearing any longer the offenſe of the Ievves, he commaundeth to baptize them. \f - \ft The 4. part. THE propagation of the Church to the Gentiles alſo.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* there vvas a certaine man in Cæſaréa, named Cornelius, Centurion of that vvhich is called the Italian band,
\v 2 religious, & fearing God vvith al his houſe, doing many almes-deedes to the people. And alvvaies praying to God,
\v 3 he ſavv in a viſion manifeſtly, about the ninthe houre of the day, an Angel of God comming in vnto him, and ſaying to him, Cornelius.
\v 4 But he beholding him, taken vvith feare, ſaid, Vvho art thou Lord? And he ſaid to him, Thy praiers and thy almes-deedes are aſcended into remembrance in the ſight of God.
\v 5 And novv ſend men vnto Ioppé, and call hither one Simon that is ſurnamed Peter.
\v 6 he lodgeth vvith one Simon a tanner, vvhoſe houſe is by the ſea ſide. he vvil tel thee vvhat thou muſt doe.
\v 7 And vvhen the Angel vvas departed that ſpake to him, he called tvvo of his houſhold, and a ſouldiar that feared our Lord, of them that vvere vnder him.
\v 8 To vvhom vvhen he had told all, he ſent them vnto Ioppé.
\v 9 And the next day vvhiles they vvere going on their iourney, and dravving nigh to the citie, Peter vvent vp into the higher partes, to pray about the ſixt houre.
\v 10 And being hungrie, he vvas deſirous to take ſomevvhat. And as they vvere preparing, there fel vpon him an exceſſe of minde:
\v 11 and he ſavv the heauen opened, and a certaine veſſel deſcending, as it vvere a great linnen ſheete vvith foure corners let dovvne from heauen to the earth,
\v 12 vvherein vvere al foure-footed beaſtes, and that creepe on the earth, and foules of the aire.
\v 13 And there came a voice to him, Ariſe Peter: kil, and eate.
\v 14 But Peter ſaid, God forbid, Lord: for I did neuer eate any common and vncleane thing.
\v 15 And \f 1 \fr 10.15 \ft Here God firſt vttered to Peter that the time vvas come to preach alſo to the Gentiles, and to cõuerſe vvith them for their ſaluation, no leſſe then vvith the Ievves, vvith ful freedõ to eate al meates vvithout reſpecte of the prohibition of certaine, made in the old Law.\f* a voice came to him againe the ſecond time, That vvhich God hath purified, doe not thou cal common.
\v 16 And this vvas done thriſe. and forthvvith the veſſel was taken vp againe into heauen.
\v 17 And vvhiles Peter doubted vvithin him ſelf, vvhat the viſion ſhould be that he had ſeen, behold the men that vvere ſent from Cornelius, inquiring for Simons houſe, ſtood at the gate.
\v 18 And vvhen they had called, they aſked, if Simon that is ſurnamed Peter, vvere lodged there.
\v 19 And as Peter vvas thinking of the viſion, the Spirit ſaid to him, Behold three men doe ſeeke thee.
\v 20 Ariſe therfore, and get thee dovvne, and goe vvith them, doubting nothing: for I haue ſent them.
\v 21 And Peter going dovvne to the men, ſaid, Behold, I am he vvhom you ſeeke: vvhat is the cauſe, for the vvhich you are come?
\v 22 Vvho ſaid, Cornelius the Centurion, a iuſt man & that feareth God, and hauing teſtimonie of al the nation of the Ievves, receiued an anſvver of an holy Angel to ſend for thee into his houſe, and to heare vvordes of thee.
\v 23 Therfore bringing them in, he lodged them. And the day folovving he aroſe and vvent vvith them: and certaine of the brethren of Ioppé accompanied him.
\v 24 And on the morow he entred into Cæſaréa. And Cornelius expected them, hauing called together his kinne, and ſpecial frendes.
\v 25 And it came to paſſe, Vvhen Peter vvas come in, Cornelius came to meete him, and falling at his feete adored.
\v 26 But Peter lifted him vp ſaying, Ariſe, my ſelf alſo am a man.
\v 27 And talking vvith him, he vvent in, and findeth many that vvere aſſembled,
\v 28 and he ſaid to them, You knovv hovv abominable it is for a man that is a Ievve, to ioyne, or to approche vnto a ſtranger: but God hath ſhevved to me, to call no man common or vncleane.
\v 29 For the vvhich cauſe, making no doubt, I came vvhen I vvas ſent for. I demaund therfore, for vvhat cauſe you haue ſent for me?
\v 30 And Cornelius ſaid, Foure daies ſince, vntil this houre, I vvas \f 1 \fr 10.30 \ft c At the time of praier ſpecially God ſendeth men comfortable viſitations.\f*\f 2 \fr 10.30 \ft \+it c In the Greeke, faſting & praying\+it*.\f* praying the ninthe houre in my houſe, and behold \f 3 \fr 10.30 \ft Note theſe apparitions and viſions to S. Peter, Cornelius, and others, in the Scriptures very oftē, againſt the incredulity of our Heretikes, that vvil beleeue neither viſion nor miracle, not expreſſed in Scripture: theſe being beleeued of Chriſtian men euē before they vvere vvritten.\f* a man ſtoode before me in vvhite apparel,
\v 31 and ſaid: Cornelius, thy praier is heard, and thy almes-deedes are in memorie in the ſight of God.
\v 32 Send therfore to Ioppé, and call hither Simon that is ſurnamed Peter: he lodgeth in the houſe of Simon a tanner by the ſea ſide.
\v 33 Immediatly therfore I ſent to thee: and thou haſt done vvel in comming. Novv therfore al vve are preſent in thy ſight, to heare al things vvhatſoeuer are commaunded thee of the Lord.
\v 34 And Peter opening his mouth, ſaid, In very deede I perceiue that God is not an accepter of perſons.
\v 35 but in euery nation, he that feareth him, and \f 1 \fr 10.35 \ft c Not ſuch as beleeue only, but ſuch as feare God and vvorke iuſtice, are acceptable to him.\f* vvorketh iuſtice, is acceptable to him.
\v 36 The word did God ſend to the children of Iſraël, preaching peace by \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* (this is Lord of al.)
\v 37 \x - \xt Mat. 4, 12.\x* You knovv the vvord that hath been made through al Ievvrie, for \f 2 \fr 10.37 \ft b The Epiſtle vpō Munday in Eaſter vveeke.\f* beginning from Galilee, after the baptiſme vvhich Iohn preached.
\v 38 \sc IESVS\sc* of Nazareth hovv God anointed him vvith the holy Ghoſt and vvith povver, vvho vvent through out doing good and healing al that vvere oppreſſed of the Deuil, becauſe God vvas vvith him.
\v 39 And vve are vvitneſſes of al things that he did in the countrie of the Ievves and in Hieruſalem, vvhom they killed hanging him vpon a tree.
\v 40 Him God raiſed vp the third day and gaue him to be made manifeſt,
\v 41 not to al the people, but to vs, vvho did eate and drinke vvith him after he roſe againe from the dead.
\v 42 \f 1 \fr 10.42 \ft The Epiſtle vpō Munday in vvhitsōvveeke.\f* And he commaunded vs to preach to the people, and to teſtifie that it is he that of God vvas appointed iudge of the liuing and of the dead.
\v 43 To him al the prophets giue teſtimonie, that al receiue remiſſion of ſinnes by his name, vvhich beleeue in him. ⊢
\v 44 As Peter vvas yet ſpeaking theſe vvordes, the holy Ghoſt fel vpon al that heard the vvord.
\v 45 And the faithful of the Circumciſion that came with Peter, vvere aſtonied, for that the grace of the holy Ghoſt vvas poured out vpon the Gentiles alſo.
\v 46 For they heard them ſpeaking with tonges, and magnifying God.
\v 47 Then Peter anſvvered, Can any man forbid vvater, that theſe ſhould not be baptized vvhich haue receiued the holy Ghoſt as vvel as vve?
\v 48 And he commaunded them to be baptized in the name of our Lord \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc*. ⊢ Then they deſired him that he vvould tarie vvith them certaine daies.
\c 11
\cd The Chriſtian Ievves reprehend the foreſaid fact of Peter in baptizing the Gentils 4 But he alleaging his foreſaid vvarrants, and ſhevving plainly that it vvas of God, 18 they like good Catholikes do yeld. 19 By the foreſaid perſecution, the Church is yet further dilated, not only into al Ievvrie, Galilee, and Samaria, but alſo into other Countries: ſpecially in Antiochia Syriæ the increaſe among the Greekes, is notable, firſt by the foreſaid diſperſed, 22 then by Barnabas, thirdly by him and Saul together: ſo that there beginneth the name of Chriſtians: 27 vvith perfite vnity betvvene them and the Church that vvas before them at Hieruſalem.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* the Apoſtles and brethren that vvere in Ievvrie, heard that the Gentiles alſo receiued the vvord of God.
\v 2 And vvhen Peter vvas come vp to Hieruſalem, they that vvere of the Circumciſion reaſoned againſt him, ſaying,
\v 3 Vvhy didſt thou enter in to men \f 1 \fr 11.3 \ft \+it c præputium habentes.\+it*\f* vncircumciſed, and didſt eate vvith them?
\v 4 But Peter began and declared to them the order, ſaying:
\v 5 \x - \xt Act. 10, 9.\x* I was in the citie of Ioppé praying, & I ſavv in an exceſſe of minde a viſion, a certaine veſſel deſcending as it vvere a great ſheete with foure corners let dovvne from heauen, and it came euen vnto me.
\v 6 Into vvhich I looking conſidered, and ſavv foure footed beaſtes of the earth, and cattel, and ſuch as creepe, and foules of the aire.
\v 7 And I heard alſo a voice ſaying to me, Ariſe Peter, kil and eate.
\v 8 And I ſaid, Not ſo Lord: for common or vncleane thing neuer entred into my mouth.
\v 9 And a voice anſvvered the ſecond time from heauen: That vvhich God hath made cleane, doe not thou call common.
\v 10 And this vvas done thriſe: and al vvere taken vp againe into heauen.
\v 11 And behold, three men immediatly vvere come to the houſe vvherein I vvas, ſent to me from Cæſaréa.
\v 12 And the ſpirit ſaid to me, that I ſhould goe vvith them, doubting nothing. And there came vvith me theſe ſixe brethren alſo: and vve vvent in to the mans houſe.
\v 13 And he told vs, hovv he had ſeen an Angel in his houſe, ſtanding and ſaying to him, Send to Ioppé, and cal hither Simon, that is ſurnamed Peter,
\v 14 vvho ſhal ſpeake to thee vvordes vvherein thou ſhalt be ſaued and al thy houſe.
\v 15 And vvhen I had begonne to ſpeake, the holy Ghoſt fel vpon them, as vpon vs alſo in the beginning.
\v 16 And I remembred the vvord of our Lord, according as he ſaid, \x - \xt Act. 1, 5.\x* \it Iohn in deede baptized vvith vvater, but you ſhal be baptized vvith the holy Ghoſt\it*.
\v 17 If therfore God hath giuen them the ſame grace, as to vs alſo that beleeued in our Lord \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc*: vvho vvas I that might prohibite God?
\v 18 Hauing heard theſe things, they \f 1 \fr 11.18 \ft Good Chriſtians heare and obey gladly ſuch truthes as be opened vnto them from God by their cheefe Paſtors, by viſion, reuelation, or otherwiſe.\f* held their peace: and glorified God, ſaying, God then to the Gentiles alſo hath giuen repentance vnto life.
\v 19 \x - \xt Act. 8, 1.\x* And they truely that had been diſperſed by the tribulation that vvas made vnder Steuen, vvalked through out vnto Phœnîce and Cypres & Antioche, ſpeaking the vvord to none, but to the Ievves only.
\v 20 But certaine of them vvere men of Cypres and Cyréne, vvho vvhen they vvere entred into Antioche, ſpake to the Greekes, preaching our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 21 \f 1 \fr 11.21 \ft The Epiſtle vpõ S. Barnabees day Iun. 11.\f* And the hand of our Lord vvas vvith them: and a great number of beleeuers vvas conuerted to our Lord.
\v 22 And the report came to the eares of the Church that vvas at Hieruſalem, touching theſe things: and they ſent \x - \xt Act. 4, 36.\x* Barnabas as farre as Antioche.
\v 23 Vvho vvhen he vvas come, and ſavv the grace of God, reioyced: and he exhorted al vvith purpoſe of hart to continew in our Lord:
\v 24 becauſe he vvas a good man, and ful of the holy Ghoſt and faith. And a great multitude vvas added to our Lord.
\v 25 And he vvent, forth to \x - \xt Act. 9, 30.\x* Tarſus, to ſeeke Saul:
\v 26 vvhom vvhen he had found, he brought him to Antioche. And they conuerſed there in the church a vvhole yere: and they taught a great multitude, ſo that the diſciples vvere at Antioche firſt named \f 1 \fr 11.26 \ft The name of CHRISTIANS.\f* \sc CHRISTIANS\sc*.
\v 27 And in theſe daies there came Prophets from Hieruſalem to Antioche,
\v 28 and one of them riſing, named Agabus, did by the Spirit ſignifie a great famine that ſhould be in the vvhole vvorld, vvhich fel vnder Claudius.
\v 29 And the diſciples according as eche man had, purpoſed euery one to ſend, for to ſerue the brethren that dvvelt in Ievvrie:
\v 30 vvhich alſo they did, ſending to the auncients by the handes of Barnabas and Saul.
\c 12
\cd Herod the firſt king that perſecuted the Church, hauing at Hieruſalem (vvhen Barnabas and Saul vvere there vvith the collation of the Antiochians) killed Iames the Apoſtle, 3 and to pleaſe the Iewes impriſoned Peter vvith the minde to kil him alſo, but fruſtrate by an Angel ſent of God at the continual praiers of the Church made for her cheefe Paſtor, 19 being puffed vp vvith ſuch pride that at Cæſaréa he refuſeth not to be honoured as God: 23 is miraculouſly ſtriken of Gods Angel. 24 And ſo after the perſecutors death, the Churches preaching proſpereth excedingly.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* \f 1 \fr 12.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpon SS. Peter and Paules day Iun. 29.\f* at the ſame time Herod the king ſet his handes, to afflicte certaine of the Church.
\v 2 And he killed Iames the brother of Iohn vvith the ſword.
\v 3 And ſeing that it pleaſed the Iewes, he added to apprehend Peter alſo. And it vvas the daies of the Azymes.
\v 4 Vvhom vvhen he had apprehended, he caſt into priſon, deliuering him to \f 1 \fr 12.4 \ft As Peters perſon vvas more notorious then others, & therfore better garded then other, for feare he ſhould eſcape: ſo Gods prouidence in preſeruing & deliuering him for the longer gouernment of his Church, is very maruelous.\f* foure quaterniõs of ſouldiars to be kept, meaning after the Paſche to bring him forth to the people.
\v 5 And Peter in deede vvas kept in priſon. But praier vvas made of the Church vvithout intermiſſion vnto God for him.
\v 6 And vvhen Herod vvould haue brought him forth, the ſame night Peter vvas ſleeping betwene two ſouldiars, bound vvith tvvo chaines: and the keepers before the doore kept the priſon.
\v 7 And behold an Angel of our Lord ſtoode in preſence: and light ſhined in the houſe: and ſtriking Peters ſide, he raiſed him, ſaying, Ariſe quickely. And the chaines fel from his handes.
\v 8 And the Angel ſaid to him, Gird thee, and put on thy ſhoes. And he did ſo. And he ſaid to him, Put thy garment about thee, & folovv me.
\v 9 And going forth he folovved him, & he knew not that it vvas true vvhich vvas done by the Angel: but he thought that he ſavv a viſion.
\v 10 And paſſing through the firſt & the ſecond vvatch, they came to the yron gate that leadeth to the citie, vvhich of it ſelf opened to them. And going out, they vvent forvvard one ſtreate: and incontinent the Angel departed from him.
\v 11 And Peter returning to him ſelf, ſaid: Novv I know in very deede that our Lord hath ſent his Angel, and deliuered me out of Herods hand, & from al the expectation of the people of the Iewes. ⊢
\v 12 And conſidering, he came to the \f 1 \fr 12.12 \ft It is much for the praiſe of theſe good Chriſtians that the aſſemble to Gods ſeruice & praier was kept in their houſes in the time of perſecution, & that the Apoſtle came thither ſtraight out of priſon, as his firſt refuge. as now Chriſtian people doe much to their commendation, in places where Hereſie doth reigne.\f* houſe of Marie the mother of Iohn, who vvas ſurnamed Marke, vvhere many were gathered and praying.
\v 13 And when he knocked at the doore of the gate, there came forth a vvenche to ſee, named Rhodè.
\v 14 And as ſhe knew Peters voice, for ioy ſhe opened not the gate, but running in ſhe told that Peter ſtoode before the gate.
\v 15 But they ſaid to her, Thou art mad. But ſhe affirmed that it vvas ſo. But they ſaid, It is his Angel.
\v 16 And Peter continued knocking. And when they had opened, they ſavv him, & were aſtonied.
\v 17 And beckening with his hand to them, that they ſhould hold their peace, he told hovv our Lord had brought him out of priſon, and he ſaid, Tel theſe things to Iames & to the brethren. And going forth he vvent \f 1 \fr 12.17 \ft Though God had ſo miraculouſly deliuered him, yet he would not tempt God by tarying among his perſecutors, but according to Chriſtes commaundement fled for a time.\f* into an other place.
\v 18 And when day vvas come, there vvas no litle a doe betvvene the ſouldiars, vvhat vvas become of Peter.
\v 19 And Herod, vvhen he had ſought him, and had not found, making inquiſition of the keepers, commaunded them to be led away: & going dovvne from Iewrie into Cæſaréa, there he abode.
\v 20 And he vvas angrie vvith the Tyrians and the Sidonians. But they vvith one accord came to him, and perſuading Blaſtus that vvas cheefe of the kings chamber, they deſired peace, for that their countries vvere nouriſhed by him.
\v 21 And vpon a day appointed, Herod being araied with kingly attire, ſate in the iudgement ſeate, and made an oration to them.
\v 22 And the people made acclamation, The voices of a God, & not of a man.
\v 23 And forthwith an Angel of our Lord \f 1 \fr 12.23 \ft Princes that take delite in the flattery and praiſes of the people, ſo much that they forget them ſelues to be men, & to giue the honour to God, may be warned by this example.\f* ſtrooke him, becauſe he had not giuen the honour to God: and being conſumed of vvormes, he gaue vp the ghoſt.
\v 24 But the vvord of our Lord increaſed and multiplied.
\v 25 And Barnabas and Saul returned from Hieruſalem, hauing accompliſhed their \x - \xt Act. 11, 29.\x* miniſterie, taking vvith them Iohn that vvas ſurnamed Marke.
\c 13
\cd The preachers of the Church of Antioche preparing them ſelues, the Holy Ghoſt out of them al, chooſeth Saul and Barnabas. 3 They being firſt conſecrated Biſhops, 4 goe their appointed circuite ouer al the land of Cypres, the Proconſul vvhereof is alſo conuerted, ſeing the miraculous excecation of a Iew by Paul. 13 Thence, into Pamphilia: 14 and Piſidia, vvhere in Antioche Paul preacheth to the Ievves, ſhewing that I E S V S is Chriſt, 38 and that in him is ſaluation, and not in their Lavv of Moyſes: 40 vvarning them to bevvare of the reprobation foretold by the Prophets. 44 But the next Sabboth, they blaſpheming, he in plaine termes forſaketh them, and turneth to the Gentiles. Whereat the Gentils be as glad on the contrarie ſide. 50 Finally the Ievves raiſing perſecution, they forſake them, pronouncing them to be obſtinate contemners. \f - \ft The 5 part. The taking of the Goſpel away from the obſtinate Iewes, and geuing of it to the Gentils, by the miniſterie of Paul and Barnabas.\f*
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* there vvere in the Church vvhich vvas at Antioche, Prophets and Doctors, among vvhom vvas Barnabas, & Simon that vvas called Niger, and Lucius of Cyréne, and Manahen vvho was the foſter-brother of Herod the Tetrarch, and Saul.
\v 2 And \f 1 \fr 13.2 \ft \+it c λειτουργούντων ἀυτῶν\+it*\f* as they vvere miniſtring to our Lord, and faſting, the holy Ghoſt ſaid: Separate me Saul and Barnabas vnto the vvorke, vvhereto I haue taken them.
\v 3 Then they faſting and praying, and impoſing hands vpon them, dimiſſed them.
\v 4 And they being ſent of the holy Ghoſt, went to Seleucia, and thence ſailed to Cypres.
\v 5 And vvhen they vvere come to Salamîna, they preached the vvord of God in the ſynagogs of the Iewes. And they had Iohn alſo in their miniſterie.
\v 6 And vvhen they had vvalked through out the vvhole iland as farre as Paphos, they found a certaine man that vvas a magician, a falſe-prophete, a Iew, vvhoſe name vvas \f 1 \fr 13.6 \fq ‘Bar-ieſu \ft \+it Barieu\+it*\f* ‘Bar-ieſu’,
\v 7 vvho vvas vvith the Proconſul Sergius Paulus a vviſe man. He ſending for Barnabas & Saul, deſired to heare the vvord of God.
\v 8 But Elymas the magician (for ſo is his name interpreted) reſiſted them, ſeeking to auert the Proconſul from the faith.
\v 9 But Saul, othervviſe Paul, repleniſhed vvith the holy Ghoſt, looking vpon him,
\v 10 ſaid: O ful of al guile, and al deceit, ſonne of the deuil, enemie of al iuſtice, thou ceaſeſt not to ſubuert the right vvaies of our Lord.
\v 11 And now behold the hand of our Lord vpon thee, and thou ſhalt be blind, not ſeing the ſunne vntil a time. And forthvvith there fel dimneſſe and darkeneſſe vpon him, and going about he ſought ſome body that vvould giue him his hand.
\v 12 Then the Proconſul, vvhen he had ſeen that vvhich vvas done, beleeued, marueling at the doctrine of our Lord.
\v 13 And vvhen Paul and they that vvere vvith him had ſailed from Paphos, they came to Pergè in Pamphylia. And Iohn departing from them, returned to Hieruſalem.
\v 14 But they paſſing through Pergè, came to Antioche in Piſidia: and entring into the ſynagogue on the day of the Sabboths, they ſate dovvne.
\v 15 And after the leſſon of the Lavv and the Prophets, the princes of the Synagogue ſent to them, ſaying, Men brethren, if there be among you any ſermon of exhortation to the people, ſpeake.
\v 16 And Paul riſing vp, and vvith his hand beckening for ſilence, ſaid, Ye men of Iſraël, and you that feare God, harken:
\v 17 The God of the people of Iſraël choſe our fathers, and exalted the people vvhen they vvere ſeiourners in the land of Ægypt, and in a mightie arme brought them out thereof, \f 1 \fr 13.17 \ft \+it Exod\+it*.\f*
\v 18 and for the ſpace of fourtie yeres tolerated their maners in the deſert. \f 1 \fr 13.18 \ft \+it Ioſue.\+it*\f*
\v 19 And deſtroying ſeuen nations in the land of Chanaan, by lot he deuided their land among them,
\v 20 as it vvere after foure hundred and fiftie yeres: and after theſe things he gaue Iudges, vntil Samuël the prophet.
\v 21 And thenceforth they deſired a king: and he gaue them \x - \xt Iud. 1. Reg. 8.\x* Saul the ſonne of Cis, a man of the tribe of Beniamin, fourtie yeres.
\v 22 and remouing him, he raiſed them vp \x - \xt 1. Reg. 16.\x* Dauid to be king: to vvhom giuing teſtimonie, he ſaid, \x - \xt Ps. 88, 21.\x* \it I haue found Dauid the ſonne of Ieſſe, a man according to my hart, vvho ſhall doe al my vvilles\it*.
\v 23 Of his ſeede God according to his \x - \xt Pſa. 131, 11.\x* promiſſe hath brought forth to Iſraël a Sauiour \sc IESVS\sc*,
\v 24 Iohn \x - \xt Lu. 3, 3.\x* preaching before the face of his comming, baptiſme of penance to al the people of Iſraël.
\v 25 And vvhen Iohn fulfilled his courſe, he ſaid, Vvhom doe \x - \xt Lu. 3, 15.\x* you thinke me to be? I am not he, but behold there commeth after me, vvhoſe ſhoes of his feete I am not vvorthie to vnlooſe.
\v 26 \f 1 \fr 13.26 \ft The Epiſtle vpõ Tueſday in Eaſter vveeke.\f* Men brethren, children of the ſtocke of Abraham, & they among you that feare God, to you the vvord of this ſaluation vvas ſent.
\v 27 For they that inhabited Hieruſalem, and the princes thereof, not knovving him, nor the voices of the prophets that are read euery Sabboth, iudging haue fulfilled them,
\v 28 and finding no cauſe of death in him, \x - \xt Lu. 23, 1.\x* deſired of Pilate, that they might kil him.
\v 29 And vvhen they had conſummated al things that vvere vvritten of him, taking him dovvne from the tree, they put him in a monument.
\v 30 But God raiſed him vp from the dead the third day:
\v 31 vvho vvas \x - \xt Act. 1, 3.\x* ſeen for many daies of them that came vp together vvith him from Galilee into Hieruſalem, vvho vntil this preſent are his vvitneſſes to the people.
\v 32 And vve preach vnto you that promiſſe vvhich vvas made to our fathers:
\v 33 that God hath fulfilled this ſame \x - \xq ‘to our children \xt Ps. 2, 7.\x* ‘to our children’, raiſing vp \sc IESVS\sc*, as in the ſecond Pſalme alſo it is vvritten: \f 2 \fr 13.33 \ft \+it ‘to vs their children\+it*,\f* \it My ſonne art thou, this day haue I begotten thee\it*.
\v 34 And that he raiſed him vp from the dead, not to returne novv any more into corruption, thus he ſaid, \x - \xt Eſa. 55, 3.\x* \it That I vvil giue you the holy things of Dauid faithful\it*.
\v 35 And therefore in an other place alſo he ſaith, \x - \xt Ps. 15, 10.\x* \it Thou ſhalt not giue thy holy one to ſee corruption\it*.
\v 36 For Dauid in his generation vvhen he had ſerued, according to the vvil of God ſlept: and he vvas laid to his fathers & ſavv corruption.
\v 37 But he vvhom God hath raiſed vp, ſavv no corruption.
\v 38 Be it knovven therfore to you, men brethren, that through him, forgiueneſſe of ſinnes is preached to you, from al the things from the vvhich you could not be iuſtified by the lavv of Moyſes.
\v 39 In him euery one that beleeueth, is iuſtified.
\v 40 Take heede therfore leſt that come vpon you vvhich is ſpoken in the prophets,
\v 41 \x - \xt Abac. 1, 5.\x* \it See ye contemners, and vvonder, and periſh: becauſe I vvorke a vvorke in your daies, a vvorke vvhich you vvil not beleeue, if any man ſhal tel it you\it*.
\v 42 And they going forth, \f 1 \fr 13.42 \ft \+it c the Gentiles deſired\+it*\f* they deſired them that the Sabboth folovving they would ſpeake vnto them theſe wordes.
\v 43 And vvhen the ſynagogue vvas dimiſſed, many of the Ievves, and of the \f 1 \fr 13.43 \ft \+it c deuout proſelytes,\+it*\f* ſtrangers ſeruing God, folovved Paul & Barnabas: vvho ſpeaking exhorted them to continue in the grace of God.
\v 44 But the next Sabboth the vvhole citie almoſt aſſembled to heare the vvord of God.
\v 45 And the Ievves ſeing the multitudes, vvere repleniſhed vvith enuy, & contradicted thoſe things vvhich vvere ſaid of Paul, blaſpheming.
\v 46 Then Paul and Barnabas conſtantly ſaid, To you it behoued vs firſt to ſpeake the vvord of God: but becauſe \f 1 \fr 13.46 \ft The Ievves of their ovvne free vvil repelling the truth, are vnvvorthy of Chriſt and vvorthily forſaken: and the Gentils though they beleeued ſpecially by Gods grace and preordination, yet they beleeue alſo by their ovvne free vvil, vvhich ſtandeth vvel vvith Gods prouidence.\f* you repell it, and iudge your ſelues vnvvorthie of eternal life: behold vve turne to the Gentils.
\v 47 For ſo our Lord commaunded vs: \x - \xt Eſa. 49, 6.\x* \it I haue put thee to be the light of the Gentils: that thou maieſt be ſaluation vnto the vtmoſt of the earth\it*.
\v 48 And the Gentils hearing it, vvere glad, and glorified the vvord of our Lord: and there beleeued as many as vvere preordinate to life euerlaſting.
\v 49 And the vvord of our Lord vvas ſpred through out the vvhole countrie.
\v 50 But the Ievves ſtirred vp religious and honeſt vvomen, and the cheefe of the citie, and raiſed perſecution againſt Paul and Barnabas: and they did caſt them forth out of their coaſtes.
\v 51 But they \x - \xt Lu. 9, 5.\x* ſhaking of the duſt of their feete againſt them, came to Icónium.
\v 52 The diſciples alſo vvere repleniſhed vvith ioy and vvith the holy Ghoſt.
\c 14
\cd Next in Iconium they preach, vvhere many being conuerted of both ſortes, the obſtinate Ievves raiſe perſecution. 6 Then in the tovvnes of Lycaonia, vvhere the Heathen firſt ſeing that Paul had healed one borne lame, are hardly perſuaded but they are Gods. 18 but aftervvard, by the inſtigation of the malitious Ievves, they ſtone Paul, leauing him for dead. 20 And ſo hauing done their circuite, they returne the ſame vvay confirming the Chriſtians, and making Prieſts for euery Church. 25 And being come home to Antioche in Syria, they report al to the Church there.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* it came to paſſe at Iconium, that they entred together into the ſynagogue of the Ievves, and ſo ſpake, that a very great multitude of Iewes and of the Greekes did beleeue.
\v 2 But the Iewes that were incredulous, ſtirred vp and incenſed the hartes of the Gentils to anger againſt the brethren.
\v 3 A long time therfore they abode, dealing confidently in our Lord, vvho gaue teſtimonie to the vvord of his grace, graunting ſignes and vvonders to be done by their handes.
\v 4 And the multitude of the citie vvas deuided: and certaine of them in deede vvere vvith the Iewes, but certaine vvith the Apoſtles.
\v 5 And when the Gentils and the Iewes vvith their princes had made an aſſault, to vſe them contumeliouſly, and to ſtone them,
\v 6 vnderſtanding it, they fled to the cities of Lycaónia, Lyſtra and Derbé, and the vvhole countrie about, and there they vvere euangelizing.
\v 7 And a certaine man at Lyſtra impotent of his feete ſate there, lame from his mothers wombe, that neuer had vvalked.
\v 8 This ſame heard Paul ſpeaking. Vvho looking vpon him, and ſeeing that he had faith for to be ſaued,
\v 9 he ſaid vvith a loud voice, Stand vp right on thy feete. And he leaped & vvalked.
\v 10 And the multitudes vvhen they had ſeen vvhat Paul had done, lifted vp their voice in the lycaónian tongue, ſaying, Gods made like to men, are deſcended to vs.
\v 11 And they called Barnabas, Iupiter: but Paul, Mercurie, becauſe he vvas the cheefe ſpeaker.
\v 12 The Prieſt alſo of \f 1 \fr 14.12 \ft \+it c διὸς τοῦ ὄντος\+it*\f* Iupiter that vvas before the citie, bringing oxen & garlands before the gates, vvould vvith the people ſacrifice.
\v 13 Vvhich thing vvhen the Apoſtles Barnabas & Paul heard, renting their coates, they leaped forth into the multitudes, crying & ſaying,
\v 14 Ye men, vvhy doe you theſe things? Vve alſo are mortal, men like vnto you, preaching to you for to conuert from theſe vaine things, to the liuing God that made the heauen, and the earth, and the ſea, and al things that are in them:
\v 15 vvho in the generations paſt ſuffred al the Gentils to goe their ovvne vvaies.
\v 16 Hovvbeit he left not him ſelf \f 1 \fr 14.16 \ft The Heathen might by the daily benefites of God haue knowen him at the leaſt to haue beene their Creatour and only Lord, though the myſterie of our Redemption vvere not opened to them.\f* without teſtimonie, being beneficial from heauen, giuing raines, and fruiteful ſeaſons, filling our hartes with foode & gladnes.
\v 17 And ſpeaking theſe things, they ſcarſe appeaſed the multitudes from ſacrificing to them.
\v 18 But there came in certaine Iewes from Antioche and Icónium: and perſuading the multitudes, and \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11, 25.\x* ſtoning Paul, they drew him out of the citie, thinking him to be dead.
\v 19 But the diſciples compaſſing him round about, he riſing vp, entred into the citie, and the next day he vvent forth with Barnabas vnto Derbè.
\v 20 And vvhen they had euangelized to that citie, and had taught many, they returned to Lyſtra and Icónium, and to Antioche:
\v 21 confirming the hartes of the diſciples, and exhorting them to continue in the faith, and that by many tribulations vve muſt enter into the kingdom of God.
\v 22 And when \f 1 \fr 14.22 \ft Vve ſee by this, firſt that SS. Paul & Barnabas were Biſhops, hauing here authoritie to giue holy Orders: ſecondly, that there vvas euen then a difference betwixt Biſhops and Prieſts, though the name in the primitiue Church vvas often vſed indifferently: laſtly, that alvvaies faſting & praying were preparatiues to the giuing of holy Orders.\f* they had ordained to them Prieſts in euery Church, and had praied with faſtings, they commended them to our Lord in whom they beleeued.
\v 23 And paſſing through Piſidia, they came into Pamphylia,
\v 24 and ſpeaking the word of our Lord in Pergé, they went downe into Attalia:
\v 25 and from thence they ſailed to Antioche, \x - \xt Act, 13, 2.\x* whence they had been deliuered to the grace of God vnto the vvorke vvhich they accompliſhed.
\v 26 And when they were come, and had aſſembled the Church, they reported what great things God had done with them, & that he had opened a doore of faith to the Gentils.
\v 27 And they abode no litle time vvith the diſciples.
\c 15
\cd Some of thoſe Iewes alſo that vvere Chriſtians, do fall, and are authors of the Hereſie of Iudaizing. 2 They referre the matter to Councel: 7 Wherein after great diſputation, Peter ſtriking the ſtroke, 12 and other confirming his ſentence vvith miracles, 13 and with Scriptures: 22 the Apoſtles and Prieſts do write and commaund in the name of the Holy Ghoſt what is to be done. 30 And the faithful thereby are ſtraightvvaies quieted in minde. 36 After vvhich, Paul and Barnabas thinking to goe againe their aboue ſaid circuite together, are by occaſion of Marke parted, to the greater increaſe of the Church.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* certaine comming dovvne from Iewrie, taught the brethren: That \x - \xt Gal. 5, 2.\x* vnles you be circumciſed according to the maner of Moyſes, you can not be ſaued.
\v 2 No litle ſedition therfore being riſen to Paul and Barnabas againſt them, they appointed that Paul and Barnabas ſhould goe vp, & certaine others of \f 1 \fr 15.2 \fq ‘the reſt \ft \+it them,\+it*\f* ‘the reſt’, to the Apoſtles and \f 2 \fr 15.2 \ft \+it c ρεσβυτέρους.\+it*\f* prieſts vnto Hieruſalem, vpon this queſtion.
\v 3 They therfore being brought on their vvay by the Church, paſſed through Phœnîce and Samaria, reporting the conuerſion of the Gentiles: and they made great ioy to al the brethren.
\v 4 And when they were come to Hieruſalem, they vvere receiued of the Church and of the Apoſtles and \f 1 \fr 15.4 \ft c Ancients here, & often in this chapter, are the ſame that Prieſts verſ. 2. as S. Hierom taketh it alſo \+it 1. Pet. 5.\+it* &\f*\f 2 \fr 15.4 \ft \+it c πρεσβυτέρων\+it*\f* Auncients, declaring vvhatſoeuer God had done vvith them.
\v 5 And there aroſe certaine of the hereſie of the Phariſees that beleeued, ſaying, That they muſt be circumciſed, commaunded alſo to keepe the lavv of Moyſes.
\v 6 And the Apoſtles and Auncients aſſembled to conſider of this vvord.
\v 7 And vvhen there vvas made a \x - \xt Act. 10, 20.\x* great diſputation, Peter riſing vp ſaid to them, Men brethren, you knovv that \f 2 \fr 15.7 \ft b See the Annot. verſ. 28 toward the ende. · Act. 10, 20.\f* of old daies God among vs choſe, that by my mouth the Gentiles ſhould heare the vvord of the Goſpel, and beleeue.
\v 8 And God vvhich knovveth the hartes, gaue teſtimonie, \x - \xt Act. 10, 45.\x* giuing vnto them the holy Ghoſt as vvel as to vs,
\v 9 and hath put no difference betvvene vs and them, \f 1 \fr 15.9 \ft By that faith which worketh by charitie. for a dead faith can not purifie the hart of man. See chap. \+it 16, 31\+it*.\f* by faith purifying their hartes.
\v 10 Novv therfore vvhy tempt you God, to put a yoke vpon the neckes of the diſciples, vvhich neither our fathers nor vve haue been able to beare?
\v 11 but by the grace of our Lord \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* vve beleeue to be ſaued, in like maner as they alſo.
\v 12 And al the multitude held their peace: and they heard Barnabas and Paul telling vvhat great ſignes and vvonders God had done among the Gentiles by them.
\v 13 And after they held their peace, Iames anſwered, ſaying, Men brethren, heare me.
\v 14 Simon hath told hovv God firſt viſited to take of the Gentiles a people to his name.
\v 15 And to this accord the vvordes of the prophets, as it is vvritten:
\v 16 \x - \xt Tim. 3, 5.\x* \it After theſe things I vvil returne, and vvil reedifie the tabernacle of Dauid, which was fallen, and the ruines thereof I vvil reedifie, and ſet it vp\it*:
\v 17 \it that the reſidue of men may ſeeke after the Lord, and al nations vpon whom my name is inuocated, ſaith the Lord that doeth theſe things\it*.
\v 18 To our Lord vvas his ovvne vvorke knovven from the beginning of the vvorld.
\v 19 For the vvhich cauſe I iudge, that they vvhich of the Gentiles are conuerted to God, are not to be diſquieted,
\v 20 but to write vnto them that they refraine them ſelues from the contaminations of Idols, and fornication, and ſtrangled things, and bloud.
\v 21 For Moyſes of old times hath in euery citie them that preach him in the ſynagogs, vvhere he is read euery Sabboth.
\v 22 Then it pleaſed the Apoſtles and Auncients vvith the vvhole Church, to choſe men out of them, & to ſend to Antioche vvith Paul and Barnabas, Iudas, vvho vvas ſurnamed Barſabas, & Silas, cheefe men among the brethren,
\v 23 \f 1 \fr 15.23 \ft c Other latin copies and the greeke read thus, \+it Writing by their handes an epiſtle conteining theſe things\+it*.\f* vvriting by their handes. The Apoſtles and Auncients, the brethren, to the brethren of the Gentiles that are at Antioche and in Syria and Cilicia, greeting.
\v 24 Becauſe vve haue heard that certaine going forth from vs, haue troubled you vvith vvordes, ſubuerting your ſoules, to vvhom vve gaue no commaundement:
\v 25 it hath pleaſed vs being gathered in one, to choſe out men and to ſend them vnto you vvith our deereſt Barnabas and Paul,
\v 26 men that haue giuen their liues for the name of our Lord \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc*:
\v 27 We haue ſent therfore Iudas & Silas, vvho them ſelues alſo wil in vvordes report vnto you the ſame things.
\v 28 For it hath ſeemed good to the holy Ghoſt & to vs, to lay no further burden vpon you then theſe neceſſarie things:
\v 29 that you abſteine from the things immolated to Idols, and bloud, and that vvhich is ſtrangled, and fornication, from the vvhich things keeping your ſelues, you ſhal doe vvell. Fare ye vvel.
\v 30 They therfore being dimiſſed went downe to Antioche: and gathering the multitude, deliuered the epiſtle.
\v 31 Vvhich vvhen they had read, they reioyced vpon the conſolation:
\v 32 but Iudas and Silas, them Selues alſo being prophets, vvith many vvordes comforted the brethren, and confirmed them.
\v 33 And hauing ſpent ſome time there, they vvere vvith peace dimiſſed of the brethren vnto them that had ſent them.
\v 34 But it ſeemed good vnto Silas to remaine there: and Iudas departed alone:
\v 35 and Paul and Barnabas taried at Antioche, teaching and euangelizing vvith many others the vvord of our Lord.
\v 36 And after certaine daies, Paul ſaid to Barnabas, Let vs returne and \x - \xt Act. 13, 14.\x* viſite our brethren in al cities vvherein vve \f 2 \fr 15.36 \ft Hereof our Catholike Biſhops tooke vp the neceſſarie vſe of often viſiting their flockes & cures committed to their charge, for confirmation in faith & vertue, & reformation of maners both of clergie & laitie. · Act. 13, 14.\f* haue preached the vvord of our Lord, how they doe.
\v 37 And Barnabas vvould haue taken vvith them Iohn alſo that vvas ſurnamed Marke.
\v 38 But Paul deſired that he (as vvho \x - \xt Act. 13, 13.\x* had departed from them out of Pamphylia, and had not gone vvith them to the vvorke) might not be receiued.
\v 39 And there roſe a diſſention, ſo that they departed one from an other, & that Barnabas in deede taking Marke ſailed to Cypres.
\v 40 But Paul choſing Silas departed, being deliuered of the brethren to the grace of God.
\v 41 And he vvalked through Syria and Cilicia, confirming the Churches: \x - \xt Luc. 10, 11.\x* commaunding them to keepe the præcepts of the Apoſtles and the Auncients.
\c 16
\cd Paul having for his part viſited the Churches of Syria, Cilicia, and Lycaonia, deliuering vnto them vvithal to keepe the Decrees of the Council: 6 beginneth a nevv iourney, ouer Phrygia, Galatia, Myſia: 8 Yea into Europe alſo he paſſeth, admoniſhed by a viſion, and commeth into Macedonia, 12 and there he beginneth the Church of the Philippians, vvorking miracles, and ſuffering perſecution.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* he came to Derbé and Lyſtra. And behold, there vvas a certaine diſciple there named Timothee, the ſonne of \f 1 \fr 16.1 \fq `a vvidovv \ft \+it Jew\+it*\f* `a vvidovv' vvoman that beleeued, of a father a Gentile.
\v 2 To this man the brethren that vvere in Lyſtra and Iconium, gaue a good teſtimonie.
\v 3 Him Paul vvould haue to goe forth vvith him: and taking him he circumciſed him becauſe of the Ievves that vvere in thoſe places. For they al knevv that his father vvas a Gentile.
\v 4 And vvhen they paſſed through the cities, they deliuered vnto them to keepe the \f 1 \fr 16.4 \ft Here againe they take order that the decrees and articles of faith agreed vpon in the Councel of Ieruſalé, ſhould be executed & obſerued. vvhereby vve ſee both the great authoritie of Councels, & the diligence that al Prelates ought to haue to ſee the decrees & Canons of the Coũcels put in executiõ.\f* decrees that vvere decreed of the Apoſtles and Auncients which were at Hieruſalem.
\v 5 And the Churches vvere confirmed in faith, and did abound in number daily.
\v 6 And paſſing through Phrygia and the countrie of Galatia, they vvere \f 1 \fr 16.6 \ft This people had not the Goſpel denied vnto them altogether, but for a time : becauſe (as Venerable Bede thinketh) God foreſavv they vvould not beleeue, & ſo ſhould haue been more greuouſly damned.\f* forbidden by the holy Ghoſt to preach the vvord in Aſia.
\v 7 And vvhen they vvere come into Myſia, they attempted to goe into Bithynia: and the Spirit of \sc IESVS\sc* permitted them not.
\v 8 And vvhen they had paſſed through Myſia, they vvent dovvne to Troas:
\v 9 and a viſion by night vvas ſhevved to Paul: There vvas a certaine man of Macedónia ſtanding and beſeeching him, and ſaying, paſſe into Macedónia, and helpe vs.
\v 10 And as ſoone as he had ſeen the viſion, forthvvith vve ſought to goe into Macedónia, being aſſured that God had called vs to euangelize to them.
\v 11 And ſailing from Troas, vve came vvith a ſtraight courſe to Samothrácia, and the day folovving to Neapolis:
\v 12 and from thence to Philippi, vvhich is the firſt citie of the part of Macedonia, a \f 1 \fr 16.12 \ft \+it Colonia\+it*, is ſuch a citie vvhere the moſt inhabitants are ſtrangers, ſent thither from other great cities & States, namely from the Romanes.\f* \it colónia\it*. And vve were in this citie certaine daies, abiding.
\v 13 And vpon the day of the Sabboths, vve vvent forth vvithout the gate beſide a riuer, vvhere it ſeemed that there vvas praier: & ſitting vve ſpake to the vvomen that vvere aſſembled.
\v 14 And a certaine vvoman named Lydia, a ſeller of purple of the citie of the Thyatirians, one that vvorſhipped God, did heare: vvhoſe hart our Lord opened to attend to thoſe things vvhich vvere ſaid of Paul.
\v 15 And vvhen ſhe vvas baptized, and her houſe, ſhe beſought vs, ſaying: If you haue iudged me to be faithful to our Lord, enter in vnto my houſe, and tarie. And ſhe conſtrained vs.
\v 16 And it came to paſſe as vve vvent to praier, a certaine vvenche hauing a Pythónical ſpirit, mette vs, that brought great gaine to her maiſters by diuîning.
\v 17 This ſame folovving Paul and vs, cried ſaying, \f 1 \fr 16.17 \ft Either the Diuel vvas compelled by the vertue of Paules preſence to ſay truth, or els (as ſuch do often times) he ſpake truth novv, that they might the more truſt him, and he better beguile them at other times.\f* Theſe men are the ſeruants of the high God, vvhich preach vnto you the vvay of ſaluation.
\v 18 And this ſhe did many daies. And Paul being ſorie, and turning, ſaid to the ſpirit, I commaund thee in the name of \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* to goe out from her. And he vvent out the ſame houre.
\v 19 But her maiſters ſeeing that the hope of their gaine vvas gone, apprehending Paul and Silas, brought them into the market place to the Princes:
\v 20 and preſenting them to the magiſtrates, they ſaid, Theſe men trouble our citie, being Ievves:
\v 21 and they preach a faſhion vvhich it is not lavvful for vs to receiue, nor doe, being Romanes.
\v 22 And the people ranne againſt them: and the magiſtrates tearing their coates, commaunded them to be beaten vvith roddes.
\v 23 And vvhen they had laid \x - \xt 2. Cor. 11.\x* many ſtripes vpon them, they did caſt them into priſon, commaunding the keeper that he ſhould keepe them diligently.
\v 24 VVho vvhen he had receiued ſuch commaundement, caſt them into the inner priſon, and made their feete faſt in the ſtockes.
\v 25 And at mid-night, Paul and Silas praying, did praiſe God. And they that vvere in priſon, heard them.
\v 26 But ſodenly there vvas made a great earthquake, ſo that the foundations of the priſon vvere ſhaken. And forthvvith al the doores vvere opened: and the bands of al vvere looſed.
\v 27 And the keeper of the priſon vvaked out of his ſleepe, and ſeeing the doores of the priſon opened, dravving out his ſvvord, vvould haue killed him ſelf, ſuppoſing that the priſoners had been fled.
\v 28 But Paul cried vvith a loud voice, ſaying, Doe thy ſelf no harme, for vve are al here.
\v 29 And calling for light, he vvent in, and trembling fel dovvne to Paul and Silas at their feete:
\v 30 and bringing them forth, he ſaid, Maiſters, vvhat muſt I doe that I may be ſaued?
\v 31 But they ſaid, \f 1 \fr 16.31 \ft It is no other faith that ſaueth but that vvhich vvorketh by Charitie. Aug. Enchirid. c. 67.\f* Beleeue in our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*: and thou ſhalt be ſaued and thy houſe.
\v 32 And they preached the vvord of our Lord to him vvith al that vvere in his houſe.
\v 33 And he taking them in the ſame houre of the night, \f 1 \fr 16.33 \ft Happie Gailers that doe mercie tovvard their godly priſoners, and receiue againe by them ſuch ſpiritual benefites.\f* c vvaſhed their vvoundes: and him ſelf vvas baptized and al his houſe incontinent.
\v 34 And vvhen he had brought them into his ovvne houſe, he laid the table for them, and reioyced vvith al his houſe, beleeuing God.
\v 35 And vvhen day vvas come, the magiſtrates ſent the ſergeants, ſaying, Let thoſe men goe.
\v 36 And the keeper of the priſon told theſe vvordes to Paul, That the magiſtrates haue ſent that you ſhould be let goe. novv therfore departing, goe ye in peace.
\v 37 But Paul ſaid to them: Being vvhipped openly, vncõdemned, men that are Romanes, they haue caſt vs into priſon: & novv do they ſend vs out ſecretly? Not ſo, but let them come, & let vs out them ſelues.
\v 38 And the ſergeants reported theſe wordes to the magiſtrates. And they vvere afraid hearing that they vvere Romanes:
\v 39 and comming they beſought them, & bringing them forth they deſired them to depart out of the citie.
\v 40 And going out of the priſon, they entred in vnto Lydia: and hauing ſeen the brethren, they comforted them, and departed.
\c 17
\cd Hovv in other parts of Macedonia he planted the Church, and namely at Theſſalonica, 5 where the obſtinate Iewes are ſo malicious, that they purſue him alſo into Berœa. 14 From whence being conducted into Greece, he preacheth at Athens both to the Iewes and Gentiles, diſputing vvith the Philoſophers, 19 and in Areopagus, perſuading them from their Idols vnto one God and IESVS CHRIST raiſed from the dead.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* vvhen they had vvalked through Amphipolis and Apollonia, they came to Theſſalonica, vvhere there vvas a ſynagogue of the Iewes.
\v 2 And Paul according to his cuſtome entred in vnto them, & three Sabboths he diſcourſed to them out of the Scriptures,
\v 3 declaring and inſinuating that it behoued \sc CHRIST\sc* to ſuffer and to riſe againe from the dead: and that this is \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc*, vvhom I preach to you.
\v 4 And certaine of them beleeued, and vvere ioyned to Paul and Silas, and of the Gentiles that ſerued God a great multitude, and noble vvomen not a fevv.
\v 5 But the Ievves \f 1 \fr 17.5 \ft \+it Zelantes\+it*. This is the zeale of Heretikes, and a liuely paterne of their dealing at this day againſt Catholike Prieſts and preachers and the good \+it Iaſons\+it* that receiue them.\f* enuying, & taking vnto them of the raſcal ſort certaine naughtie men, and making a tumult, ſtirred the citie: and beſetting Iaſons houſe, ſought to bring them forth vnto the people.
\v 6 And not finding them, they drevv Iaſon and certaine brethren to the princes of the citie, crying, That theſe are they that ſtirre vp the vvorld, and are come hither,
\v 7 vvhom Iaſon hath receiued, and al theſe doe againſt the decrees of Cæſar, ſaying that there is an other king, \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 8 And they moued the people, and the princes of the citie hearing theſe things.
\v 9 And taking a ſatisfaction of Iaſon and of the reſt, they dimiſſed them.
\v 10 But the brethren forthwith by night ſent away Paul and Silas vnto Berœa. Vvho vvhen they vvere come, entred into the ſynagogue of the Iewes.
\v 11 (And theſe were more noble then they that are at Theſſalonica, vvho receiued the vvord vvith al greedines, daily ſearching the ſcriptures, if theſe things vvere ſo.
\v 12 And many ſurely of them beleeued, and of honeſt women Gentiles, and men not a few.)
\v 13 And vvhen the Iewes in Theſſalonica vnderſtood, that at Berœa alſo the vvord of God was preached by Paul, they came thither alſo, mouing and troubling the multitude.
\v 14 And then immediatly the brethren ſent away Paul, to goe vnto the ſea: but Silas and Timothee remained there.
\v 15 And they that conducted Paul, brought him as farre as Athens, and receiuing commaundement of him to Silas and Timothee, that they ſhould come to him very ſpeedily, they departed.
\v 16 And vvhen Paul expected them at Athens, his ſpirit vvas incenſed vvithin him, ſeeing the citie giuen to Idolatrie.
\v 17 He diſputed therfore in the ſynagogue vvith the Ievves, & them that ſerued God, and in the market-place, euery day vvith them that were there.
\v 18 And certaine Philoſophers of the Epicures and the Stoikes diſputed vvith him, and certaine ſaid, Vvhat is it that this b \f 1 \fr 17.18 \ft \+it b σπερμολόγος.\+it*\f* vvord-ſovver vvould ſay? But others, He ſeemeth to be a preacher of nevv c \f 2 \fr 17.18 \ft \+it dæmoniorum.\+it*\f* gods. becauſe he preached to them \sc IESVS\sc* and the reſurrection.
\v 19 And apprehending him, they led him to Areopagus, ſaying, May vve know vvhat this new doctrine is that thou ſpeakeſt of?
\v 20 for thou bringeſt in certaine nevv things to our eares. Vve vvil know therfore vvhat theſe things may meane.
\v 21 ( And al the Athenians, and the ſtrangers ſeiourning there, emploied them ſelues to nothing els but either to ſpeake, or to heare ſome newes.)
\v 22 \f 1 \fr 17.22 \ft The Epiſtle for S. Dionyſius Areopagita. Octob. 9.\f* But Paul ſtanding in the middes of Areopagus, ſaid: Ye men of Athens, in al things I perceiue you as it vvere ſuperſtitious.
\v 23 For paſſing by and ſeeing your c \f 1 \fr 17.23 \ft The Aduerſaries (in the nevv Teſt. 1580) tranſlate, \+it your deuotions\+it*, moſt corruptly againſt the nature of the Greeke word \+it (2 Thes. 2, 4\+it*) and moſt wickedly, againſt the laudable deuotion of good Chriſtians, calling the Pagans idolatrie and ſuperſtition, their deuotions.\f*\f 2 \fr 17.23 \ft \+it τὰ σεβάσματα\+it*\f* Idols, I found an altar alſo vvherevpon vvas vvritten, To the vnknowen God. That therfore vvhich you vvorſhippe, not knovving it, the ſame do I preach to you.
\v 24 The God that made the vvorld and al things that are in it, he being Lord of heauen & earth, dvvelleth \x - \xt Act. 7, 48.\x* not in \f 2 \fr 17.24 \ft God iſ not concluded in Temples, nor needeth them for his neceſſitie of dvvelling, or other vſes of indigence. See Annot. c. 7. Act. v. 48. · Act. 7, 48.\f* temples made vvith hand,
\v 25 neither is he ſerued vvith mens hands, needing any thing, vvhereas him ſelf giueth life vnto al, and breathing, and al things:
\v 26 and he made of one al mankinde, to inhabite vpon the vvhole face of the earth, aſſigning ſet times, and the limits of their habitation,
\v 27 for to ſeeke God, if happily they may feele or finde him, although he be not farre from euery one of vs.
\v 28 For in him vve liue and moue and be, as certaine alſo of your ovvne poëtes ſaid, \f 1 \fr 17.28 \ft \+it Aratus.\+it*\f* \it For of his kinde alſo vve are\it*.
\v 29 Being therfore of Gods kinde, vve may not ſuppoſe, the Diuinitie to be like vnto gold or ſiluer, or ſtone, the grauing of art and deuiſe of man.
\v 30 And the times truely of this ignorance vvhereas God diſpiſed, novv he denounceth vnto men that al euery vvhere doe penance,
\v 31 for that he hath appointed a day wherein he vvil iudge the world in equitie, by a man whom he hath appointed, giuing al men faith, rayſing him vp from the dead.
\v 32 And vvhen they had heard the reſurrection of the dead, certaine in deede mocked, but certaine ſaid, Vve vvil heare thee againe concerning this point.
\v 33 So Paul vvent forth out of the middes of them.
\v 34 But certaine men ioyning vnto him, did beleeue: among vvhom vvas alſo \f 1 \fr 17.34 \ft Dyonyſius Areopagita.\f* Dionyſius Areopagîta, and a vvoman named Dámaris, and others vvith them. ⊢
\c 18
\cd At Corinth in Achaia, he worketh vvith his owne hands, preaching IESVS to be CHRIST, vnto the Iewes vpon their Sabboths. 6 But they being obſtinate and blaſpheming, he in plaine termes forſaketh them, and turneth to the Gentils, among whom according to a viſion that he had to embolden him, he planteth the Church in great numbers, 12 the obſtinat Iewes in vaine ſoliciting the Proconſul againſt him. 18 From thence at length departing he returneth 19 by Epheſus (where he promiſeth the Iewes to returne to them) 22 and ſo to Antioch in Syria (from whence he began his iourney Act. 15) 23 but not reſting, by and by he goeth againe to viſite the new Churches that he planted Act. 16 in Galatia and Phrygia: 24 Apollo in his abſence mightily confounding the Iewes at Epheſus, 27 and afterward at Corinth.
\p
\v 1 \sc AFTER\sc* theſe things, departing from Athens, he came to Corinth.
\v 2 and finding a certaine Iew, named Aquila, borne in Pontus, vvho of late vvas come out of Italie, and Priſcilla his vvife (becauſe Claudius had commaunded al Iewes to depart from Rome,) he came to them.
\v 3 And becauſe he vvas of the ſame craft, he remained with them, and wrought, (and they vvere tentmakers by their craft.)
\v 4 And he diſputed in the ſynagogue euery Sabboth, interpoſing the name of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*, and he exhorted the Iewes and the Greekes.
\v 5 And vvhen Silas and Timothee vvere come from Macedonia, Paul vvas inſtant in preaching, teſtifying to the Iewes that \sc IESVS\sc* is \sc CHRIST\sc*.
\v 6 But they contradicting and blaſpheming, he ſhaking his garments, ſaid to them, Your bloud vpon your ovvne head: I being cleane, from hence forth vvil goe to the Gentiles.
\v 7 And departing thence, he entred into the houſe of a certaine man, named Titus Iuſtus, one that ſerued God, vvhoſe houſe vvas adioyning to the ſynagogue.
\v 8 And Criſpus the prince of the Synagogue beleeued our Lord, vvith al his houſe: and many of the Corinthians hearing beleeued, and vvere baptized.
\v 9 And our Lord ſaid in the night by a viſion to Paul, Doe not feare, but ſpeake, and hold not thy peace,
\v 10 for-becauſe I am vvith thee: and no man ſhal ſet vpon thee to hurt thee: for I haue much people in this citie.
\v 11 And he ſate there a yere & ſixe moneths, teaching among them the vvord of God.
\v 12 But Gallio being Proconſul of Achaia, the Iewes vvith one accord roſe vp againſt Paul, and brought him to the iudgement ſeate,
\v 13 ſaying, That this man contrarie to the Law perſuadeth men to worſhippe God.
\v 14 And Paul beginning to open his mouth, Gallio ſaid to the Iewes, If it vvere ſome vniuſt thing, or an heinous facte, O you men Iewes, I ſhould by reaſon beare you.
\v 15 But if they be queſtions of vvord & names, and of your law, your ſelues looke vnto it: I vvil not be iudge of theſe things.
\v 16 And he droue them from the iudgement ſeate.
\v 17 And al apprehending Sóſthenes the prince of the ſynagogue, ſtrooke him before the iudgement ſeate: and Gallio cared for none of thoſe things.
\v 18 But Paul vvhen he had ſtaied yet many daies, taking his leaue of the brethren, ſailed to Syria, (and vvith him Priſcilla and Aquila,) vvho had ſhorne his head in Cenchris. for he had \x - \xt Nu. 6, 18. Act. 21, 24.\x* a vow.
\v 19 And he came vnto Epheſus, and them he left there. But him ſelf entring into the ſynagogue, diſputed vvith the Iewes.
\v 20 And vvhen they deſired him, that he would tarie a longer time, he conſented not,
\v 21 but taking his leaue, and ſaying, I vvil returne to you againe God vvilling, he departed from Epheſus.
\v 22 And going dovvne to Cæſaréa, he vvent vp, and ſaluted the Church, and came downe to Antioche.
\v 23 And hauing taried there a certaine time, he departed, vvalking in order through the countrie of Galatia and Phrygia, confirming al the diſciples.
\v 24 And a certaine Iew, named \f 1 \fr 18.24 \ft Apollo.\f* Apollo, borne at Alexandria, an eloquent man, came to Epheſus, mighty in the ſcriptures.
\v 25 This man was taught the vvay of our Lord: and being feruent in ſpirit he ſpake, and taught diligently thoſe things that pertaine to \sc IESVS\sc*, knovving only the baptiſme of Iohn.
\v 26 This man therfore began to deale confidently in the ſynagogue. Vvhom vvhen Priſcilla and Aquila had heard, they tooke him vnto them, and expounded to him the vvay of our Lord more diligently.
\v 27 And vvhereas he vvas deſirous to goe to Achaia, the brethren exhorting vvrote to the diſciples to receiue him. Vvho, vvhen he vvas come, profited them much that had beleeued.
\v 28 For he vvith vehemencie conuinced the Iewes openly, ſhewing by the ſcriptures, that \sc IESVS\sc* is \sc CHRIST\sc*.
\c 19
\cd How Paul began the Church of Epheſus, firſt in 12 that vvere baptized with Iohns baptiſme, 8 then preaching three moneths in the Synagogue of the Iewes, vntil for their obſtinacie and blaſpheming, he forſooke them, diſputing afterward in a certaine ſchoole for two yeres ſpace to the maruelous increaſe of the Church, ſpecially through his great miracles alſo, in healing diſeaſes with the touche of his clothes, and expelling diuels, 13 vvho yet contemned the Exorciſts of the Iewes. 18 How the Chriſtians there confeſſe their actes, and burne their vnlawful bookes: 21 and how he foretold that after he had been at Hieruſalem, he muſt ſee Rome. 23 and what a great ſedition vvas raiſed againſt him at Epheſus, by them that got their liuing of vvorking to the idolatrous Temple of Diana.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* \f 1 \fr 19.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpon Whitſon-eue.\f* it came to paſſe vvhen Apollo vvas at Corinth, that Paul hauing gone through the higher partes came to Epheſus, and found certaine diſciples:
\v 2 and he ſaid to them, Haue you receiued the holy Ghoſt, beleeuing? But they ſaid to him, Nay, neither haue vve heard Whether there be a holy Ghoſt.
\v 3 But he ſaid, In vvhat then vvere you baptized? Vvho ſaid, c \f 1 \fr 19.3 \ft c Iohns Baptiſm not ſufficient.\f* In Iohns baptiſme.
\v 4 And Paul ſaid: \x - \xt Mt. 3, 11. Mr. 1, 8. Lu. 3, 16.\x* Iohn baptized the people vvith the baptiſme of penance, ſaying: That they ſhould beleeue in him that vvas to come after him, that is to ſay, c \f 2 \fr 19.4 \ft c Chriſt's Baptiſm neceſſarie.\f* in \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 5 Hearing theſe things, they vvere baptized in the name of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 6 And vvhen Paul had \f 1 \fr 19.6 \ft S. Paul miniſtred the Sacrament of Confirmation. \+it See annot. c. 8, 17\+it*.\f* impoſed hands on them, the holy Ghoſt came vpon them, and they ſpake vvith tongues, and prophecied.
\v 7 And all the men vvere about tvvelue.
\v 8 And entring in to the ſynagogue, he ſpake confidently for three moneths, diſputing and exhorting of the kingdom of God. ⊢
\v 9 But vvhen certaine vvere indûrate, and beleeued not, il-ſpeaking the vvay of our Lord before the multitude, departing from them, he ſeparated the diſciples, daily diſputing in the ſchole of one Tyrannus.
\v 10 An this vvas done for the ſpace of tvvo yeres, ſo that al vvhich dvvelt in Aſia, heard the vvord of our Lord, Iewes and Gentils.
\v 11 And God vvrought by the hand of Paul miracles not common:
\v 12 ſo that there were alſo brought from his body napkins or handkerchefs vpon the ſicke, and the diſeaſes departed from them, and the vvicked ſpirits vvent out.
\v 13 And certaine alſo of the Iudaical exorciſts that vvent about, aſſaied to inuocate vpon them that had euil ſpirits, the name of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*, ſaying, I adiure you by \sc IESVS\sc* vvhom Paul preacheth.
\v 14 And there vvere certaine ſonnes of Sceua a Iewe, cheefe prieſt, ſeuen, that did this.
\v 15 But the vvicked ſpirit anſwering, ſaid to them, \sc IESVS\sc* I know, and Paul I knovv: but you, what are ye?
\v 16 And the man in whom the wicked ſpirit vvas, leaping vpon them, and maſtring \f 1 \fr 19.16 \fq `both \ft \+it them\+it*\f* `both', preuailed againſt them, ſo that they fled out of that houſe naked and vvounded.
\v 17 And this vvas made notorious to al the Iewes and the Gentiles that dwelt at Epheſus: and feare fel vpon al them, and the name of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* vvas magnified.
\v 18 And many of them that beleeued, came confeſſing and declaring \f 1 \fr 19.18 \ft They made not only a general confeſſiō wherein al mē ſhew thē ſelues alike to be ſinners, as our Proteſtants do: but euery one confeſſed his owne proper deedes and faultes.\f* their deedes.
\v 19 And many of them that had folovved curious things, brought together their bookes, and burnt them before al: and counting the prices of them, they found the money to be fiftie thouſand pence.
\v 20 \f 1 \fr 19.20 \ft The 6. part.\f* So mightely increaſed the vvord of God and vvas confirmed.
\v 21 And vvhen theſe things vvere ended, Paul purpoſed in the Spirit, vvhen he had paſſed through Macedonia and Achaia, to goe to Hieruſalem, ſaying, After I ſhall haue been there, I muſt ſee \f 1 \fr 19.21 \ft The 6 part. Of taking away the Goſpel from Hieruſalem the head citie of the Iewes, and giuing it to Rome the head citie of the Gentils.\f* Rome alſo.
\v 22 And ſending into Macedonia tvvo of them that miniſtred vnto him, Timothee and Eraſtus, him ſelf remained for a time in Aſia.
\v 23 And at that time there vvas made no litle trouble about the way of our Lord.
\v 24 For one named Demetrius, a ſiluerſmith, that made ſiluer c \f 1 \fr 19.24 \ft The Proteſtants tranſlate, \+it ſhrines\+it*, in the bible an. 1577: to make the people thinke that it toucheth the holy \+it ſhrines\+it* of Sainctes: moſt corruptly, the greeke ſignifying plainly, \+it temples\+it*, and that of heathē gods.\f*\f 2 \fr 19.24 \ft \+it c ναοὺς.\+it*\f* temples of Diána, procured to the artificers no ſmal gaine:
\v 25 whom calling together and them that were the ſame kinde of workemen, he ſaid, Sirs, you know that our gaine is of this occupation:
\v 26 and you ſee, and heare that this ſame Paul by perſuaſion hath auerted a great multitude not only of Epheſus, but almoſt of al Aſia, ſaying, That they are not gods vvhich be made by handes.
\v 27 And not only vnto vs is this part in danger to be reproued, but alſo the temple of great Diana ſhal be reputed for nothing, yea & her maieſtie ſhal begin to be deſtroied, vvhom al Aſia & the vvorld worſhippeth.
\v 28 Hearing theſe things, they were repleniſhed with anger, and cried out ſaying, Great is Diana of the Epheſians.
\v 29 And the vvhole citie vvas filled vvith confuſion, & they ranne violently vvith one accord into the theátre, catching Gaius and Ariſtarchus Macedonians, Paules companions.
\v 30 And vvhen Paul vvould haue entred in to the people, the diſciples did not permit him.
\v 31 And certaine alſo of the Princes of Aſia that vvere his frendes, ſent vnto him, deſyring that he vvould not aduenture him ſelf into the theátre:
\v 32 and others cried an other thing. For the aſſemblie vvas confuſe, & the more part knevv not for what cauſe they were aſſembled.
\v 33 And of the multitude they drevv forth Alexander, the Ievves thruſting him forvvard. But Alexander vvith his hand deſyring ſilence, vvould haue giuen the people ſatisfaction.
\v 34 Vvhom as ſoone as they perceiued to be a Iewe, there vvas made one voice of al, almoſt for the ſpace of tvvo houres crying out, Great is Diana of the Epheſians.
\v 35 And when the Scribe had appeaſed the multitudes, he ſaith, Ye men of Epheſus, for vvhat man is there that knovveth not the citie of the Epheſians to be a vvorſhipper of great Diana, & \f 1 \fr 19.35 \ft Here the Heretikes adde to the text this word, \+it image\+it*, more then is in the greeke, to put a ſcruple into the peoples minde concerning holy \+it Images\+it*.\f*\f 2 \fr 19.35 \ft \+it c τοῦ διοπετοῦς.\+it*\f* c Iupiters childe?
\v 36 Foraſmuch therfore as theſe things can not be gainſaid, you muſt be quieted, and doe nothing raſhly.
\v 37 For you haue brought theſe men, being neither ſacrilegious, nor blaſpheming your Goddeſſe.
\v 38 But if Demetrius and the artificers that are vvith him, haue matter to ſay againſt any man, there are Courtes kept in the common place, & there are Proconſuls, let them accuſe one an other.
\v 39 And if you aſke any other matter: it may be reſolued in a lavvful aſſemblie.
\v 40 For we are in danger alſo to be accuſed for this daies ſedition: vvhereas there is no man guilty by vvhom vve may giue an account of this concourſe. And vvhen he had ſaid theſe things, he dimiſſed the aſſemblie.
\c 20
\cd Hauing viſited the Churches of Macedonia and Achaia (as he purpoſed Act. 19) and now about to ſaile from Corinth tovvard Hieruſalem, becauſe of the Iewes lying in vvaite for him, he is conſtrained to returne into Macedonia. 6 And ſo at Philippos taking boate, commeth to Troas, where vpon the Sunday, vvith a ſermon, and a miracle, he greatly confirmeth that Church. 13 Thence comming to Milétum, 17 he ſendeth to Epheſus for the Clergie of thoſe partes: to whom he maketh a Paſtoral ſermon, committing vnto their charge the flocke begunne by him there, and novv like to be ſeen of him no more, conſidering the troubles that by reuelation he looketh for at Hieruſalem.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* after that the tumult was ceaſed, Paul calling the diſciples, and exhorting them, tooke his leaue, and ſet forvvard to goe into Macedonia.
\v 2 And vvhen he had vvalked through thoſe partes, & had exhorted them vvith much ſpeach, he came to Greece:
\v 3 vvhere when he had ſpent three moneths, the Iewes laid wait for him as he was about to ſaile into Syria: and he had councel to returne through Macedonia.
\v 4 And there accompanied him Soſipater of Pyrrhus, of Berœa: and of Theſſalonians, Ariſtarchus, and Secundus: and Caius of Derbè, and Timothee: and of Aſia, Tychicus and Tróphimus.
\v 5 Theſe going before, ſtaied for vs at Troas:
\v 6 but vve ſailed after the daies of Azymes from Philippi, and came to them vnto Troas in fiue daies, vvhere vve abode ſeuen daies.
\v 7 And in the firſt of the Sabboth vvhen we vvere aſſembled to \f 1 \fr 20.7 \ft S. Paul did here breake bread on the Sunday as it is broken in the Sacramēt of the body of Chriſt, and had both before & after the celebrating of the Sacramēt a ſermon to the people. \+it Aug. ep. 86 ad Caſulanū. Vener. Beda in 20 Act\+it*.\f* breake bread, Paul diſputed with them, being to depart on the morow, and he continued the ſermon vntil midnight.
\v 8 And there vvere a great number of lampes in the vpper chamber vvhere vve vvere aſſembled.
\v 9 And a certaine yong man named Eútychus, ſitting vpon the window, vvhereas he vvas oppreſſed vvith heauy ſleepe ( Paul diſputing long) driuen by ſleepe, fel from the third loft downe, and was taken vp dead.
\v 10 To whom when Paul was gone dovvne, he lay vpon him: and embracing him he ſaid, Be not troubled, for his ſoule is in him.
\v 11 And going vp and breaking bread and taſting, and hauing talked ſufficiently to them vntil day light, ſo he departed.
\v 12 And they brought the lad aliue, and were not a litle comforted.
\v 13 But we going vp into the ſhip, ſailed to Aſſon, from thence meaning to receiue Paul. for ſo he had ordained, him ſelf purpoſing to iourney by land.
\v 14 And vvhen he had found vs in Aſſon, taking him with vs vve came to Mityléne.
\v 15 And ſailing thence, the day folovving vve came ouer againſt Chios: and the other day we arriued at Samos: and the day folovving vve came to Milétum.
\v 16 for Paul had purpoſed to ſaile leauing Epheſus, leſt any ſtay ſhould be made him in Aſia. For he haſtened, if it vvere poſſible for him, to keepe the day of Pentecoſt at Hieruſalem.
\v 17 \f 1 \fr 20.17 \ft The Epiſtle for S. Steven Pope. Aug. 2.\f* And ſending from Milétum to Epheſus, he called the \f 2 \fr 20.17 \ft That is, \+it Prieſts\+it* as \+it Act. 15, 4\+it*. See the marginal annot. there.\f*\f 3 \fr 20.17 \ft \+it c πρεσβυτέρους\+it*\f* c Auncients of the Church.
\v 18 Who being come to him, and aſſembled together, he ſaid to them, You knovv \x - \xt Act. 19, 1.\x* from the firſt day that I entred into Aſia, in vvhat maner I haue been vvith you al the time,
\v 19 ſeruing our Lord vvith al humilitie and teares, and tentations that did chaunce to me by the conſpiracies of the Iewes:
\v 20 Hovv I haue vvithdravven nothing that vvas profitable, but that I preached it to you, & taught you openly and from houſe to houſe,
\v 21 teſtifying to Iewes and Gentils \f 1 \fr 20.21 \ft Apoſtolike preaching commendeth not faith only but penance alſo to the people.\f* penance tovvard God and faith in our Lord \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc*.
\v 22 And now behold, being bound by the ſpirit, I goe to Hieruſalem: not knovving vvhat things ſhal befall me in it,
\v 23 but that the Holy Ghoſt through out al cities doth proteſt to me, ſaying: that bands and tribulations abide me at Hieruſalem.
\v 24 But I feare none of theſe things, neither doe I make my life more pretious then my ſelf, ſo that I may cōſúmat my courſe & miniſterie vvhich I receiued of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*, to teſtifie the Goſpel of the grace of God.
\v 25 And novv behold I doe knovv, that you ſhal no more ſee my face al you, through whom I haue paſſed preaching the kingdom of God.
\v 26 Wherefore I take you to witneſſe this preſent day that I am cleere from the bloud of al.
\v 27 For I haue not ſpared to declare vnto you al the counſel of God.
\v 28 Take heede to your ſelues and to the vvhole flocke vvherein the \f 1 \fr 20.28 \ft Biſhops or Prieſts (for then theſe names vvere ſometime vſed indifferently) gouernours of the Church of God, & placed in that roome & high function by the Holy Ghoſt.\f* Holy Ghoſt hath placed you biſhops, to rule the Church of God vvhich he hath purchaſed with his ovvne bloud.
\v 29 I knovv that after my departure there vvil rauening wolues enter in among you, not ſparing the flocke.
\v 30 And out of your ovvne ſelues ſhal ariſe men ſpeaking peruerſe things, to draw avvay diſciples after them ſelues.
\v 31 For the vvhich cauſe be vigilant, keeping in memorie that for three yeres night and day I ceaſed not with teares to admoniſh euery one of you.
\v 32 And now I commend you to God and to the vvord of his grace, vvho is able to edifie, and to giue inheritance in al the ſanctified.
\v 33 No mans ſiluer and gold or garment haue I coueted.
\v 34 Your ſelues knovv that for ſuch things as vvere needful for me and them that are vvith me, theſe hands haue miniſtred.
\v 35 I haue ſhevved you al things, that ſo labouring, you muſt receiue the vveake, and remember the vvord of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*, becauſe he ſaid, It is a more bleſſed thing to giue rather then to take.
\v 36 And vvhen he had ſaid theſe things, falling on his knees he praied vvith al them.
\v 37 And there vvas great vveeping made of al, and falling vpon the necke of Paul, they kiſſed him,
\v 38 being ſorie moſt of al for the vvord which he had ſaid, that they ſhould ſee his face no more. And they brought him going vnto the ſhippe.
\c 21
\cd From Milétum going on his iourney, 4 he can not be diſſuaded neither at Tyre, 8 nor at Cæſarea (in both which places the Holy Ghoſt reueled how he ſhould be handled in Hieruſalem, 10 the Prophet Agabus expreſly foretelling that the Iewes there ſhould deliuer him to the Gentils) 15 but to Hieruſalem he commeth: vvhere being welcome to the Chriſtians, and namely to Iames the Biſhop, and to the Prieſts, while he goeth about to ſatisfie the Chriſtian Iewes there, who had been miſinformed of him as if he had taught it to be vnlawful for the Iewes to keepe Moyſes Lavv: 27 he is inuaded by the infidel Iewes, and ready to be murdered by them, vntil the Romane ſouldiars do reſcue him.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* when it came to paſſe that vve ſailed, being caried from them, with a ſtraight courſe we came to Coos, and the day folowing to Rhodes, and from thence to Patara.
\v 2 And when vve had found a ſhip that paſſed ouer to Phœnice, going vp into it we ſailed.
\v 3 And when we were in the ſight of Cypres, leauing it on the left hand, vve ſailed into Syria, and came to Tyre: for there the ſhip vvas to diſcharge her lode.
\v 4 And finding diſciples, vve taried there ſeuen daies: vvho ſaid to Paul by the Spirit, that he ſhould not goe vp to Hieruſalem.
\v 5 And the daies being expired, departing vve went forvvard, al bringing vs on the vvay, vvith their vviues and children, til vve vvere out of the citie: and falling vpon our knees on the ſhore, vve praied.
\v 6 And vvhen vve had bid one an other farevvel, vve vvent vp into the ſhip: and they returned vnto their owne.
\v 7 But we hauing ended the nauigation, from Tyre came dovvne to Ptolomáis: and ſaluting the brethren, vve taried one day with them.
\v 8 And the next day departing, vve came to Cæſaréa. And entring into the houſe of \x - \xt Act. 6, 5.\x* Philip the Euangeliſt, vvho vvas one of the ſeuen, vve taried with him.
\v 9 And he had \f 1 \fr 21.9 \ft As S. Peter had a wife, but vſed her not after his calling, as it is noted els where out of S. Hierom \+it Luc. 4, 38\+it*: ſo may it be ſaid of S. Philip being Deacon.\f* foure daughters virgins, that did prophecie.
\v 10 And as we abode there for certaine daies, there came a certaine prophet from Ievvrie, named Agabus.
\v 11 He, when he was come to vs, tooke Paules girdle: and binding his owne handes & feete, he ſaid, Thus ſaith the holy Ghoſt: The man whoſe girdle this is, ſo ſhal the Iewes binde in Hieruſalem, & ſhal deliuer him into the handes of the Gentiles.
\v 12 Which when vve had heard, vve & they that vvere of the ſame place, deſired him that he would not goe vp to Hieruſalem.
\v 13 Then Paul anſvvered, and ſaid, What doe you, vveeping and afflicting my hart? for I am ready not only to be bound, but to die alſo in Hieruſalem for the name of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 14 And vvhen vve could not perſuade him, we ceaſed, ſaying, The vvil of our Lord be done.
\v 15 And after theſe daies, being prepared, vve vvent vp to Hieruſalem.
\v 16 And there came alſo of the diſciples from Cæſaréa vvith vs, bringing with them one Iaſon a Cyprian (vvith vvhom vve ſhould lodge) an old diſciple.
\v 17 And when vve vvere come to Hieruſalem, the brethren receiued vs gladly.
\v 18 And the day folovving Paul vvent in vvith vs to Iames, and al the Auncients vvere aſſembled.
\v 19 Vvhom vvhen he had ſaluted, he told particularly vvhat God had done among the Gentiles by his miniſterie.
\v 20 But they hearing it, magnified God, and ſaid to him: Thou ſeeſt (brother) how many thouſands there are among the Ievves that haue beleeued: and al are zelátours of the Lavv.
\v 21 But they haue heard of thee that thou doeſt teach thoſe Iewes that are among the Gentiles, to depart from Moyſes: ſaying that they ought not to circumciſe their children, nor vvalke according to the cuſtome.
\v 22 What is it then? needes muſt the multitude aſſemble: for they vvil heare that thou art come.
\v 23 Doe this therfore vvhich vve tel thee, There are vvith vs foure men, that haue a vow on them.
\v 24 Taking theſe vnto thee, ſanctifie thy ſelf vvith them: and beſtovv on them, that they may \x - \xt Nu. 6, 18.\x* ſhaue their heads: and al ſhal knovv that the things vvhich they heard of thee, are falſe: but that thy ſelf alſo walkeſt keeping the Law.
\v 25 But concerning them that beleeue of the Gentils, \x - \xt Act. 15, 20.\x* vve haue written, decreeing that they ſhould refraine them ſelues from the immolated to Idols, and bloud, and ſuffocated, and fornication.
\v 26 Then Paul taking the men vnto him, the next day being purified vvith them entred into the temple, ſhevving the accompliſhment of the \x - \xt Nu. 6, 13.\x* daies of the purification, vntil an oblation vvas offered for euery one of them.
\v 27 But vvhiles the ſeuen daies were a finiſhing, thoſe Ievves that vvere of Aſia, vvhen they had ſeen him in the temple, ſtirred vp al the people, and laid handes vpon him,
\v 28 crying, Ye men of Iſraël, help: this is the man that againſt the people & the Law and this place teaching al men euery where, hath alſo moreouer brought in Gentiles into the temple, and hath violated this holy place.
\v 29 (For they had ſeen Tróphimus the Epheſian in the citie vvith him, vvhom they ſuppoſed that Paul had brought into the temple.)
\v 30 And the vvhole citie vvas in an vproare: and there vvas made a concourſe of the people. And apprehending Paul, they drevve him forth of the temple: and immediatly the doores vvere ſhut.
\v 31 And as they ſought to kil him, it vvas told the Tribune of the band, That al Hieruſalem is in a confuſion.
\v 32 Vvho forthvvith taking vnto him ſouldiars & Centurions, ranne downe to them. Who, when they had ſeen the Tribune and the ſouldiars, ceaſed to ſtrike Paul.
\v 33 Then the Tribune comming neere apprehended him, and commaunded him to be bound vvith tvvo chaines: and he demaunded vvho he vvas, and what he had done.
\v 34 And ſome cried one thing, ſome an other, in the multitude. And vvhereas he could not knovv the certaintie for the tumult, he commaunded him to be led c \f 1 \fr 21.34 \ft In caſtra. \+it So in the places folovving\+it*.\f* into the caſtel.
\v 35 And vvhen he vvas come to the ſtaires, it chaunced that he vvas caried of the ſouldiars becauſe of the violence of the people.
\v 36 For the multitude of the people folovved, crying, Avvay vvith him.
\v 37 And vvhen Paul began to be brought into the caſtel, he ſaith to the Tribune, Is it lavvful for me to ſpeake ſome thing to thee? Vvho ſaid, Canſt thou ſpeake Greeke?
\v 38 Art not thou the Ægyptian that before theſe daies did raiſe a tumult, and didſt lead forth into the deſert foure thouſand men that vvere murderers?
\v 39 And Paul ſaid to him, \x - \xt Act. 22, 3.\x* I am a man truely a Iewe of Tarſus, a citizen not of an obſcure citie of Cilicia. And I deſire thee, permit me to ſpeake to the people.
\v 40 And vvhen he had permitted him, Paul ſtanding on the ſtaiers, beckened with his hand to the people. and great ſilence being made, he ſpake vnto them in the Hebrevv tongue, ſaying.
\c 22
\cd Being licenſed by the Tribune to ſpeake to the people, he ſheweth them that he vvas once as earneſt on that ſide as they now be: 6 and how ſtrange and miraculous his conuerſion vvas. 17 They heare him quietly, vntil he began to make mention of a viſion that ſent him avvay from them to the Gentils. 22 Then they crie out vpon him ſo, 23 that for their crying the Tribune commaundeth him to be ſcourged. 25 Which yet by his wiſedom he eſcapeth.
\p
\v 1 \sc MEN\sc* brethren and fathers, heare vvhat account I doe render novv vnto you.
\v 2 (And vvhen they had heard that he ſpake to them in the Hebrevv tongue, they did the more keepe ſilence.
\v 3 And he ſaith,) \x - \xt Act. 21, 39.\x* I am a man a Iewe, borne at Tarſus in Cilicia, but brought vp in this citie, at the feete of Gamaliel inſtructed according to the veritie of the law of the fathers, an emulátour of the Law as alſo al you are this day:
\v 4 who \x - \xt Act. 8, 3.\x* perſecuted this vvay vnto death, binding & deliuering into cuſtodies men & women,
\v 5 as the high Prieſt doth giue me teſtimonie, and al the auncients. of vvhom \x - \xt Act. 9, 2.\x* receiuing letters alſo to the brethren, I vvent to Damaſcus, that I might bring them thence bound to Hieruſalem, to be puniſhed.
\v 6 And it came to paſſe as I vvas going, and drawing nigh to Damaſcus at midday, ſodenly from heauen there ſhone round about me much light:
\v 7 and falling on the ground, I heard a voice ſaying to me, Saul, Saul, vvhy perſecuteſt thou me?
\v 8 And I anſwered, Vvho art thou Lord? And he ſaid to me, I am \sc IESVS\sc* of Nazareth, vvhom thou perſecuteſt.
\v 9 And they that vvere vvith me, ſavv the light in deede, but the voice they heard not of him that ſpake with me.
\v 10 And I ſaid, Vvhat ſhal I doe Lord? And our Lord ſaid to me, Ariſe and goe to Damaſcus: and there it ſhal be told thee of al things that thou muſt doe.
\v 11 And vvhereas I did not ſee for the brightneſſe of that light, being led of my companions by the hand, I came to Damaſcus.
\v 12 And one Ananias, a man according to the Law hauing teſtimonie of al the Ievves inhabitants,
\v 13 comming to me, and ſtanding by me, ſaid to me, Brother Saul, looke vp. And I the ſelf ſame houre looked vp on him.
\v 14 But he ſaid, The God of our fathers hath prëordained thee, that thou ſhouldeſt knovv his wil, and ſee the iuſt one, and heare a voice from his mouth:
\v 15 becauſe thou ſhalt be his witnes to al men, of thoſe things vvhich thou haſt ſeen and heard.
\v 16 And now what tarieſt thou? Riſe vp, and be baptized, & \f 1 \fr 22.16 \ft The Sacrament of Baptiſme doth it ſelf vvaſh away ſinnes as here is plaine, & therfore doth not only ſignifie (as the Heretikes affirme) that our ſinnes be forgiuen before, or othervviſe by faith only remitted. Vvhereby the Churches doctrine is proued to be fully agreable to the Scriptures, that the Sacraments giue grace \+it ex opere operato\+it*, that is, by the force & vertue of the vvorke and vvord, done & ſaid in the Sacrament.\f* vvaſh avvay thy ſinnes inuocating his name.
\v 17 And it befel me returning into Hieruſalem, and praying in the temple, that I vvas in a traunce,
\v 18 and ſavv him ſaying vnto me, Make haſt, and depart quickely out of Hieruſalem: becauſe they vvil not receiue thy teſtimonie of me.
\v 19 And I ſaid, Lord, they know that I did caſt into priſon and beate in euery ſynagogue them that beleeued in thee.
\v 20 And vvhen the bloud of Steuen thy \f 1 \fr 22.20 \ft \+it c μάρτυρος. Act. 7, 38.\+it*\f* c vvitnes vvas ſhed, \f 2 \fr 22.20 \ft Act. 7, 38. · μάρτυρος. · Not only the Principals but al that conſent to the death or vexation of Chriſtian men for the Catholike faith, do highly offend. vvhich the Apoſtle confeſſeth here, that Gods mercie may be more notoriouſly glorified in him hereby.\f* I ſtoode by and conſented, and kept the garments of them that killed him.
\v 21 And he ſaid to me, Goe, for into the Gentiles a farre vvil I ſend thee.
\v 22 And they heard him vntil this vvord, and they lifted vp their voice, ſaying, Avvay vvith ſuch an one from the earth: for it is not meete he ſhould liue.
\v 23 And when they cried out, and threvv off their garments, and caſt duſt into the aire,
\v 24 the Tribune commaunded him to be caried into the caſtel, and to be beaten with vvhippes, and that he ſhould be tormented: to know for vvhat cauſe they did ſo crie at him.
\v 25 And vvhen they had bound him very ſtraight vvith thongs, Paul ſaith to the Centurion ſtanding by him: Is it lavvful for you to whippe a man that is a Romane and vncondemned?
\v 26 Vvhich the Centurion hearing, vvent to the Tribune, and told him, ſaying, Vvhat wilt thou doe? for this man is a citizen of Rome.
\v 27 And the Tribune comming, ſaid to him, Tel me, art thou a Romane? But he ſaid, Yea.
\v 28 And the Tribune anſwered, I obtained this citie vvith a great ſumme. And Paul ſaid, But I was alſo borne to it.
\v 29 Immediatly therfore they departed from him that vvere to torment him. The Tribune alſo feared after he vnderſtoode that he was a citizē of Rome, and becauſe he had bound him.
\v 30 But the next day meaning to know more diligently for vvhat cauſe he vvas accuſed of the Ievves, he looſed him, and commaunded the c \f 1 \fr 22.30 \ft c ἀρχιερεῖς.\f* Prieſts to come together and al the Councel: & bringing forth Paul, he ſet him among them.
\c 23
\cd As the people in the tumult, ſo alſo the very cheefe of the Ievves in their Councel ſhew them ſelues obſtinate, and wilful perſecutors of the truth in S. Pauls perſon. Whoſe behauiour towardes them is ful of conſtancie, modeſtie, and wiſedom. 11 (Chriſt alſo by a viſion encouraging him, and foretelling that he ſhal to Rome.) 12 Yea they conſpire vvith 40 men to kil him traiterouſly. 16 But the matter being detected, the Romane Tribune conuaigheth him ſtrongly to Cæſaréa.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* Paul looking vpon the Councel, ſaid, Men brethren, I vvith al good conſcience haue conuerſed before God, vntil this preſent day.
\v 2 And the high Prieſt Ananias commaunded them that ſtoode by him, to ſmite him on the mouth.
\v 3 Then Paul ſaid to him, \f 1 \fr 23.3 \ft He ſaid not this through perturbation of minde or of a paſſion, but by way of prophecie, that this figuratiue high prieſthod then trimmed like a whited wall, was to be deſtroied, where as now the true prieſthod of Chriſt was \+it cōe\+it*.\f* God ſhal ſtrike thee, thou whited vvall. And thou ſitting iudgeſt me according to the lavv, and contrarie to lavv doeſt thou commaund me to be ſmitten?
\v 4 And they that ſtoode by, ſaid, Doeſt thou reuile the high Prieſt of God?
\v 5 And Paul ſaid, I knewe not, brethren, that he is the high Prieſt. For it is written: \x - \xt Exo. 22, 28.\x* \it The prince of thy people thou ſhalt not miſſpeake\it*.
\v 6 And Paul knovving that the one part vvas of Sadducees, and the other of Phariſees, \x - \xt Phil. 3, 5.\x* he cried out in the Councel, Men brethren, \f 2 \fr 23.6 \ft Phil. 3, 5. · Such prudent euaſions from danger are lawful. which S. Chryſoſtome calleth (ſpecially in this Apoſtle) the wiſdom of the ſerpent, as otherwiſe in his teaching and preaching & patience he vſed the ſimplicitie of a doue.\f* I am a Phariſee, the ſonne of Phariſees: of the hope and reſurrection of the dead am I iudged.
\v 7 And vvhen he had ſaid theſe things, there roſe diſſenſion betvvene the Phariſees and Sadducees, and the multitude vvas diuided.
\v 8 For the Sadducees ſay \x - \xt Mt. 22, 23.\x* there is no reſurrection, nor Angel, nor ſpirit: but the Phariſees confeſſe both.
\v 9 And there vvas made a great crie. And certaine of the Phariſees riſing vp, ſtroue ſaying, Vve finde no euil in this man. what if a ſpirit hath ſpoken to him, or an Angel?
\v 10 And vvhen there vvas riſen great diſſenſion, the Tribune fearing leſt Paul ſhould be torne in peeces by them, commaunded the ſouldiars to goe dovvne, and to take him out of the middes of them, and to bring him into the caſtel.
\v 11 And the night folovving our Lord ſtanding by him, ſaid, Be conſtant: for as thou haſt teſtified of me in Hieruſalem, ſo \f 1 \fr 23.11 \ft Though God who could not lie, had promiſed Paul that he ſhould goe to Rome: yet the Apoſtle omitted not humane meanes to defend him ſelf from his enemies and otherwiſe. neither ſaid he as the Heretikes called Predeſtinates, Let them do what they wil, they can not hurt me, for I am predeſtinate to goe to Rome. See his doings and ſayings to ſaue him ſelf, in the chap. folowing.\f* muſt thou teſtifie at Rome alſo.
\v 12 And when day vvas come, certaine of the Ievves gathered them ſelues together, & vovved them ſelues, ſaying, that they vvould neither eate nor drinke til they killed Paul.
\v 13 And they vvere more then fourtie men that had made this conſpiracie:
\v 14 vvho came to the cheefe prieſts and the auncients, and ſaid, By execration vve haue vovved our ſelues, that vve vvil eate nothing, til vve kill Paul.
\v 15 Novv therfore giue you knowledge to the Tribune vvith the Councel, that he bring him forth to you, as if you meant to knovv ſome more certaintie touching him. But we, before he come neere, are ready for to kil him.
\v 16 Vvhich when Paules ſiſters ſonne had heard, of their lying in vvaite, he came and entred into the caſtel and told Paul.
\v 17 And Paul calling to him one of the Centurions, ſaid, Bring this yong man to the Tribune, for he hath ſome thing to tel him.
\v 18 \f 1 \fr 23.18 \ft c See the courteſie & equitie of Heathen Officers toward their priſoners, to ſaue them from al iniurie and villanie.\f* And he taking him, brought him to the Tribune, and ſaid, The priſoner Paul deſired me to bring this yong man vnto thee, hauing ſome thing to ſay to thee.
\v 19 And the Tribune taking him by the hand, vvent aſide vvith him apart, and aſked him, Vvhat is it that thou haſt to tel me?
\v 20 And he ſaid, The Ievves haue agreed to deſire thee, that to morovv thou vvilt bring forth Paul into the Councel, as though they meant to inquire ſome more certaintie touching him.
\v 21 But doe not thou credite them, for there lie in vvaite for him more then fourtie men of them, vvhich haue vowed neither to eate nor to drinke, til they kil him: and they are novv ready, expecting thy promiſſe.
\v 22 The Tribune therfore dimiſſed the yong man, commaunding that he ſhould ſpeake to no man that he had notified theſe things vnto him.
\v 23 And calling tvvo Centurions, he ſaid to them, Make ready tvvo hundred ſouldiars, to goe as farre as Cæſaréa, and ſeuentie horſemen, and launces tvvo hundred, from the third houre of the night:
\v 24 and prepare beaſts. that ſetting Paul on, they might bring him ſafe to Felix the Preſident
\v 25 (For he feared leſt perhaps the Ievves might take him avvay, and kil him, and him ſelf aftervvard ſhould ſuſtaine reproche, as though he vvould haue taken money)
\v 26 vvriting a letter conteining this much. \it Claudius Lyſias to the moſt excellent Preſident Felix, greeting\it*.
\v 27 This man being apprehended of the Ievves, and ready to be killed of them, I comming in vvith the band deliuered him, vnderſtanding that he is a Romane:
\v 28 and meaning to know the cauſe that they obiected vnto him, I brought him downe into their Councel.
\v 29 Vvhom I found to be accuſed concerning queſtions of their lavv: but hauing no crime worthie of death or of bandes.
\v 30 And vvhen it vvas told me of embuſhments that they had prepared againſt him, I ſent him to thee, ſignifying alſo to the accuſers, to ſpeake before thee. Fare vvel.
\v 31 And the ſouldiars according as it vvas commaunded them, taking Paul, brought him by night to Antipatris.
\v 32 And the next day ſending avvay the horſemen to goe vvith him, they returned to the caſtel.
\v 33 Vvho when they vvere come to Cæſaréa, and had deliuered the letter to the Preſident, they did ſet Paul alſo before him.
\v 34 And when he had read, and had aſked of vvhat prouince he vvas: and vnderſtanding that of Cilicia:
\v 35 I vvil heare the, ſaid he, vvhen thy accuſers are come. And he commaunded him to be kept in Herods palace.
\c 24
\cd They proſecute him to Cæſarea, bringing vvith them an oratour, vvho before the Preſident Felix accuſeth him. 10 He anſvvereth, defending him ſelf from the crimes they charged him vvith, but confeſſing his religion plainly. 22 The Iudge perceiuing his religion to be irreprehenſible, yeldeth not to condemne him at their pleaſure, 24 yea he oftentimes vvith his vvife heareth his preaching, 27 but yet doth not his dutie to deliuer him out of priſon.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* after fiue daies the high prieſt Ananias deſcended, vvith certaine auncients and one Tertullus an oratour, vvho vvent to the Preſident againſt Paul.
\v 2 And Paul being cited, Tertullus began to accuſe, ſaying. Vvhereas vve liue in much peace by thee, & many things are corrected by thy prouidence:
\v 3 vve doe alvvaies and in al places receiue it, moſt excellent Felix, vvith al thanks-geuing.
\v 4 But leſt I hinder thee any longer, I deſire thee of thy clemencie breifely to heare vs.
\v 5 Vve haue found this man peſtiferous, and raiſing ſeditiōs to al the Ievves in the vvhole vvorld, and authour of the ſedition c \f 1 \fr 24.5 \ft \+it τῆς αἱρέσεως\+it*\f* of the ſecte of the Nazarenes,
\v 6 vvho alſo hath attempted to violat the temple, vvhom alſo being apprehended vve vvould haue iudged according to our lavv.
\v 7 But Lyſias the Tribune comming in, vvith great force tooke him avvay out of our handes,
\v 8 commaunding his accuſers to come to thee, of vvhom thou maieſt thy ſelf iudging, vnderſtand of al theſe things, vvhereof vve accuſe him.
\v 9 And the Ievves alſo added, ſaying that theſe things vvere ſo.
\v 10 But Paul anſvvered, (the Preſident making a ſigne vnto him for to ſpeake.) Knovving that of many yeres thou art iudge ouer this nation, I vvil vvith good courage anſvver for my ſelf.
\v 11 For thou maieſt vnderſtand that it is not aboue tvvelue daies to me, ſince I vvent vp to adore in Hieruſalem.
\v 12 and neither in the temple did they finde me diſputing vvith any man, or cauſing concourſe of the multitude, neither in the ſynagogs, nor in the citie:
\v 13 neither can they proue vnto thee the things vvhereof they novv accuſe me.
\v 14 But this I confeſſe to thee, that c \f 1 \fr 24.14 \ft \+it c κατὰ τὴν ὁδὸν\+it*\f* according to the \f 2 \fr 24.14 \ft Becauſe Tertullus the Ievves orator called Chriſtian religion the ſect or (as it is there vers.5 in the Greeke) the hereſie of the Nazarens: S. Paul anſvvereth and ſhevveth that it is no hereſie. And as for the vvord, \+it Sect\+it*, in this place: it is in the Greeke, \+it According to the vvay, vvhich they call hereſie\+it*, as alſo Act. 9, 2. 24, 22. And therfore the vvord ſect here is ſo taken. See \+it Annot. c. 28, 22\+it*.\f* ſecte, vvhich they call hereſie, I doe ſo ſerue \f 3 \fr 24.14 \fq ‘the father my God \ft \+it the God of my Fathers, (or) the Father, and my God,\+it*\f* ‘the father my God’, beleeuing al things that are vvritten in the Law & the Prophets:
\v 15 hauing hope in God, the vvhich theſe alſo them ſelues expect, that there ſhal be a reſurrection of iuſt and vniuſt.
\v 16 In this my ſelf alſo doe ſtudie to haue a cōſcience vvithout offenſe tovvard God & tovvard men alvvaies.
\v 17 And after many yeres \x - \xt Ro. 15, 28.\x* I came to beſtovv almes vpon my nation, & oblations, and vovves.
\v 18 In the vvhich they found me \x - \xt Act. 21, 26.\x* purified in the temple: not vvith multitude nor vvith tumult.
\v 19 But certaine Ievves of Aſia, vvho ought to be preſent before thee and to accuſe, if they had any thing againſt me:
\v 20 or let theſe men thē ſelues ſay, if they haue found in me any iniquitie, foraſmuch as I ſtand in the Councel,
\v 21 but of this one voice only that I cried ſtanding among them, That \x - \xt Act. 23, 6.\x* of the reſurrection of the dead am I iudged this day of you.
\v 22 And Felix differred them, knovving moſt certainely of this vvay, ſaying, Vvhen Lyſias the Tribune is come dovvne, I vvil heare you.
\v 23 And he commaunded the Centurion to keepe him, and that he ſhould haue reſt, neither to prohibit any of his to miniſter vnto him.
\v 24 And after ſome daies, Felix comming vvith Druſilla his vvife, vvhich vvas a Ievve, called Paul, and heard of him the faith that is in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 25 And he diſputing of \f 1 \fr 24.25 \ft The Apoſtolike teaching vvas not of only or ſpecial faith, but of iuſtice, & chaſtitie, & iudgement, that is to ſay, of the terrour of Hel and other Gods iudgements in the next life anſvverable to our deedes in this vvorld: by vvhich the hearers vvere firſt terrified, and ſo induced to penance. Hovv ſay Heretikes then that ſuch things make men hypocrites?\f* iuſtice and chaſtitie, and of the iudgement to come, Felix being terrified, anſvvered, For this time, goe thy vvay: but in time conuenient I vvil ſend for thee.
\v 26 hoping alſo vvithal, that money vvould be giuen him of Paul, for the vvhich cauſe alſo oftentimes ſending for him, he ſpake vvith him.
\v 27 But vvhen tvvo yeres vvere ended, Felix had a ſucceſſour Portius Feſtus. And Felix being vvilling to ſhevv the Ievves a pleaſure, left Paul in priſon.
\c 25
\cd After tvvo yeres empriſonment the Iewes continew their ſute againſt him, ſoliciting the nevv Preſident Feſtus, 6 firſt at Hieruſalem, then at Cæſarea: 9 vvhere through the Iewes partialitie he is faine to appeale vnto the Emperour: 13 and is in the meane time brought forth by Feſtus (giuing him good teſtimonie, notvvithſtanding the exclamations of the Iewes againſt him) vnto king Agrippa and his Queene Bernice.
\p
\v 1 \sc FESTVS\sc* therfore vvhen he vvas come into the prouince, after three daies vvent vp to Hieruſalem from Cæſaréa.
\v 2 And the cheefe prieſts, & principal men of the Iewes vvent vnto him againſt Paul: and they deſired him,
\v 3 requeſting fauour againſt him, that he vvould commaund him to be brought to Hieruſalem, laying vvaite for to kil him in the vvay.
\v 4 But Feſtus anſvvered, that Paul is in Cæſaréa: and that he vvould very ſhortly goe thither.
\v 5 They therfore, ſaith he, that are of abilitie among you, going dovvne vvith me, if there be any crime in the man, let them accuſe him.
\v 6 And hauing taried among them not aboue eight or ten daies, he vvent dovvne to Cæſaréa, and the next day he ſate in the iudgement ſeate: and he commaunded Paul to be brought.
\v 7 Vvho being brought, there ſtoode about him the Iewes that vvere come dovvne from Hieruſalem, obiecting many and greuous c \f 1 \fr 25.7 \ft \+it crimes as v. 27\+it*.\f* cauſes vvhich, they could not proue,
\v 8 Paul making anſvver, That neither againſt the lavv of the Iewes, nor againſt the temple, nor againſt Cæſar haue I any thing offended.
\v 9 But Feſtus vvilling to ſhevv the Iewes a pleaſure, anſvvering Paul, ſaid, Vvilt thou goe vp to Hieruſalem, and there be iudged of theſe things before me?
\v 10 And Paul ſaid, At Cæſars iudgement ſeate doe I ſtand, vvhere I ought to be iudged: the Iewes I haue not hurt, as thou very vvel knovveſt.
\v 11 For if I haue hurt them, or done any thing vvorthie of death, I refuſe not to die. but if none of thoſe things be, vvhereof theſe accuſe me, no man can giue me to them. \f 1 \fr 25.11 \ft If S. Paul both to ſaue him ſelf from vvhipping and from death ſought by the Iewes, doubted not to crie for ſuccour of the Romane lavves, and to appeale to Cæſar the Prince of the Romans not yet Chriſtened: how much more may vve call for aide of Chriſtian Princes and their lavves, for the puniſhment of Heretikes, and for the Churches defenſe againſt them? \+it S. Auguſtine ep. 50\+it*.\f* I appeale to Cæſar.
\v 12 Then Feſtus hauing conferred vvith the Councel, anſvvered, Haſt thou appealed to Cæſar? to Cæſar ſhalt thou goe.
\v 13 And vvhen certaine daies vvere paſſed, king Agrippa & Bernice came dovvne to Cæſaréa to ſalute Feſtus.
\v 14 And as they taried there a good many daies, Feſtus ſignified to the king, of Paul, ſaying, A certaine perſon vvas left priſoner by Felix,
\v 15 concerning vvhom, vvhen I vvas at Hieruſalem, the cheefe prieſts and the auncients of the Iewes came vnto me, deſyring condemnation againſt him.
\v 16 To vvhom I anſvvered, That it is not the Romanes cuſtome to yeld vp any man before that he vvhich is accuſed haue his accuſers preſent and take place to make his anſvver for to cleere him ſelf of the crimes.
\v 17 Vvhen they therfore vvere aſſembled hither, vvithout any delaie, the day folovving, ſitting in the iudgement ſeat, I commaunded the man to be brought.
\v 18 Of vvhom, vvhen the accuſers ſtoode vp, they brought no cauſe vvhich I thought il of:
\v 19 but certaine queſtions of their ovvne ſuperſtition they had againſt him, and of \f 1 \fr 25.19 \ft This vvhom he termeth by contempt, one Ieſvs, hath novv made al the Romane Emperours and Princes of the vvorld to knovv him, and hath giuen the ſeate of the Cæſars to his poore ſeruants, Peter & his ſucceſſors.\f* one \sc IESVS\sc* deceaſed, vvhom Paul affirmed to liue.
\v 20 Doubting therfore of this kinde of queſtion, I ſaid, vvhether he vvould goe to Hieruſalem, & there be iudged of theſe things.
\v 21 But Paul appealing to be kept vnto the knovvledge of Auguſtus, I commaunded him to be kept, til I ſend him to Cæſar.
\v 22 And Agrippa ſaid to Feſtus, My ſelf alſo vvould heare the man. To morovv, ſaid he, thou ſhalt heare him.
\v 23 And the next day vvhen Agrippa and Bernice vvere come vvith great pompe, and had entred into the hall of audience vvith the Tribunes and principal men of the citie, at Feſtus commaundement Paul vvas brought.
\v 24 And Feſtus ſaith, King Agrippa, and al ye men that are preſent together vvith vs, you ſee this man, concerning vvhom al the multitude of the Iewes called vpon me at Hieruſalem, requeſting and crying out that he ought not to liue any longer.
\v 25 Yet haue I found nothing that he hath committed vvorthie of death. But foraſmuch as he him ſelf appealed to Auguſtus, I haue determined to ſend him.
\v 26 Of vvhom vvhat to vvrite for certaintie to my lord, I haue not. For the vvhich cauſe I haue brought him forth to you, and eſpecially to thee, king Agrippa, that examination being made, I may haue vvhat to vvrite.
\v 27 For it ſeemeth to me vvithout reaſon, to ſend a priſoner, & not to ſignifie c \f 1 \fr 25.27 \ft \+it c τὰς κατ ἀυτοῦ αἰτίας.\+it*\f* his cauſes.
\c 26
\cd In that honorable Audience being permitted to ſpeake, 2 he declareth to the king vvhat he firſt vvas, 12 and hovv miraculouſly he vvas conuerted, 19 and that he hath preached ſince, as he vvas commaunded from heauen, and as the Prophets had foretold of Chriſt. 24 Which ſtrange tale Feſtus the Heathen Preſident hearing, ſaith that he is mad. 25 But he anſvvereth, and exhorteth them al to be Chriſtians as he is. 30 They finally pronounce that he might be ſet at libertie, but only for his appeale.
\p
\v 1 \sc BVT\sc* Agrippa ſaid to Paul, Thou art permitted to ſpeake for thy ſelf. Then Paul ſtretching forth his hand, began to make his anſvver.
\v 2 Touching al things vvhereof I am accuſed of the Iewes, king Agrippa, I account my ſelf happie for that I am to defend my ſelf this day before thee,
\v 3 eſpecially vvhereas thou knovveſt al things that are among the Ievves, cuſtomes and queſtions: for the vvhich cauſe I beſeeche thee, heare me patiently.
\v 4 And my life truely from my youth, vvhich vvas from the beginning in my nation in Hieruſalem, al the Ievves doe knovv:
\v 5 knovving me before from the beginning (if they vvil giue teſtimonie) that according to the moſt ſure \f 1 \fr 26.5 \ft \+it c ἁίρεσιν\+it*\f* ſecte of our religion I liued a Phariſee.
\v 6 And novv for the hope of the promiſſe that vvas made of God to our fathers, doe I ſtand ſubiect to iudgement:
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 26.7 \ft \+it b in quam\+it*\f* the vvhich, our tvvelue tribes \f 2 \fr 26.7 \ft \+it c λατρεῦον\+it*\f* ſeruing night and day, hope to come vnto. Of the vvhich hope, o king, I am accuſed of the Iewes.
\v 8 Vvhat incredible thing is it iudged vvith you, if God raiſe the dead?
\v 9 And my ſelf truely had thought that I ought to doe againſt the name of \sc IESVS\sc* of Nazareth many contrarie things.
\v 10 Vvhich alſo I \x - \xt Act. 8, 3.\x* did at Hieruſalem, and many of the ſaincts did I ſhut vp in priſons, hauing receiued authoritie of the cheefe prieſts: and vvhen they vvere put to death, \f 2 \fr 26.10 \ft \+it c detuli ſententiam.\+it*\f* I brought the ſentence.
\v 11 And through out al the ſynagogs often times puniſhing them, I compelled them to blaſpheme: and yet more mad againſt them, I perſecuted them euen vnto foraine cities.
\v 12 Among vvhich things vvhiles \x - \xt Act. 9, 3.\x* I vvent to Damaſcus vvith authoritie and permiſſion of the cheefe prieſts,
\v 13 at midday, in the vvay, I ſavv (o king) from heauen a light to haue ſhined round about me and them that vvere in companie vvith me, aboue the brightnes of the ſunne.
\v 14 And vvhen al vve vvere fallen dovvne on the ground, I heard a voice ſpeaking to me in the Hebrew tongue: Saul, Saul, vvhy perſecuteſt thou me? It is hard for thee to kicke againſt the pricke.
\v 15 And I ſaid, Vvho art thou Lord? And our Lord anſvvered, I am \sc IESVS\sc* vvhom thou doeſt perſecute.
\v 16 But riſe vp and ſtand vpon thy feete: for to this end haue I appeared to thee, that I may ordaine thee a miniſter and vvitnes of thoſe things vvhich thou haſt ſeen, and of thoſe things vvherein I vvil appeare to thee,
\v 17 deliuering thee out of the peoples & nations vnto the vvhich novv I ſend thee,
\v 18 to open their eies, that they may be conuerted from darkenes to light, and from the povver of Satan to God, that they may receiue remiſſion of ſinnes and lot among the ſaincts by the faith that is in me.
\v 19 Vvherevpon, king Agrippa, I vvas not incredulous to the heauenly viſion:
\v 20 but to them firſt that are at Damaſcus, and at Hieruſalem, and vnto al the countrie of Ievvrie, and to the Gentiles \x - \xt Act. 9, 20.\x* did I preach that they ſhould doe \f 2 \fr 26.20 \ft Penance often inculcated, and vvorkes agreable to the ſame. · Act. 9, 20.\f* penance, and turne to God, doing vvorkes vvorthie of penance.
\v 21 For this cauſe the Iewes, vvhen I vvas in the temple, \x - \xt Act. 21, 30.\x* apprehending me, attempted meaning to kil me.
\v 22 But aided by the help of God, I ſtand vntil this day, teſtifying to ſmall and to great, ſaying nothing beſide thoſe things vvhich the Prophets did ſpeake ſhould come to paſſe, & Moyſes,
\v 23 if \sc C H R\sc* I S T vvere paſſible, if the firſt of the reſurrection from the dead, he vvere to ſhevv light to the people and to the Gentiles.
\v 24 As he ſpake theſe things and made his anſvver, Feſtus vvith a loud voice ſaid, Thou art mad, Paul: much learning turneth thee to madneſſe.
\v 25 And Paul ſaid, I am not mad, moſt excellent Feſtus: but I ſpeake vvordes of veritie and ſobrietie.
\v 26 for the king knovveth of theſe things, to vvhom alſo I ſpeake conſtantly. for I thinke none of theſe things to be vnknovven to him. For neither vvas any of theſe things done in a corner.
\v 27 Beleeueſt thou the prophets, king Agrippa? I knovv that thou beleeueſt.
\v 28 And Agrippa ſaid to Paul: A litle thou perſuadeſt me to become a \sc C H R I S T I A N\sc*.
\v 29 And Paul ſaid, I vviſh of God, both in litle, and in much, not only thee, but alſo al that heare this day, to become ſuch as I am alſo, except theſe bandes.
\v 30 And the king roſe vp, and the Preſident, and Bernice, and they that ſate by them.
\v 31 And going a ſide, they ſpake among them ſelues, ſaying, That this man hath done nothing vvorthie of death or bandes.
\v 32 And Agrippa ſaid to Feſtus, This man might be releaſed, if he had not appealed to Caeſar.
\c 27
\cd What a dangerous nauigation he had tovvards Rome: and that by his prediction and counſel the ſhip might haue been ſaued. And for his ſake (as God alſo reuealed to him before) al the companie vvas preſerued, being 276 perſons.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* after it vvas decreed that he ſhould ſaile into Italie, and that Paul vvith other priſoners ſhould be deliuered to a Centurion named Iulius, of the band Auguſta,
\v 2 vve going vp into a ſhip of Adrumétum, beginning to ſaile about the places of Aſia, looſed from the land, Ariſtarchus the Macedonian of Theſſalonica continuing vvith vs.
\v 3 And the day folovving vve came to Sidon. And Iulius intreating Paul courteouſly, permitted him to goe to his frendes, and to take care of him ſelf.
\v 4 And vvhen vve had looſed thence, vve ſailed vnder Cypres: becauſe the vvindes vvere contrarie.
\v 5 And ſailing the ſea of Cilicia and Pamphilia, vve came to \f 1 \fr 27.5 \fq `Lyſtra \ft \+it Myra\+it*\f* `Lyſtra', vvhich is in Lycia:
\v 6 and there the Centurion finding a ſhip of Alexandria ſailing into Italie, remoued vs into it.
\v 7 And vvhereas many daies vve ſailed ſlovvly, & vvere ſcarſe come ouer againſt Gnidus, the vvinde hindering vs, vve ſailed neere Crete by Salmóne:
\v 8 and vvith much a doe ſailing by it, vve came into a certaine place that is called Good-hauens, nigh to the vvhich vvas a citie Thalaſſa.
\v 9 And vvhen much time vvas ſpent, and vvhereas novv it vvas not ſafe ſailing, becauſe the \f 1 \fr 27.9 \ft It may ſignifie the Iewes faſt of the ſeuenth moneth September, after vvhich the nauigation vvas perilous, vvinter approching.\f* faſt novv vvas paſt, Paul comforted them,
\v 10 ſaying to them, Ye men, I ſee that the ſailing beginneth to be vvith hurt and much damage not only of the lading and the ſhip, but alſo of our liues.
\v 11 But the Centurion beleeued the gouernour and maiſter of the ſhip, more then thoſe things vvhich vvere ſaid of Paul.
\v 12 And vvhereas it vvas not a commodious hauen to vvinter in, very many taking counſel appointed to ſaile thence, if by any meanes they might comming to Phœníce, vvinter there, a hauen of Crete looking tovvard the \f 1 \fr 27.12 \ft names of vvindes.\f* Afrike and the Chore.
\v 13 And the ſouthvvinde blovving, they thinking that they had obteined their purpoſe, vvhen they had parted from Aſſon, ſailed along by Crete.
\v 14 But not long after, a tempeſtuous vvinde that is called Euro-aquilo, droue againſt it.
\v 15 And vvhen the ſhippe vvas caught and could not make vvay againſt the vvinde, giuing vp the ſhip to the vvindes, vve vvere driuen.
\v 16 And running vpon a certaine iland, that is called \f 1 \fr 27.16 \ft c Græc. Clauda.\f* Cauda, vve could ſcarſe get the cock-boate.
\v 17 Vvhich being taken vp, they vſed helps, girding the ſhip, and fearing leſt they ſhould fall into \f 1 \fr 27.17 \ft c a place of quicke ſands.\f* the Syrte, letting dovvne the veſſel, ſo vvere they caried.
\v 18 And vvhen vve vvere mightily toſſed vvith the tempeſt, the next day they caſt forth,
\v 19 And the third day vvith their ovvne handes they threvve forth the tacklinges of the ſhip.
\v 20 And neither ſunne, nor ſtarres appearing for many daies, and no ſmal ſtorme being tovvard, al hope vvas novv taken avvay of our ſauing.
\v 21 And vvhen there had been long faſting, then Paul ſtanding in the middes of them, ſaid, You ſhould in deede, O ye men, haue heard me, and not haue parted from Crete, & haue gained this hurt and loſſe.
\v 22 And novv I exhort you to be of good cheere. for there ſhal be no loſſe of any ſoule among you, but of the ſhip.
\v 23 For an Angel of the God vvhoſe I am, and \f 1 \fr 27.23 \ft \+it ᾧ λατρεύω\+it*.\f* vvhom I ſerue, ſtoode by me this night,
\v 24 ſaying, Feare not Paul, thou muſt appeare before Cæſar: and behold God hath \f 1 \fr 27.24 \ft Paul (ſaith S. Hierom) had ſo many ſoules in the ſhip giuen him, that is, ſo many men ſaued for his ſake: and after he is vvith Chriſt, ſhal he ſhut his mouth, and not be able once to ſpeake for them that haue beleeued in his Goſpel? \+it Hiero.adu. vigil\+it*. Vvhereby he proueth that if God do much for the merits of Saincts in this life, much more at their interceſſion & praier in heauen.\f* giuen thee al that ſaile vvith thee.
\v 25 For the vvhich cauſe be of good cheere ye men: for I beleeue God, that it ſhal ſo be, as it hath been ſaid to me.
\v 26 And vve muſt come vnto a certaine iland.
\v 27 But after the fourtenth night vvas come on vs, as vve vvere ſailing in Adria about midnight, the ſhipmen deemed that there appeared ſome countrie to them.
\v 28 Vvho alſo ſounding, found tvventie fadomes: and being parted a litle from thence, they found fiftene fadomes.
\v 29 And fearing leſt vve ſhould fall into rough places, caſting out of the ſterne foure ankers, they vviſhed that day vvere come.
\v 30 But as the ſhipmen ſought to flee out of the ſhip, hauing let dovvne the cock-boate into the ſea, pretending as if they vvere about to caſt out ankers out of the fore part of the ſhip,
\v 31 Paul ſaid to the Centurion and to the ſouldiars, Vnles theſe tarie in the ſhip, you can not be ſaued.
\v 32 Then the ſouldiars cut of the ropes of the cock-boate: and ſuffered it to fal avvay.
\v 33 And vvhen it began to be light, Paul deſired all to take meate, ſaying, This day is the fourtenth day that you expect and remaine faſting, taking nothing.
\v 34 For the vvhich cauſe I deſire you to take meate for your health ſake: for there ſhal not an heare of the head periſh of any of you.
\v 35 And vvhen he had ſaid theſe things, taking bread, he gaue thankes to God in the ſight of them al: and vvhen he had broken it, he began to eate.
\v 36 And being al made of better cheere, they alſo tooke meate.
\v 37 And vve vvere in al in the ſhip, ſoules tvvo hundred ſeuentie ſixe.
\v 38 And being filled vvith meate, they lighted the ſhip, caſting the vvheat into the ſea.
\v 39 And vvhen day vvas come, they knevv not the land: but they ſpied a certaine creeke that had a ſhore, into the vvhich they minded, if they could, to caſt a land the ſhip.
\v 40 And vvhen they had taken vp the ankers, they committed them ſelues to the ſea, looſing vvithal the rudder bands: and hoiſing vp the maine ſaile according as the vvinde blevve, they vvent on tovvard the ſhore.
\v 41 And vvhen vve vvere fallen into a place betvvene tvvo ſeas, they graueled the ſhip: and the fore-part truly ſticking faſt remained vnmoueable: but the hinder part vvas broken by the violence of the ſea.
\v 42 And the counſel of the ſouldiars vvas, that they ſhould kil the priſoners: leſt any ſvvimming out, might runne avvay.
\v 43 But the Centurion vvilling to ſaue Paul, forbade it to be done: & he commaunded them that could ſvvimme, to caſt out them ſelues firſt, and eſcape, and goe forth to land:
\v 44 and the reſt, ſome they caried on bordes, and ſome vpon thoſe things that vvere of the ſhip. And ſo it came to paſſe, that al the ſoules eſcaped to land.
\c 28
\cd After their ſhipwracke hauing vvintered in the Iland (now named Malta) where many miracles were wrought by Paul, they take ſhip againe, and ſo by Sicile they come to Puteoli in Italie, the Chriſtian Romans comming a great way to meete him, to his great ioy. 16 Finally being come to Rome, in his lodging he declareth to the Iewes his cauſe, 23 and on a day appointed preacheth IESVS vnto them. 25 And ſeeing their incredulitie, he ſhevveth how it was foretold by Eſay: 28 but that the Gentils wil not be incredulous. 30 To whom he there preacheth two whole yeres without prohibition.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* vvhen we had eſcaped, then vve knevv that the \f 1 \fr 28.1 \ft This iland (now Malta) is the ſeate of the knightes of the Rhodes. the inhabitants wherof haue a ſpecial deuotion to S. Paul: to whom both the cheefe Church (being the Biſhops Seate) is dedicated, and the whole Iland (as they count it) conſecrated. Where the people ſhew yet to ſtrāgers, his priſon and other memories of his miracles.\f* iland vvas called \f 2 \fr 28.1 \fq `Mityléne \ft \+it Melita\+it*\f* `Mityléne'. But the Barbarous ſhewed vs no ſmal courteſie.
\v 2 For, kindling a fire they refreſhed vs al, becauſe of the imminent raine and the colde.
\v 3 And vvhen Paul had gathered together ſome number of ſtickes, and had laide them on the fire, a viper iſſuing out of the heate, inuaded his hand.
\v 4 But as the Barbarous ſaw the beaſt hanging on his hand, they ſaid one to an other, Vndoubtedly this man is a murderer, vvho being eſcaped out of the ſea, \f 1 \fr 28.4 \ft \+it ἡ δίκη\+it*\f* Vengeance doth not ſuffer him to liue.
\v 5 And he in deede ſhaking of the beaſt into the fire, ſuffred no harme.
\v 6 But they ſuppoſed that he ſhould be turned into a ſwelling, and that he vvould ſodenly fall and die. But expecting long and ſeeing that there vvas no harme done on him, being changed they ſaid, that he was a God.
\v 7 And in thoſe places were lands of the prince of the ile, named Publius, vvho receiuing vs, for three daies intreated vs courteouſly.
\v 8 And it chaunced that the father of Publius lay vexed vvith feuers and the bloudy flixe. Vnto whom Paul entred: and vvhen he had praied, \f 1 \fr 28.8 \ft Miraculous healing by impoſition of the Apoſtle's hands.\f* and impoſed hands on him, he healed him.
\v 9 Vvhich being done, al in the ile alſo that had infirmities, came, and vvere cured:
\v 10 who alſo honoured vs vvith many honours, and when vve vvere ſailing away, laded vs vvith neceſſaries.
\v 11 And after three moneths, we ſailed in a ſhip of Alexandria, that had wintered in the iland, vvhoſe ſigne vvas the Caſtors.
\v 12 And vvhen vve vvere come to Syracûſa, vve taried there three daies.
\v 13 Thence compaſſing by the ſhore, vve came to Rhegium: and after one day the South winde blovving, vve came the ſecond day to Putéoli,
\v 14 vvhere finding brethren, vve were deſired to tarie vvith them ſeuen daies: and ſo vve came to Rome.
\v 15 And from thence, vvhen the brethren had heard, they came to meete vs vnto \it Apij-forum\it*, and the Three-tauerns. Vvhom vvhen Paul had ſeen, giuing thanks to God, he tooke courage.
\v 16 And vvhen vve vvere come to Rome, Paul vvas permitted to remaine to him ſelf with a ſouldiar that kept him.
\v 17 And after the third day he called together the cheefe of the Iewes. And vvhen they vvere aſſembled, he ſaid to them, Men brethren, I doing nothing againſt the people, or the cuſtome of the fathers, vvas deliuered priſoner from Hieruſalem into the hands of the Romanes,
\v 18 who vvhen they had examined me, vvould haue releaſed me, for that there vvas no cauſe of death in me.
\v 19 But the Ievves contradicting it, I vvas compelled to appeale vnto Cæſar, not as hauing any thing to accuſe my nation.
\v 20 For this cauſe therfore I deſired to ſee you and to ſpeake to you. for, becauſe of the hope of Iſraël, am I compaſſed vvith this chaine.
\v 21 But they ſaid to him, Vve neither receiued letters concerning thee from Iewrie, neither did any of the brethren that came hither, report or ſpeake any euil of thee.
\v 22 But vve deſire of thee to heare vvhat thou thinkeſt: for concerning this \f 1 \fr 28.22 \ft c αἱρέσεως.\f* ſecte, it is knowen to vs that it is gaineſaid euery vvhere.
\v 23 And vvhen they had appointed him a day, they came to him vnto his lodging very many: to vvhom he expounded, teſtifying the kingdom of God, and vſing perſuaſion to them of \sc IESVS\sc* out of the law of Moyſes and the Prophets, from morning vntil euening.
\v 24 And certaine beleeued thoſe things that were ſaid: but certaine beleeued not.
\v 25 And vvhereas they did not agree among them ſelues, they departed, Paul ſaying one vvord: That wel did the holy Ghoſt ſpeake by Eſaie the prophet to our fathers,
\v 26 ſaying, \x - \xt Eſa. 6, 9. Mat. 13, 14. Mr. 4, 12. Lu. 8, 10. Io. 12, 40. Ro. 11, 8.\x* \it Goe to this people, and ſay to them, Vvith the eare you ſhal heare, and ſhal not vnderſtand: and ſeeing you ſhal ſee, and ſhal not perceiue\it*.
\v 27 \it For the hart of this people is vvaxen groſſe, and vvith their eares haue they heauily heard, and their eies \it* \f 1 \fr 28.27 \ft Here alſo \+it (as Mat. 13\+it*.) it is plaine that they would not ſee, nor heare, & that their excecatiō is to be attributed to thē ſelues & not to God. \+it See annota. Io. 12, 40\+it*.\f* \it they haue ſhut: leſt perhaps they may ſee vvith their eies, and heare vvith their eares, and vnderſtand vvith their hart, and be conuerted, and I heale them\it*.
\v 28 Be it knowen therfore to you, that this Saluation of God is ſent to the Gentiles, and they vvil heare.
\v 29 And vvhen he had ſaid theſe things, the Ievves vvent out from him, hauing much queſtioning among them ſelues.
\v 30 And he taried ful tvvo yeres in his hired lodging: and he receiued al that came into him,
\v 31 preaching the kingdom of God, and teaching the things that concerne our Lord \sc IESVS CHRIST\sc* vvith al confidence, vvithout prohibition.
\s1 The end of the Acts of the Apostles.
\ip Whereunto we join for the reader's behalf, two Tables of the two chief Apostles, and a note of the rest, as an abridgement of the said book, and a supply of some things not there mentioned.
\ip The sum of the acts of the apostles, containing specially the gests of the two principal Apostles, SS. Peter and Paul, in such order of time and years of the Emperors, and from Christ's Nativity, and Ascension, as they were done: so far as by holy Scriptures or Ecclesiastical writers may be gathered. Wherein though it be not possible to set down the precise and undoubted time or year of every thing, because neither S. Luke nor others do note particularly and orderly the moments of every action of the said Apostles: yet we follow the most probable and plain plat that we find in the holy Scripture and ancient writers. Whereby the studious reader may easily discover the folly of the Protestants, that can find no time when Peter might possibly come to Rome, be Bishop, and die there: diverse things in S. Paul's acts being no less hard to reconcile to the course of S. Luke's narration, than any thing touching the history of S. Peter, namely his three years preaching in Arabia: all which must needs be true, whether we hit the very just time or no, and how so ever author's differ about the same.
\s1 OF THE OTHER APOSTLES.
\ip THE Acts of the rest of the twelve Apostles be not much written of in this book: but as \f - \ft Genebr. out of diverse authors.\f*other Ecclesiastical writers do testify, they preached specially in these nations, as followeth. \it Andrew in Achaia, John in Asia, Philip in Phrygia, James in Jewry, Bartholomew in Scythia, Thomas in India, Matthew in Aethiopia, Simon in Persia, Thaddaeus in Mesopotamia, the other James in Spain, Matthias in Palestine.\it* So distributing themselves throughout the world, to gather one Catholic Church of all Nations, according as Christ gave them commission \it Mat. 28, 19\it*: and as it was prophesied of them before, Psal. 18. \it Their sound is gone forth into every country, and their words into the ends of the whole world\it*. But before they departed one from another (the time whereof is not certainly known) \f - \ft Ruff. in expos. Symb. Apost. Ambr. ser. \+it 38.\+it* Hiero. ep. \+it 61. c. 9.\+it* advers. erro. Iov. Hieros.\f*all Twelve assembling together, & full of the Holy Ghost, each laying down his sentence, agreed upon twelve principal articles of the Christian faith, and appointed them for a rule to all believers: Which is therefore called and is THE APOSTLES' CREED: \it Not written in paper\it*, as the Scripture, \it but from the Apostles delivered by tradition\it*. Ruff. & Hiero. locis citatis. Which, as of old \it (Hiero. cont. Lucifer.)\it* so at this day all solemnly profess in their Baptism, either by themselves or by others: and all that be of age and capacity, are bound to know and believe every article of the same. Which are these that follow.
